[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Welcome to the Course!"]
[Result "*"]
[PlyCount "1"]

{Welcome to part 1 of the Shankland Method!  I originally released this course as a video only for ichess back in 2016. ichess has since gone out of business, and when the rights were returned to me, I knew I wanted to relaunch an updated and massively improved version.  In this course, we will see all 54 games seen in the original ichess videos. Half of them were taken from my own tournament practice, and the other half were taken from games that made a strong impression on me. There are a lot of upgrades to this new version of the Shankland Method. There will be full annotations in the text, and all of the key exercise moments will be given as trainable variations for solving. There’s quite a lot – over 150, in fact!  The way it works is that you go through each exercise from a given game, and then, at the end, you will see the full game annotated.  I have also re-recorded the video, which will be helpful in two ways. First off, the technology available now is much better than what I was working with at the time, and the resolution and sound will be much better. Second off, I am a better chess player now, and I found a lot of new points within the games that merited additional commentary that I did not share the first time around.  Additionally, I included 10 new games from my own practice in the years since the original Shankland Method videos were released. These are games where the Shankland Method came up at some moment and proved to be the decisive factor. While the Shankland Method might be named after me, it’s absolutely impossible to fully master! In the 10 games, there is a 5-5 split between games where I executed the Shankland Method correctly and games where I messed up and left at least a half point on the table.  When it comes to the Shankland Method itself, it can be summed up with one simple question. “What happens if I do it anyway?”. When chess players think of calculation, they are often thinking of sharp positions with lots of hanging pieces or direct assaults on a vulnerable king. While these positions undoubtedly require a lot of calculation, there is very little (if any) literature or media covering the elements of strategic calculation.  The great positional players of past and present always used good calculation to help them achieve their positional goals. This is best done by figuring out what move you really want to make in an ideal world. Oftentimes, you will find that your opponent seems to be stopping you from doing it. So you ask: “What happens if I do it anyway?” I find that a lot of the time, there is a tactical resource of some kind that allows you to execute your ideal move despite it looking impossible for some reason or another. This is one of the most important skills for any aspiring chess player to master.  While being able to use the Shankland Method perfectly is an impossible goal for even the most brilliant among us (you’ll see plenty of examples of very, very strong players messing it up!) , we can still train to be better at it than we were before. I found that in my own practice, using the Shankland Method was the #1 way that I would score victories over strong players. I also found that a lot of the times that I left a half point on the table were because I failed to use the Shankland Method properly. I don’t think there is any element in chess that is more important in practical middlegame and endgame play.  With all that in mind, let’s get to work!} *

