1. d4 {For the first game in the course, we take a look at one that allowed Topalov to equalize the score in the 2010 World Championship match against Anand.} d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. Ne5 e6 7. f3 c5 {This move had been seen in two of the previous games as well. Black quickly simplifies to an unpleasant but symmetrical endgame where he hopes that with a few active moves, he can activate his pieces and make an easy draw.} (7... Bb4 {is much more combative, and has always been my choice in this position with Black.}) 8. e4 Bg6 9. Be3 cxd4 10. Qxd4 Qxd4 11. Bxd4 Nfd7 12. Nxd7 Nxd7 13. Bxc4 {The battle lines are drawn. Black's bishop on g6 is well restricted by White's pawn center, and otherwise, the position is totally balanced. If Black can fix the issue of this bishop, he should be fine, but this is not necessarily a trivial thing to do.} Rc8 14. Bb5 a6 15. Bxd7+ Kxd7 16. Ke2 f6 {Naturally, Black looks to make room for his bishop. If he is able to play ... e5 and ... Bf7 , he should solve all of his problems.} 17. Rhd1 Ke8 (17... e5 18. Bxe5+) 18. a5 Be7 (18... e5 {I'm not sure why Vishy avoided this natural move.} 19. Bb6 Bf7 {Black should be fine. He can complete his development with ... Be6 , ... Kf7 , and ... Be7 .}) 19. Bb6 Rf8 20. Rac1 f5 21. e5 Bg5 22. Be3 ({After} 22. f4 {, we have reached one of the most critical moments of the game. Black is almost equalizing, but White can keep up the pressure by using the Shankland Method.}) *

