22... f4 {Now, we have reached one of the most critical moments of the game. Black is almost equalizing, but White can keep up the pressure by using the Shankland Method.} 23. Ne4 {White wants to bring the knight to d6 , but this move looks ridiculous because so much stuff is hanging! But, what happens if I do it anyway?} (23. Nb5 {This was a fine move order as well.}) (23. Bd2 Rf5 {won't do.}) 23... Rxc1 (23... fxe3 24. Rxc8+) (23... Bxe4 24. Rxc8+) 24. Nd6+ (24. Bxc1 $2 Bxe4 $1 {Black trades off the bad bishop, avoids a check on d6 , and will be better with a superior structure.}) 24... Kd7 25. Bxc1 {A fine introduction to the Shankland Method. By using some calculation, White managed to land the c3 -knight knight on the fantastic d6 -square. Without this calculation, in the starting position, White would have assumed the attacked bishop had to move, and the knight would never reach the d6 -outpost.} *

