1. d4 {For the first game in the course, we take a look at one that allowed Topalov to equalize the score in the 2010 World Championship match against Anand.} d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. Ne5 e6 7. f3 c5 {This move had been seen in two of the previous games as well. Black quickly simplifies to an unpleasant but symmetrical endgame where he hopes that with a few active moves, he can activate his pieces and make an easy draw.} (7... Bb4 {is much more combative, and has always been my choice in this position with Black.}) 8. e4 Bg6 9. Be3 cxd4 10. Qxd4 Qxd4 11. Bxd4 Nfd7 12. Nxd7 Nxd7 13. Bxc4 {The battle lines are drawn. Black's bishop on g6 is well restricted by White's pawn center, and otherwise, the position is totally balanced. If Black can fix the issue of this bishop, he should be fine, but this is not necessarily a trivial thing to do.} Rc8 14. Bb5 a6 15. Bxd7+ Kxd7 16. Ke2 f6 {Naturally, Black looks to make room for his bishop. If he is able to play ... e5 and ... Bf7 , he should solve all of his problems.} 17. Rhd1 Ke8 (17... e5 18. Bxe5+) 18. a5 Be7 (18... e5 {I'm not sure why Vishy avoided this natural move.} 19. Bb6 Bf7 {Black should be fine. He can complete his development with ... Be6 , ... Kf7 , and ... Be7 .}) 19. Bb6 Rf8 20. Rac1 f5 21. e5 Bg5 22. Be3 f4 {Now, we have reached one of the most critical moments of the game. Black is almost equalizing, but White can keep up the pressure by using the Shankland Method.} 23. Ne4 {White wants to bring the knight to d6 , but this move looks ridiculous because so much stuff is hanging! But, what happens if I do it anyway?} (23. Nb5 {This was a fine move order as well.}) (23. Bd2 Rf5 {won't do.}) 23... Rxc1 (23... fxe3 24. Rxc8+) (23... Bxe4 24. Rxc8+) 24. Nd6+ (24. Bxc1 $2 Bxe4 $1 {Black trades off the bad bishop, avoids a check on d6 , and will be better with a superior structure.}) 24... Kd7 25. Bxc1 {By using some calculation, White managed to land the c3 -knight knight on the fantastic d6 -square. Without this calculation, in the starting position, White would have assumed the attacked bishop had to move, and the knight would never reach the d6 -outpost.} Kc6 {Now Black has some real problems to solve. The knight is very good on d6 .} 26. Bd2 Be7 27. Rc1+ Kd7 28. Bc3 Bxd6 29. Rd1 (29. exd6 e5) 29... Bf5 30. h4 g6 (30... Kc7 31. exd6+) 31. Rxd6+ Kc8 32. Bd2 Rd8 33. Bxf4 Rxd6 34. exd6 {This opposite bishop endgame is quite complex, and outside the scope of the course. The half point was passed around a few times, even by the two best players in the world in that era.} Kd7 (34... Bc2 $1 {Black needed to get the bishop to a4 immediately.}) 35. Ke3 (35. Kd2 $1 {This would have stopped Black from regrouping the bishop to c6 . Eventually White can push the king up the board.}) 35... Bc2 36. Kd4 Ke8 37. Ke5 Kf7 38. Be3 Ba4 39. Kf4 Bb5 40. Bc5 Kf6 41. Bd4+ Kf7 42. Kg5 Bc6 43. Kh6 Kg8 44. h5 Be8 45. Kg5 Kf7 46. Kh6 Kg8 47. Bc5 gxh5 48. Kg5 Kg7 49. Bd4+ Kf7 50. Be5 h4 51. Kxh4 Kg6 52. Kg4 Bb5 53. Kf4 Kf7 54. Kg5 {Vishy had defended admirably to here, but just when the draw was within reach, he fumbled at the finish line.} Bc6 (54... Ke8 $1 {Black puts the bishop on d3 and king on d7 , and no further progress can be made.} 55. f4 Kd7 56. g4 Bd3 57. f5 {My suspicion is that Vishy saw it to here and thought that White wins, but there is an important saving resource.} exf5 58. gxf5 h6+ $1 {Without this move, Black would lose to f5 - f6 .} 59. Kf6 Be4 {White cannot get the king out of the way from in front of the pawn.}) 55. Kh6 Kg8 56. g4 {Vishy resigned here, and for good reason. Let's see what would have happened if he had kept playing.} Bb5 57. g5 Be8 58. Bg7 {Zugzwang. Black must allow g6 , and then the bishop on g7 ensures that the king will not be able to run to f8 to contest White's king coming to e7 .} Bc6 (58... Kf7 59. Kxh7) 59. g6 hxg6 60. Kxg6 Be8+ 61. Kf6 *

