11. b3 {This position is fairly well-known nowadays, but it was less so at the time. Black seems like he has some problems to solve- he has less space, playing against a Maroczy Bind setup, and would like to break out. White seemingly has the ... b5 and ... d5 pawn breaks under control... or does he?} d5 {What happens if I do it anyway? This looks like it blunders a pawn, but it turns out that the tactics work in Black's favor, and he equalizes immediately.} 12. Nxd5 {This might look tempting, but a couple of accurate moves leave White in bad shape.} (12. exd5 {is critical, and we'll see it in our next exercise.}) (12. cxd5 $2 Rxc3) 12... e6 (12... Nxd5 $2 13. cxd5 $1 {Now that the c3 -knight has been exchanged, White can take back with the c -pawn on d5 .} e6 14. f4 $1 exd5 15. e5 {Black has some problems to solve here.}) 13. Nxf6+ {Continuing down the wrong path of trying to hold the material.} (13. Ne3 {This should be preferred, though Black is pleasantly better after} Nxe4 $1 14. fxe4 Qxd4) 13... Bxf6 14. Be3 Rd8 {The knight is kicked off of d4 , and White will lose an exchange in the corner.} *

