[PlyCount "12"]

12. exd5 {As seen in our previous exercise, other captures fail. How does Black meet this natural reply?} Nxd5 {Again, what happens if I do it anyway? White cannot take with the pawn, and once he takes with the knight, ... e6 will get the lost piece back in short order.} 13. Nxd5 e6 14. Bh6 exd5 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 {Now, Kamsky tried to use the Shankland Method, but at a time where it did not work.} 16. c5 {Structurally, this is definitely the move White would like to make. It looks like it is possible to do it anyway since Black cannot take on c5 with the rook, but he has a stronger move.} (16. Nb5 {Something like this should be preferred, and the game will fizzle to sterile equality.} dxc4 17. Nd6 Rd8 18. Nxb7 Qxd1 19. Raxd1 Rxd1 20. Rxd1 cxb3 21. axb3 Nc6) 16... Na6 {I find it hard to believe Kamsky missed this move, but I'm not sure what he had in mind, either. The c5 -pawn drops.} (16... Rxc5 $2 17. Ne6+ {Is the tactical point behind White's play. He will win an exchange following Qd4 + next, and Black is very much lost.}) 17. Nc2 Nxc5 {We can stop here. Calculation has allowed Black to equalize.} *

