1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Bd7 4. Bxd7+ Qxd7 5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 g6 7. O-O Bg7 8. d4 cxd4 9. Nxd4 O-O 10. f3 Rc8 11. b3 {This position is fairly well-known nowadays, but it was less so at the time. Black seems like he has some problems to solve- he has less space, playing against a Maroczy Bind setup, and would like to break out. White seemingly has the ... b5 and ... d5 pawn breaks under control... or does he?} d5 {What happens if I do it anyway? This looks like it blunders a pawn, but it turns out that the tactics work in Black's favor, and he equalizes immediately.} 12. exd5 (12. Nxd5 $6 {This might look tempting, but a couple of accurate moves leave White in bad shape.} Nxd5 $2 13. cxd5 $1 {Now that the c3 -knight has been exchanged, White can take back with the c -pawn on d5 .} e6 14. f4 $1 exd5 15. e5 {Black has some problems to solve here. Or e6! Nxf6+? Continuing down the wrong path of trying to hold the material.} {( Ne3 This should be preferred, though Black is pleasantly better after Nxe4! fxe4 Qxd4⩱ )} Bf6 16. Be3 Rd8 $1 {The knight is kicked off of d4 , and White will lose an exchange in the corner.}) (12. cxd5 $2 Rxc3) 12... Nxd5 {Again, what happens if I do it anyway? White cannot take with the pawn, and once he takes with the knight, ... e6 will get the lost piece back in short order.} 13. Nxd5 e6 14. Bh6 exd5 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 {Now, Kamsky tried to use the Shankland Method, but at a time where it did not work.} 16. c5 {Structurally, this is definitely the move White would like to make. It looks like it is possible to do it anyway since Black cannot take on c5 with the rook, but he has a stronger move.} (16. Nb5 {Something like this should be preferred, and the game will fizzle to sterile equality.} dxc4 17. Nd6 Rd8 18. Nxb7 Qxd1 19. Raxd1 Rxd1 20. Rxd1 cxb3 21. axb3 Nc6) 16... Na6 {I find it hard to believe Kamsky missed this move, but I'm not sure what he had in mind, either. The c5 -pawn drops.} (16... Rxc5 $2 17. Ne6+ {is the tactical point behind White's play. He will win an exchange following Qd4 + next, and Black is very much lost.}) 17. Nc2 Nxc5 {Calculation has allowed Black to equalize.} 18. Qd4+ f6 19. Ne3 Ne6 20. Qh4 (20. Qxd5 {Of course this had to be played.} Qxd5 21. Nxd5 Rc2 22. Rf2 Rac8 {Black is a bit more comfortable but I have to imagine White should make a draw here without a ton of difficulty.}) 20... Rc5 21. Rad1 d4 {And now White is just a pawn down. Gelfand never let him back into the game.} 22. Ng4 Rf8 23. Rfe1 Rh5 24. Qg3 Rd5 25. Rd2 Qd6 26. Qh4 h5 27. Nf2 {Now, Black has any number of good moves, but I like the one Gelfand chose.} g5 {Black would very much like to play this move, expelling the active queen. It looks like it blunders the h5 -pawn. But we ask what happens if I do it anyway?} 28. Qe4 (28. Qxh5 Rh8 $1 {White collapses on h2 .}) 28... Re5 29. Qb1 Rxe1+ 30. Qxe1 Rd8 31. g3 Nc5 32. Qe2 a5 33. Qb5 b6 34. a3 Qe6 35. Rb2 {Now, time for one last Shankland Method of this game.} d3 {Other moves win, but this one is the cleanest by far. Black clearly wants to push the d -pawn up the board, but it looks like the pawn will now be lost following b4 , removing a defended. But... What if I do it anyway?} 36. b4 axb4 37. axb4 Nb3 38. Qa4 (38. Nxd3 Qe3+ $1 {Black wins material.} 39. Nf2 Qc1+) 38... Nd4 {The rest was easy for Gelfand.} 39. Kg2 Nc2 40. Rxc2 dxc2 41. Qxc2 g4 42. fxg4 hxg4 43. Kg1 Rd4 44. Qc7+ Kg6 45. Qc2+ f5 46. Qc3 Rc4 47. Qd2 Kh7 48. h3 gxh3 49. Nxh3 Qc6 50. Qe3 Rc1+ 51. Kf2 Qc2+ 52. Kf3 Rf1+ 53. Nf2 Rxf2+ *

