14. Bg5 {Black is in a tough spot. The knight on f6 is pinned, and e5 is a big threat. Only the Shankland Method will save him.} h6 {This is clearly the move Black wants to play, breaking the pin, but it looks like it loses a pawn. But we ask, what happens if I do it anyway? This is a fairly simple example, but it gets much tougher later on.} 15. Bxh6 Nxe4 {The tactics favor Black, and he gets his pawn back with a fine position.} (15... Bxh6 $2 {This is the wrong execution. After} 16. Qxh6 {Black cannot take the pawn back, and White is totally winning.} Nxe4 17. Nxe4 Bxe4 18. Ng5 $1) 16. Qf4 (16. Nxe4 $2 Qxh4 17. Nxh4 Bxh6 {Black equalized material and has a nice advantage thanks to his active bishop pair.}) 16... Nxc3 17. Bxg7 Kxg7 {We can stop here. Black has comfortably equalized using the Shankland Method.} *

