[PlyCount "102"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. Qb3 {The Russian System is my recommendation in my 1.d4 LTR in Chessable.} dxc4 6. Qxc4 O-O 7. e4 Be6 8. Qb5 Bd7 9. Qc5 b6 10. Qg5 c5 11. dxc5 Bc6 12. Qh4 bxc5 13. Be2 e6 14. Bg5 {Black is in a tough spot. The knight on f6 is pinned, and e5 is a big threat. Only the Shankland Method will save him.} h6 {This is clearly the move Black wants to play, breaking the pin, but it looks like it loses a pawn. But we ask, what happens if I do it anyway? This is a fairly simple example, but it gets much tougher later on.} 15. Bxh6 Nxe4 {The tactics favor Black, and he gets his pawn back with a fine position.} (15... Bxh6 $2 {This is the wrong execution. After} 16. Qxh6 {Black cannot take the pawn back, and White is totally winning.} Nxe4 17. Nxe4 Bxe4 18. Ng5 $1) 16. Qf4 (16. Nxe4 $2 Qxh4 17. Nxh4 Bxh6 {Black equalized material and has a nice advantage thanks to his active bishop pair.}) 16... Nxc3 17. Bxg7 Kxg7 {Black has comfortably equalized using the Shankland Method.} 18. bxc3 {The position is fairly balanced now, and nothing too notable happened for the next several moves until the Shankland Method showed its face again.} Qf6 19. Qe3 Nd7 20. O-O Rab8 21. Rfd1 Rfd8 22. h3 Bd5 23. a4 a5 24. Rab1 Bb3 25. Rd6 Bxa4 26. Ra1 Qe7 27. Ra6 Bb5 28. Bxb5 Rxb5 29. Ra7 Rdb8 30. Ne5 R8b7 31. Rxb7 Rxb7 32. Rxa5 Qd6 {What did Morozevich miss here?} 33. f4 (33. Qh6+ $1 {What happens if I do it anyway? As soon as you ask, the answer is obvious: White wins.} Kxh6 34. Nxf7+ Kg7 35. Nxd6 {White won a very critical pawn, and Black's structure is in shambles. My computer offers +4.} Rc7 36. Ne8+ {or Rb6 Ne4}) 33... Rb1+ 34. Kh2 Nxe5 35. fxe5 Qd1 {Just a move after missing a clean win, White now has a tough defensive task ahead. The computer claims this is dead equal, but in human terms, White's position is very difficult because his king will never find safety.} 36. Qf2 Qh1+ 37. Kg3 Rf1 38. Qxc5 Rd1 39. Qe3 Qf1 40. Kh2 Re1 41. Qd2 Qg1+ 42. Kg3 Re3+ 43. Kf4 g5+ 44. Kg4 Kh6 {Now White is in only move territory, and Morozevich did not find the way.} 45. Ra4 (45. Ra8 $1 Rxe5 46. Rh8+ $1 Kg7 47. Rh5 $1 {This was the only way to hold. I find it totally inhuman.}) 45... Rxe5 46. h4 f6 (46... f5+ $1 {This was a bit cleaner.} 47. Kf3 Qf1+ 48. Kg3 f4+ 49. Kg4 Qb5 $1 {Not the easiest move to see from afar. Black wins with ... Re3 next and the queen will come to f5 .}) 47. Kf3 {White can offer some resistance here, but I have to imagine he should lose in human practice. Indeed, he lasted only a few moves.} Qh2 48. hxg5+ fxg5 49. Re4 (49. Rg4 $1 {This was the only way.} Rf5+ 50. Ke2 Qg1 51. Kd3 $1 {White keeps fighting, though he is still not out of the woods. Only machines can play chess like this.}) 49... Rf5+ 50. Ke3 Qg3+ {White loses his queen, and he resigned here. While there were some interesting moments in the king hunt at the end of the game, ultimately the result was decided by who used the Shankland Method at a critical moment, and who didn't.} 51. Ke2 (51. Kd4 Rd5+) 51... Rf2+ *

