23... Be4 {White to play and win. Use the Shankland Method!} 24. Bxe4 {The more trades we make, the closer we get to a winning pawn endgame. This move looks impossible because it drops the f4 -pawn. But... What happens if I do it anyway?} Rxe4 25. Re1 {White would absolutely love to exchange off all of the pieces to reach a pawn endgame. Then, the protected passed pawn on d5 and Black's ineffective kingside majority would prove decisive. It looks like this move blunders the f4 -pawn. But, what happens if I do it anyway?} (25. d6 Ke8) 25... Re7 (25... Rxf4 26. d6 $1 {White makes a queen on the spot.} Rf5 27. d7 Rd5 28. Re8#) (25... f5 26. Rxe4 ({(not} 26. f3 {due to} Rxf4 {)}) 26... fxe4 27. Ke2 Ke7 28. Ke3 f5 29. c4 {Even though Black undoubled his pawns, his majority clearly will not make a passer.}) 26. Rxe7 {And the pawn endgame is lost.} *

