1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ (5. Qe2 {is mostly played nowadays.}) 5... exf6 6. g3 Bd6 7. Bg2 Qe7+ 8. Qe2 Qxe2+ 9. Kxe2 O-O 10. Re1 Nd7 11. d4 Nb6 12. Kf1 Bf5 13. c3 Na4 14. Re3 Rfe8 15. Nd2 h5 (15... Rxe3 {I would have taken the chance to compromise White's structure.} 16. fxe3 Bd3+ 17. Kf2 Re8 {Black is ready for ... h4 , with decent counterplay.}) 16. Nc4 Bc7 {Now, time to apply the Shankland method!} 17. b3 {This move looks impossible because Black can exchange off the e3 -rook to undermine the c3 -pawn. But, what happens if I do it anyway?} (17. Bd2 Rxe3 18. Nxe3 Bd3+) 17... Nb6 {Now, I had another opportunity to use the Shankland Method. This time, I failed to.} (17... Rxe3 $2 18. Nxe3 $1 {when} Nxc3 {is simply met by} 19. Nxf5 {and after Bd3+ Ke1! , it turns out Black is unable to take Nxc3 thanks to Kd2 .}) 18. Nxb6 (18. Na5 $1 {This would have been a better try to fight for an edge. The knight is fantastic on a5 , harassing the weak queenside pawns. Concretely, however, it looks like a bad idea because of ... Nd5 , but... Shankland method! I could have followed my own advice at the time.} Nd5 {and if} 19. Bxd5 $2 Bxa5 {, White will lose the c3 -pawn. After Nxb7! Nxe3+ Bxe3⩲ , White has fantastic compensation for the missing exchange.} 20. Be4 Bxe4 21. Rxe4 {Nc5} Re7 {Na6}) 18... axb6 19. d5 c5 20. Rxe8+ Rxe8 21. Bf4 Bxf4 22. gxf4 Kf8 23. Rd1 {I offered a draw with this move, which seems reasonable enough. The position is very equal. Luckily for me, my opponent declined the offer with a game losing blunder!} Be4 {White to play and win. Use the Shankland Method!} 24. Bxe4 {The more trades we make, the closer we get to a winning pawn endgame. This move looks impossible because it drops the f4 -pawn. But... What happens if I do it anyway?} Rxe4 25. Re1 {White would absolutely love to exchange off all of the pieces to reach a pawn endgame. Then, the protected passed pawn on d5 and Black's ineffective kingside majority would prove decisive. It looks like this move blunders the f4 -pawn. But, what happens if I do it anyway?} (25. d6 Ke8) 25... Re7 (25... Rxf4 26. d6 $1 {White makes a queen on the spot.} Rf5 27. d7 Rd5 28. Re8#) (25... f5 26. Rxe4 ({(not} 26. f3 {due to} Rxf4 {)}) 26... fxe4 27. Ke2 Ke7 28. Ke3 f5 29. c4 {Even though Black undoubled his pawns, his majority clearly will not make a passer.}) 26. Rxe7 Kxe7 27. c4 {The rest was trivial.} b5 28. cxb5 Kd6 29. a4 b6 30. Ke2 Kxd5 31. Kd3 f5 (31... g5 32. Kc3 gxf4 (32... g4 33. b4)) 32. Kc3 f6 33. b4 Kd6 34. a5 cxb4+ 35. Kxb4 bxa5+ 36. Kxa5 Kc7 37. Ka6 Kb8 38. h4 (38. Kb6) 38... g5 39. hxg5 fxg5 40. fxg5 h4 41. g6 h3 42. g7 h2 43. g8=Q+ *

