[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r1k2b1r/pppb1pp1/2n4p/2p1P3/5N2/1P3N2/PBP2PPP/3RR1K1 b - - 0 15"]
[PlyCount "10"]

15... Nd8 {What started as a harmless Berlin endgame has now become a comfortable edge for White. Though White's well-placed pieces look nice and pretty, White must turn that positional edge into something tangible. How should he continue?} 16. e6 {Shankland Method! Seizing the opportunity to break through and put to good use White's optimal piece placements. An initial glimpse at e6 appears to hang a pawn, but Pascal has precisely timed and calculated this decision, and the tactics work out in White's favor.} (16. h3 Ne6 17. Nxe6 Bxe6 {allows the blockade. Black must be better already.}) 16... Nxe6 (16... Bxe6 {Black could have potentially hung around with Bxe6 , but the positional ramifications of such a move were likely a strong turn off.} 17. Nxe6 fxe6 18. Ne5 {At the very least proves positionally devastating for Black. The pressure on the e6 and g7 pawns, while also maintaining control of the d and e files, is extremely advantageous for White. Though Black objectively is better off in this variation, the result would likely not be in doubt. Or Nxe6? Ne5+- With similar tactical consequences to the game. Black will be crushed on the f7 -square. Nd8} Nf7 19. Nxf7 {Re8+}) 17. Ne5 Be8 (17... Nxf4 $5 18. Nxf7 $3 {is potentially what Black may have missed} Rg8 {defending the Rook...} 19. Re8+ $3 Bxe8 20. Rd8# {Who needs Queens to deliver checkmate to a weak King!}) 18. Nxf7 Bxf7 19. Nxe6 Bd6 20. Nxg7 {Winning a clean pawn for nothing, and with it the game. The rest is simply a matter of technique.} *

