1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 9. Nc3 h6 10. b3 (10. Rd1+ {This would be the punishment, immediately forcing Black into a ... Ke8 plan. Whenever Black plays with the king on e8 , he would prefer to have his pawn on h5 instead of h6 . I cover this endgame in much more detail in my Berlin LTR here on Chessable.} Ke8 11. h3 {is the most principled way for White.}) 10... Bd7 11. Bb2 Ne7 12. Rad1 Kc8 13. Rfe1 c5 14. Ne2 Nc6 15. Nf4 Nd8 {What started as a harmless Berlin endgame has now become a comfortable edge for White. Though White's well-placed pieces look nice and pretty, White must turn that positional edge into something tangible. How should he continue?} 16. e6 {Shankland Method! Seizing the opportunity to break through and put to good use White's optimal piece placements. An initial glimpse at e6 appears to hang a pawn, but Pascal has precisely timed and calculated this decision, and the tactics work out in White's favor.} (16. h3 Ne6 17. Nxe6 Bxe6 {allows the blockade. Black must be better already.}) 16... Nxe6 (16... Bxe6 {Black could have potentially hung around with Bxe6 , but the positional ramifications of such a move were likely a strong turn off.} 17. Nxe6 fxe6 18. Ne5 {At the very least proves positionally devastating for Black. The pressure on the e6 and g7 pawns, while also maintaining control of the d and e -files, is extremely advantageous for White. Though Black objectively is better off in this variation, the result would likely not be in doubt. Or Nxe6? Ne5+- With similar tactical consequences to the game. Black will be crushed on the f7 -square. Nd8} Nf7 19. Nxf7 {Re8+}) 17. Ne5 Be8 (17... Nxf4 $5 18. Nxf7 $3 {Is potentially what Black may have missed} Rg8 {defending the Rook...} 19. Re8+ $3 Bxe8 20. Rd8# {Who needs Queens to deliver checkmate to a weak King!}) 18. Nxf7 Bxf7 19. Nxe6 Bd6 20. Nxg7 {Winning a clean pawn for nothing, and with it the game. The rest is simply a matter of technique.} Kd7 21. Be5 Kc6 22. Bxd6 cxd6 23. Re7 Bg6 24. c4 Rh7 25. Re6 Rxg7 26. Rdxd6+ Kc7 27. Rxg6 {Another pawn lost and the rest is filler to an already concluded story.} Re7 28. Rde6 Rd7 29. h4 Rf8 30. Rxh6 Rd1+ 31. Kh2 Rxf2 32. Rh7+ Rd7 33. Ree7 Rxe7 34. Rxe7+ Kb6 35. h5 Rxa2 36. h6 Rf2 37. h7 Rf8 38. g4 Ka5 39. g5 {This was a very clear and well executed display of the Shankland Method in a relatively simple position. But don't worry, harder ones are coming! Using short and accurate calculations, Pascal was able to justify a strategically strong breakthrough that, to an untrained eye, may have seemed futile. The theme of strong dynamics backing up optimal positional play will continue to be displayed throughout the duration of this course.} *

