[PlyCount "8"]

17... Ne7 {How should White proceed?} 18. e4 {The structure and the way White has set up his pieces are clearly begging for e4 to be played. It looks like there will be an issue after ... h4 since White's knight will be forced back, and his pawn center can get harassed. But what happens if I do it anyway?} h4 {Now, choosing the right route for the knight is critical.} 19. Nh1 (19. Ne2 $2 {This looks more active and was my first instinct, but following} dxe4 $1 20. fxe4 {Now} Nxe4 {would run into} 21. Qxf7+ {, but after Ng6! , White is unable to maintain the pawn on e4 , and will have to push e5 . Once Black can blockade on the light squares, his position is perfectly acceptable. e5 Nd5=}) 19... Ng6 (19... dxe4 $1 {Black should have tried this anyway.} 20. fxe4 Ng6 {Since the e1 -rook is not obstructed, White already has one defender of the e4 -pawn, and he can add another with the queen as well. Black still has some problems to solve, but he won't be demolished like he was in the game.} 21. Qf3 Qg4 22. Nf2 Qxf3 23. gxf3) 20. e5 {I did not have to be asked twice about this one.} Nh5 21. Bc1 {The bishop regroups to influence the kingside and fight for the f4 -square. White is ready for f4 to come next, and Black's position will collapse.} *

