[PlyCount "79"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 Nf6 6. e3 c5 7. cxd5 exd5 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Ne2 b6 10. O-O Ba6 11. f3 (11. dxc5 bxc5 12. Bxa6 Nxa6 13. c4 {is possible, but I was going for the transposition into an f3 -Nimzo.}) 11... Bxd3 12. Qxd3 Re8 13. Ng3 Nc6 14. Bb2 c4 15. Qe2 h5 16. Qf2 {Modern engines immediately shoot out this move as best, but this was not the case at the time of the game. I was very happy with finding this novelty, which puts the entire variation under pressure. It's even included in the Wikipedia article about the 2016 Olympiad!} Qd7 (16... h4 17. Nf5 Ne7 18. Nxh4 {is a safe capture due to} g5 19. Qg3) 17. Rae1 Ne7 {How should White proceed?} 18. e4 {The structure and the way White has set up his pieces are clearly begging for e4 to be played. It looks like there will be an issue after ... h4 since White's knight will be forced back, and his pawn center can get harassed. But what happens if I do it anyway?} h4 {Now, choosing the right route for the knight is critical.} 19. Nh1 (19. Ne2 $2 {This looks more active and was my first instinct, but following} dxe4 $1 20. fxe4 {Now} Nxe4 {would run into} 21. Qxf7+ {, but after Ng6! , White is unable to maintain the pawn on e4 , and will have to push e5 . Once Black can blockade on the light squares, his position is perfectly acceptable. e5 Nd5=}) 19... Ng6 (19... dxe4 $1 {Black should have tried this anyway.} 20. fxe4 Ng6 {Since the e1 -rook is not obstructed, White already has one defender of the e4 -pawn, and he can add another with the queen as well. Black still has some problems to solve, but he won't be demolished like he was in the game.} 21. Qf3 Qg4 22. Nf2 Qxf3 23. gxf3) 20. e5 {I did not have to be asked twice about this one.} Nh5 21. Bc1 {The bishop regroups to influence the kingside and fight for the f4 -square. White is ready for f4 to come next, and Black's position will collapse.} Nhf4 22. Bxf4 Nxf4 23. Qxh4 Nd3 24. Re3 Re6 25. f4 Rh6 26. Qg3 Qa4 27. Nf2 {Any number of moves won, but I like this one a lot. White trades off Black's most active piece for his most passive one, and he also makes sure that the third rank is clear so that the rook on e3 defends the c3 -pawn.} (27. Qf3 Rd8 28. Ng3 Qxa3 29. Nf5 Rg6 30. Qh5) 27... Nxf2 28. Qxf2 Qxa3 {Black equalizes material, but the pawn mass and piece constellation clearly suggest he should get checkmated. He did.} 29. f5 a5 30. Rg3 Kh8 31. Qf4 Qf8 32. f6 gxf6 (32... g6 {This would not have helped.} 33. Rff3 {White is ready for Rg3 - h3 next, and my computer claims mate in 16.}) 33. exf6 Re8 34. Qf5 {This was not the most incisive, but it was definitely good enough. I never did have the killer instinct of going after the king when simple moves do the job.} (34. Rg7 $1 {This would have been a good time to use the Shankland Method, but it was not strictly necessary.} Rxf6 {This move looks like an issue. But we always ask... What if I do it anyway? Not} 35. Qxf6 Qxg7 {but} 36. Qh4+ $1 {Kxg7 Qxf6+} Kg8 37. Rf5 $1 {and White wins.}) 34... Rh7 (34... a4 {After this one, Stockfish says Black won't be mated anymore. Fine, he won't be.} 35. Qxd5 a3 36. Qxc4 {He will lose down two pawns for nothing instead.}) 35. Rh3 {Now Black really will be mated.} Qg8 36. Rff3 Re1+ 37. Kf2 Re2+ 38. Kxe2 Qxg2+ 39. Ke1 Qg1+ 40. Rf1 *

