[PlyCount "14"]

11... Qxc6 {This was my first move out of preparation. Time to use the Shankland Method!} 12. e5 {This is clearly the move White wants to play. It might seem totally obvious that Black is lost, but one should notice the resource ... b4 . This would kick the knight off of c3 and gain the d5 -square for Black's own attacked knight on f6 . But we have to ask: What happens if I do it anyway?} (12. a3 {White is clearly better after a simple move as well, but it definitely lets a chance slip.}) 12... b4 (12... Nh7 13. Ne4 {and Black is strategically busted.}) 13. exf6 (13. Ne2 $2 {A lame move like this one would leave Black with a fine position.} Nd5) 13... bxc3 14. fxg7 {This is the key point that White needs to see in advance. Without the intermediate capture on g7 , we would be more or less lost!} Rg8 (14... cxd2 15. gxh8=Q+) 15. Qd3 Rb8 (15... cxb2 16. Qh7 {wins immediately for White.}) 16. b3 Rxg7 17. Bd4 Rg5 18. Be2 {This seems simple enough to me. Black cannot take on g2 as he will be mated on the open g -file, and White is ready to take on c3 with the bishop next. Black is done for.} (18. Qxc3 $6 {This was a very poor decision. There is no universe where Black should have been allowed to exchange queens.}) (18. Bxc3 {is premature due to} Rd5) *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
