43... Rc4 {Now, White is winning in more ways than one, but one way is the cleanest by far, and it can be found using the Shankland Method once again.} 44. Rxg4 {This is clearly the move White wants to play. It looks like ... e5 is a problem, winning a piece. But what happens if I do it anyway?} e5 (44... Ke8 45. Rf4 {The rest of the game was easy.}) 45. Rg5 {Tactically, this works for White. Black cannot take on d4 , and he may as well resign.} exd4 46. Rxd5+ {And the black rook is lost.} *

