{This was one of the most recent games I had included when the original Shankland Method came out.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Qd2 Nf6 8. O-O-O Be7 9. f3 b5 10. Kb1 h5 {So far, this is all normal theory. This particular line of the Taimanov was quite common at the time. Nowadays, the Taimanov is not super common, and when it is played, White usually does not go for the standard English Attack with Be3 , Qd2 , and O-O-O .} 11. Nxc6 Qxc6 {This was my first move out of preparation. Time to use the Shankland Method!} 12. e5 {This is clearly the move White wants to play. It might seem totally obvious that Black is lost, but one should notice the resource ... b4 . This would kick the knight off of c3 and gain the d5 -square for Black's own attacked knight on f6 . But we have to ask: What happens if I do it anyway?} (12. a3 {White is clearly better after a simple move as well, but it definitely lets a chance slip.}) 12... b4 (12... Nh7 13. Ne4 {and Black is strategically busted.}) 13. exf6 (13. Ne2 $2 {A lame move like this one would leave Black with a fine position.} Nd5) 13... bxc3 14. fxg7 {This is the key point that White needs to see in advance. Without the intermediate capture on g7 , we would be more or less lost!} Rg8 (14... cxd2 15. gxh8=Q+) 15. Qd3 Rb8 (15... cxb2 16. Qh7 {wins immediately for White.}) 16. b3 Rxg7 17. Bd4 Rg5 18. Qxc3 {This was a very poor decision. There is no universe where Black should have been allowed to exchange queens.} (18. Be2 {This seems simple enough to me. Black cannot take on g2 as he will be mated on the open g -file, and White is ready to take on c3 with the bishop next. Black is done for.}) (18. Bxc3 {is premature due to} Rd5) 18... Qxc3 19. Bxc3 Bb7 {Now White still has some work to do. Luckily, I was not punished for failing to be sufficiently incisive.} 20. Bd2 Rg6 21. Be3 {By taking the a7 - g1 diagonal under control, I am now ready for Rg1 next to untangle my pieces and complete development.} h4 22. Rg1 h3 23. g3 Bxf3 24. Rd2 a5 25. Bxh3 d5 {Black got a lot more saving chances than he deserved, and the computer thinks he is only a bit worse. Still, a pawn is a pawn, and White can push for a long time.} 26. Bg2 Bxg2 27. Rgxg2 a4 28. Kb2 f5 29. c3 axb3 30. axb3 Kf7 31. b4 Bf6 32. Kb3 Rg4 33. Rd3 {I like this move a lot. White uses this rook to secure the c3 -pawn and clears the second rank for the other rook to reach a2 .} Rc4 34. Ra2 Rbc8 35. Bc5 Rc7 36. Ra6 (36. Ra7 $2 R4xc5 {Oops!}) 36... Rd7 37. Ra7 {Now and only now.} Ke8 (37... Rxc5 38. Rxd7+) 38. Rxd7 Kxd7 {Despite my far-from-perfect play, I did manage to reach this position and to find the only winning move. Time to use the Shankland Method again!} 39. Bd4 {This is clearly the move White wants to play, but it looks like it blunders allowing ... e5 . But what happens if I do it anyway?} Be7 (39... e5 40. Bxe5 $1 {The point. Without this move, Black would be fine as is; he loses immediately.} Bxe5 41. Rxd5+ {and Black loses material.}) 40. h4 Rc8 41. h5 Bf8 42. g4 fxg4 43. Rg3 Rc4 {Now, White is winning in more ways than one, but one way is the cleanest by far, and it can be found using the Shankland Method once again.} (43... e5 44. Bxe5 Ke6 {would have complicated the game, but was still losing.}) 44. Rxg4 {This is clearly the move White wants to play. It looks like e6 - e5 is a problem, winning a piece. But what happens if I do it anyway?} Ke8 (44... e5 45. Rg5 $1 {Tactically, this works for White. Black cannot take on d4 , and he may as well resign.} exd4 46. Rxd5+ {and the black rook is lost.}) 45. Rf4 {The rest was easy.} Bg7 46. Rg4 Bxd4 47. cxd4 Rc1 48. Rh4 Kf7 49. h6 Kg8 50. h7+ Kh8 51. Ka4 (51. b5 Rc4) 51... Rc4 52. Ka5 e5 53. b5 e4 54. b6 Rc3 55. Kb4 Rc8 56. Rh3 Re8 57. Kc5 e3 58. b7 e2 59. Re3 {This was far from a perfect game, but using the Shankland Method was a big part of my reeling in the full point.} Rxe3 60. b8=Q+ Kxh7 61. Qh2+ *

