18... Rf6 {Now, Black's plan looks pretty straightforward. Lift the rook to g6 , put the queen on d5 , tear open the long diagonal, and give mate. White should be thinking of ways to stop this from happening.} 19. d4 {The best thing White can do is open the center as a means to invade Black's position and hopefully exchange some pieces. It looks like this move walks right into ... e4 when Black gets another attacker, but we ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} Rg6 (19... e4 20. Qc4+ Kh8 21. Ne5 {would transpose to something similar to the game.}) 20. Rfd1 (20. dxe5 Nd4 {Suddenly, all bets are off.} 21. Bxd4 Rxg2+ 22. Kxg2 Qg5+ 23. Kh1 Qg4 {and White is in trouble.}) 20... e4 (20... Nxd4 21. Bxd4 exd4 22. Rxd4 Rxg2+ 23. Kf1) 21. Ne5 Nxe5 22. dxe5 Qg5 23. g3 f4 {Black's attack looks menacing, but I found a clean and crisp way to diffuse it.} 24. Qc4+ Kh8 25. Qf7 {The bishop on b7 gets hit, winning me a tempo.} Bc6 26. Qe7 {And now, the queens come off. White is not out of the woods yet and still needs to be accurate, but I was fairly confident that I would win the game.} *

