1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. Ng5 d5 5. exd5 Na5 6. Bb5+ c6 7. dxc6 bxc6 8. Bd3 Nd5 9. Nf3 Bd6 10. O-O Nf4 11. Nc3 O-O ({Black should have gone for} 11... Nxd3 {. Now, I saved my bishop.}) 12. Be2 c5 13. d3 Nxe2+ 14. Qxe2 Bb7 15. Ne4 Nc6 16. Be3 {Black did not play very well in the opening and early middlegame, and he is worse here, but his next move did not do him any favors.} f5 {While it is always good to ask, 'What happens if I do it anyway?', you have to not pull the trigger if the answer is 'It does not work.'} (16... Qe7 $1 {Black should have saved his c5 -pawn. After} 17. Rfe1 f5 18. Nxd6 Qxd6 {White is surely better, but it's a game.}) 17. Nxc5 Bxc5 18. Bxc5 Rf6 {Now, Black's plan looks pretty straightforward. Lift the rook to g6 , put the queen on d5 , tear open the long diagonal, and give mate. White should be thinking of ways to stop this from happening.} 19. d4 {The best thing White can do is open the center as a means to invade Black's position and hopefully exchange some pieces. It looks like this move walks right into ... e4 when Black gets another attacker, but we ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} Rg6 (19... e4 20. Qc4+ Kh8 21. Ne5 {is similar to the game.}) 20. Rfd1 (20. dxe5 Nd4 {Suddenly, all bets are off.} 21. Bxd4 Rxg2+ 22. Kxg2 Qg5+ 23. Kh1 Qg4 {and White is in trouble.}) 20... e4 (20... Nxd4 21. Bxd4 exd4 22. Rxd4 Rxg2+ 23. Kf1) 21. Ne5 Nxe5 22. dxe5 Qg5 23. g3 f4 {Black's attack looks menacing, but I found a clean and crisp way to diffuse it.} 24. Qc4+ Kh8 25. Qf7 {The bishop on b7 gets hit, winning me a tempo.} Bc6 26. Qe7 {And now, the queens come off. White is not out of the woods yet and still needs to be accurate, but I was fairly confident that I would win the game.} e3 27. Qxg5 Rxg5 28. Rd6 Rc8 29. Re1 h5 30. Bxa7 Bf3 {Around here, I probably should have sacrificed the bishop on e3 to keep things simple- that would certainly be my instinct now- but I never did give Black a chance to come back.} 31. e6 (31. Bxe3 fxe3 32. Rxe3 {Black has no counterplay, and White has way too many pawns.}) 31... Rg6 32. c3 (32. Bxe3 {Again, this was extremely convincing.}) 32... h4 33. Rd4 (33. Bxe3 {You get the idea.}) 33... hxg3 34. fxg3 (34. hxg3 $2 {Not to be recommended!} Rh6) 34... fxg3 35. Rxe3 gxh2+ 36. Kxh2 {I made things messier than they needed to be, but I was always in control. Black is done for.} Bc6 37. e7 Re8 38. b4 Rg2+ 39. Kh3 g5 40. b5 *

