{Armenia won the 2012 Olympiad, with Russia taking second place. The two teams faced off in round 6, splitting the match 2-2, but Russia drew first blood when Kramnik beat Aronian. After Rac8 , it looks like the game might soon fizzle to a draw as everything is exchanged on the c -file, but the Shankland Method can quickly dispel that notion.} 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 cxd5 5. Bf4 Nc6 6. e3 a6 (6... Bf5 {is a straightforward equalizer.}) 7. Bd3 g6 (7... Bg4 {was a must.} 8. Nge2 {White can press a little bit, but Black is solid.}) 8. h3 Bf5 9. Nf3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Bg7 {The bishop will be pointless here.} 11. O-O O-O 12. Rfc1 e6 13. Na4 Ne4 14. Nc5 Nxc5 15. Rxc5 Qd7 16. Rac1 Rfc8 17. a3 Bf8 18. R5c2 f6 19. Nd2 Bd6 (19... Na7 {was the only move to stay in the game, but tough to find. The point is to meet} 20. Rc7 {with} Qd8 {.}) 20. Bxd6 Qxd6 21. Nb3 Rc7 ({Black should have tried} 21... b6 22. Nd2) 22. Na5 *

