1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 cxd5 5. Bf4 Nc6 6. e3 a6 (6... Bf5 {is a straightforward equalizer.}) 7. Bd3 g6 (7... Bg4 {was a must.} 8. Nge2 {White can press a little bit, but Black is solid.}) 8. h3 Bf5 9. Nf3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Bg7 {The bishop will be pointless here.} 11. O-O O-O 12. Rfc1 e6 13. Na4 Ne4 14. Nc5 Nxc5 15. Rxc5 Qd7 16. Rac1 Rfc8 17. a3 Bf8 18. R5c2 f6 19. Nd2 Bd6 (19... Na7 {was the only move to stay in the game, but tough to find. The point is to meet} 20. Rc7 {with} Qd8 {.}) 20. Bxd6 Qxd6 21. Nb3 Rc7 ({Black should have tried} 21... b6 22. Nd2 {.}) 22. Na5 {Armenia won the 2012 Olympiad, with Russia taking second place. The two teams faced off in round 6, splitting the match 2-2, but Russia drew first blood when Kramnik beat Aronian.} Rac8 {Here, it looks like the game might soon fizzle to a draw as everything is exchanged on the c -file, but the Shankland Method can quickly dispel that notion.} 23. Nxb7 {White would love to grab the loose pawn, but it seems properly defended. But we should always ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} Rxb7 24. Qxa6 Rbc7 25. b4 {White gets two pawns for the piece, and Black is in all kinds of pins with b5 coming next.} Qd7 26. Qb6 {White makes sure the knight remains stuck.} Qe8 {This move is geared at making sure that the queen is not hanging on d7 once the knight moves off of c6 . Kramnik voluntarily fell for the trap by asking 'What happens if I do it anyway?', though here, it has to be said that any number of moves win routinely.} (26... Rb7 27. Rxc6 $1 Rxb6 28. Rxc8+ Kg7 29. R1c7 {White gets the queen back and is totally winning with his two extra pawns.}) (26... Rb8 27. Qxb8+ Nxb8 28. Rxc7) (26... Nxd4 27. Rxc7 Ne2+ 28. Kh2) 27. b5 (27. Rc5 {This would have been my instinct, stopping any hope of ... Nxd4 tricks. Now, b5 comes next, and Black may as well resign here.}) 27... Nxd4 28. Rxc7 {Here we see the point behind retreating the queen to e8 . She is not hanging, and Black is free to play ... Ne2 and take on c1 . Still, Kramnik was ready for it.} Ne2+ 29. Kh1 Nxc1 30. Rxc8 (30. Ra7 {This looks a bit easier to me, hoping for Qb7 next, but the text is quite fine.} Rb8 31. Qc7 {or} Ra8 32. Rxa8 Qxa8 {Qxe6++- are winning.}) 30... Qxc8 31. Qc6 Qd8 (31... Qxc6 32. bxc6 {and the pawn is unstoppable.}) 32. b6 (32. Qxc1 {This was simple enough as well, but Kramnik's move was more direct.}) 32... Kf7 33. Qc7+ Ke8 34. Qa7 d4 35. b7 {Black had seen enough.} *

