14... Rc8 {This was a bit optimistic. I had to think, though - grandmasters don't ignore moves like Rxg7 with no plan in mind. I quickly figured out what he was hoping for.} 15. Rxg7 {What happens if I do it anyway? I understood that Black wanted to push ... h6 to bring the bishop to g5 , and my rook would get trapped on g7 . But, I had calculated accurately and realized that this would not work.} h6 16. Kb1 Bg5 17. Qh3 {And now we see White's point. While the rook is indeed trapped on g7 , the threat of Rxg5 means that Black is unable to play ... Kf8 .} Qd8 {This only hastened the defeat, but Black's position was well beyond saving anyway.} (17... Kf8 $2 {always fails to} 18. Rxg5) 18. Bxc4 Rxc4 19. Rxd6 ({My opponent tried Qe7 Rxd7! Everything wins, but this looks the cleanest to me. Qxd7} 19. Rxg5 {and you can check out the rest of the game, but we're done. If Kxd7 , Qd3+ should win as well but after} f6 {Qe6 Rxf7+ Kd6} 20. Qd3 {, Black will be mated in short order.}) ({Kf8 Rxd7! Qxd7} 19. Rxg5 {White has two knights and a pawn for the rook, and he is totally winning. The last point is to recapture correctly on c3 .} Rxc3 20. bxc3 {Keeping the queen on h3 and not breaking the pin with Qxc3?} hxg5 {Oops!}) *

