{Time for the Shankland Method after Ba3 .} 1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. Ne5 e6 {I really loved the piece sac line of the Slav in my earlier days, and I was a bit disappointed when modern engines sort of rendered it unplayable. But I did get my wins in before that happened!} 7. f3 Bb4 8. Nxc4 ({The piece sac is} 8. e4 Bxe4 9. fxe4 Nxe4 10. Bd2 Qxd4 {and I've played it a lot.}) 8... O-O 9. Kf2 {Nowadays, this is considered something like a refutation.} c5 (9... e5 {In modern times, this has emerged as Black's most testing move.}) 10. e4 Bxc3 11. bxc3 Bxe4 12. fxe4 Nxe4+ 13. Kg1 Nxc3 {All of this was still preparation. Nowadays, Qe1 is considered to be quite terrible for Black.} 14. Qd3 cxd4 *

