18. Bd3 {It looks like White can hope to consolidate his position and keep the pawn chain at bay with moves like Re1 and Nd2 next, stopping the e -pawn from getting past e5 . Eventually, he can activate his extra rook with h4 . But now, the Shankland Method comes into play.} e5 19. Nd2 (19. Re1 e4 {We can go ahead with this thanks to the loose knight on c4 .}) 19... f5 {What happens if I do it anyway? This is clearly the move Black wants to make, but it looks like the pawn hangs on f5 . Just peeling a bit below the surface shows that it is immune from capture, thanks to a check on e2 . Now, Black's center is rolling along.} 20. h4 (20. Bxf5 $2 Ne2+ $1 {Black wins the queen, and the game.}) (20. Re1 $1 e4 {and now White has to take with the bishop:} 21. Nxe4 fxe4 22. Bxe4 d3+ {is winning. E.g.:} 23. Qf2 Qxf2+ 24. Kxf2 d2 {. Instead, after Bxe4! , with a couple of only moves, White could keep resisting. His position looks very difficult to me, but the machine thinks he is only a little worse. d3+ is not working so well: Qf2 Qxf2+ Kxf2 fxe4 Nxe4 and Black still has some work to do.}) 20... e4 {The rest of the game played itself. Shove the pawns forward!} *

