1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. Ne5 e6 {I really loved the piece sac line of the Slav in my earlier days, and I was a bit disappointed when modern engines sort of rendered it unplayable. But I did get my wins in before that happened!} 7. f3 Bb4 8. Nxc4 ({The piece sac is} 8. e4 Bxe4 9. fxe4 Nxe4 10. Bd2 Qxd4 {and I've played it a lot.}) 8... O-O 9. Kf2 {Nowadays, this is considered something like a refutation.} c5 (9... e5 {In modern times, this has emerged as Black's most testing move.}) 10. e4 Bxc3 11. bxc3 Bxe4 12. fxe4 Nxe4+ 13. Kg1 Nxc3 {All of this was still preparation. Nowadays, Qe1 is considered to be quite terrible for Black.} 14. Qd3 cxd4 15. Ba3 {Now, time for the Shankland Method.} Nc6 {What happens if I do it anyway? Black develops a new piece and prepares for ... e5 to mobilize the central pawn mass, paying no heed to the hanging rook on f8 .} ({The automatic} 15... Re8 $2 {is a mistake.} 16. Nd6 {and Black can resign.}) 16. Bxf8 {This was probably not even the best. The bishop was better than the rook.} Qxf8 17. Qg3 {Trying to stop my pawns.} Qc5 18. Bd3 {It looks like White can hope to consolidate his position and keep the pawn chain at bay with moves like Re1 and Nd2 next, stopping the e -pawn from getting past e5 . Eventually, he can activate his extra rook with h4 . But now, the Shankland Method comes into play.} e5 19. Nd2 (19. Re1 e4 {We can go ahead with this thanks to the loose knight on c4 .}) 19... f5 {What happens if I do it anyway? This is clearly the move Black wants to make, but it looks like the pawn hangs on f5 . Just peeling a bit below the surface shows that it is immune from capture, thanks to a check on e2 . Now, Black's center is rolling along.} 20. h4 (20. Bxf5 $2 Ne2+ $1 {Black wins the queen, and the game.}) (20. Re1 $1 e4 {and now White has to take with the bishop:} 21. Nxe4 fxe4 22. Bxe4 d3+ {is winning. E.g.:} 23. Qf2 Qxf2+ 24. Kxf2 d2 {. Instead, after Bxe4! , with a couple of only moves, White could keep resisting. His position looks very difficult to me, but the machine thinks he is only a little worse. d3+ is not working so well: Qf2 Qxf2+ Kxf2 fxe4 Nxe4 and Black still has some work to do.}) 20... e4 {The rest of the game played itself. Shove the pawns forward!} 21. Bc4+ Kh8 22. h5 d3+ 23. Kf1 f4 24. Qh4 (24. Qxf4 Rf8 {and the skewer wins.}) 24... h6 25. Bb5 e3 26. Bxc6 bxc6 27. Nf3 d2 {White's extra rook clearly is not doing much for him here...} ({The computer has fun with} 27... e2+ 28. Ke1 Ne4) 28. Nxd2 exd2 29. Qg4 Ne4 30. Ke2 Qe3+ *

