{What should Black do after a4?} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 9. Nc3 h6 {This move order is not supposed to be great for Black, but this was not common knowledge at the time.} 10. b3 (10. Rd1+ {This would be the punishment, immediately forcing Black into a ... Ke8 plan. Whenever Black plays with the king on e8 , he would prefer to have his pawn on h5 instead of h6 . I cover this endgame in much more detail in my Berlin LTR here on Chessable.} Ke8 11. h3 {is the most principled way for White.}) 10... Be6 11. Bb2 c5 12. Rad1+ Kc8 13. Nd5 g5 14. c4 b6 15. h3 Kb7 16. Rd2 {Up to here, the game has looked fairly normal, and Black got a nice version of the ... Kc8 plans. Now, Bacrot demonstrated a fine understanding with a key regroup.} Ne7 {The knight on f5 is often misplaced, and Bacrot reroutes it to g6 instead.} 17. Rfd1 Ng6 18. Nf6 Bg7 19. Nh5 Rhg8 20. g4 Bh8 21. Kh2 a5 *

