[PlyCount "8"]

22. a4 {What should Black do?} b5 {Shankland Method time! Black clearly should be trying to break down White's queenside and hit him on the light squares, making use of his powerful, unopposed bishop on e6 . This move looks outrageous because two pawns can take it, but once you ask, 'What happens if I do it anyway?' the answer is quite easy to see - Black is good to go, and it only takes two moves of calculation to prove it.} 23. cxb5 (23. axb5 a4 $1 {White's queenside will collapse.}) 23... Bxb3 24. Rc1 Rgd8 25. Rxc5 Bxa4 {Black is opening the position for his bishops, and he has given himself a passed pawn. Despite material equality, the computer claims White is already dead lost.} *

