1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 9. Nc3 h6 {This move order is not supposed to be great for Black, but this was not common knowledge at the time.} 10. b3 (10. Rd1+ {This would be the punishment, immediately forcing Black into a ... Ke8 plan. Whenever Black plays with the king on e8 , he would prefer to have his pawn on h5 instead of h6 . I cover this endgame in much more detail in my Berlin LTR here on Chessable.} Ke8 11. h3 {is the most principled way for White.}) 10... Be6 11. Bb2 c5 12. Rad1+ Kc8 13. Nd5 g5 14. c4 b6 15. h3 Kb7 16. Rd2 {Up to here, the game has looked fairly normal, and Black got a nice version of the ... Kc8 plans. Now, Bacrot demonstrated a fine understanding with a key regroup.} Ne7 {The knight on f5 is often misplaced, and Bacrot reroutes it to g6 instead.} 17. Rfd1 Ng6 18. Nf6 Bg7 19. Nh5 Rhg8 20. g4 Bh8 21. Kh2 a5 22. a4 {What should Black do?} b5 {Shankland Method time! Black clearly should be trying to break down White's queenside and hit him on the light squares, making use of his powerful, unopposed bishop on e6 . This move looks outrageous because two pawns can take it, but once you ask, 'What happens if I do it anyway?' the answer is quite easy to see- Black is good to go, and it only takes two moves of calculation to prove it.} 23. cxb5 (23. axb5 a4 $1 {White's queenside will collapse.}) 23... Bxb3 24. Rc1 Rgd8 25. Rxc5 Bxa4 {Black is opening the position for his bishops, and he has given himself a passed pawn. Despite material equality, the computer claims White is already dead lost.} 26. Bc3 Bd1 27. Nd4 Bxe5+ 28. f4 Bxd4 29. Bxd4 Bb3 (29... Nh4 $1 {This was a clean finisher. White will not stop the knight from reaching f3 .}) 30. fxg5 hxg5 31. b6 {Gashimov finds the best practical chance.} (31. Rxg5 a4 {White equalizes material, but the a-pawn easily wins the game.} 32. b6 cxb6 33. Rb5 Rd6) 31... c6 (31... cxb6 32. Rb5) 32. Rxg5 Rd6 33. Bc3 Rxd2+ 34. Bxd2 Kxb6 (34... a4 $1 {I am not sure why Black avoided this move.}) 35. Be3+ Kb7 36. Rc5 {Now White can set up some semblance of a defense. His position is still difficult, and Gashimov eventually lost, but Black could have been much cleaner.} a4 37. Rc3 Bd5 (37... a3 38. Rxb3+) 38. Bc1 Ne5 39. Nf6 Nc4 40. h4 a3 41. Rb3+ Kc8 42. Rb1 a2 43. Ra1 Be6 44. h5 Ne5 45. Bb2 (45. h6 $1 {I'm not sure how White managed to not play this move, but time pressure can lead to strange decisions.} Ng6) 45... Nxg4+ 46. Nxg4 Bxg4 47. h6 Bf5 {The pawn is under control. Black still needs to work a bit, but he should win the game without a ton of trouble. And he did.} 48. Rf1 Ra5 49. Kg3 {Now taking on f5 is a real threat.} (49. Rxf5 $2 Rxf5 50. h7 Rh5+ $1 {This check saves the day.}) 49... Rb5 {Well spotted. White's bishop does not have a great square.} (49... Bg6 $2 {I could easily see a human making this mistake. What should White play?} 50. Rxf7 $1 {Shankland Method!} Bxf7 51. h7 Rh5 52. h8=Q+ Rxh8 53. Bxh8 {White will bring his king to a1 and make an easy draw. He can even sacrifice the bishop for the c -pawn when the time is right.}) 50. Rxf5 (50. Ba1 Rb1) (50. Bd4 Rb3+ $1 51. Kf4 Bg6 {The king on f4 blocks White from taking on f7 , and Black wins.}) 50... Rb3+ {An important intermediate check seals White's fate. Moving the king will allow Black's rook to get behind the pawn.} (50... Rxb2 $2 51. Ra5 $1 {White makes a draw.}) 51. Rf3 (51. Kg4 Rxb2 52. Ra5 {This only works with White's king back on g3 .} Rh2) 51... Rxb2 52. h7 (52. Ra3 Rb3+ $1) 52... Rh2 {Well spotted.} 53. Kxh2 a1=Q 54. Rxf7 Qh8 {This is still not easy, but Bacrot was up to the challenge. It's tough to beat a 2700 player! I highly recommend knocking them out the first time you have a chance because the second one might be much tougher if it even comes at all.} 55. Kg3 c5 56. Kf4 c4 57. Ke4 Kb8 58. Ke3 Qd8 59. Ke4 (59. Rg7 Qd3+) 59... c3 60. Rg7 Qh4+ 61. Kd3 c2 62. Rg8+ (62. Kxc2 Qc4+ 63. Kb1 Qb3+) 62... Kc7 63. h8=Q Qxh8 64. Rxh8 c1=Q 65. Rh2 Kd6 66. Rc2 Qb1 67. Kc3 Kd5 68. Rd2+ Kc5 69. Rb2 Qe1+ 70. Kb3 Kd4 71. Ka2 Kc3 72. Rb3+ Kc4 73. Rb2 Qd1 74. Rb8 Qc2+ 75. Rb2 Qa4+ 76. Kb1 Kc3 77. Rb6 Qd1+ 78. Ka2 Qd5+ 79. Kb1 Qf5+ *

