1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 7. Nb3 Be6 8. h3 Nbd7 9. g4 Rc8 10. Qd2 b5 11. O-O-O Nb6 {How should White proceed?} 12. g5 {This move might look obvious, but the response b5 - b4 is a bit intimidating!} Nfd7 {Now what?} (12... b4 13. Na4 $1 {This is the key move White needs to see before pushing g4 - g5 .} ({(} 13. Ne2 Nxe4 {or gxf6 bxc3 fxg7 cxd2+ are not an option.)}) 13... Nxe4 14. Qxb4 $1 Nc4 15. Nb6 $1 {Black is in bad shape and facing major tactical issues.}) 13. f4 {This is the move White wants to play, but it looks like it ignores the idea of Nb6 - c4 , which could kick the b3 -knight back to a1 . But we know what to ask.} exf4 (13... Nc4 14. Bxc4 bxc4 15. f5 $1 {The point. White does not retreat the knight and instead traps the e6 -bishop, and he is totally winning.}) 14. Bxf4 Ne5 15. Kb1 (15. Nd4 {I didn't like} Nbc4 16. Qg2 Qa5 {because I can't play} 17. Nd5 {due to the loose a2 -pawn.}) 15... Be7 16. Nd4 Nbc4 17. Qg2 O-O (17... Qa5 {can now be met by} 18. Nd5 {.}) 18. Nd5 Bxd5 19. exd5 Qa5 20. Bxc4 Nxc4 {How should White proceed?} 21. Nc6 {Other moves are good too, but this is obviously the most thematic and the one we'd really like to do. It seems like there is a problem, but is there really?} Qc7 {Black meekly retreated, but now White wins without having to calculate anything.} (21... Rxc6 22. dxc6 Nxb2 {This looks scary, but White has it under control. Now, not} 23. Kxb2 Qb4+ {but Bd2! when neither b4 Kxb2 nor Qa3 Bc1! White wins.}) 22. Rhe1 Bd8 (22... Rfe8 23. Qe4 $1 {when Black can't play} Bf8 24. Qxe8 Rxe8 25. Rxe8 {and Kf8 runs into Qxh7 .}) 23. Qg3 f6 24. Re6 fxg5 25. Bxg5 Bxg5 26. Qxg5 Rce8 ({If} 26... Rf7 {, not} 27. Ne7+ Rxe7 28. Rxe7 Na3+ {, which saves Black.}) ({Instead, Re7 Rxe7 Nxe7+ or} 26... Qb6 {Rxf7+- and White wins.}) 27. Ne7+ Kh8 28. Rg1 Nd2+ 29. Kc1 g6 30. Nxg6+ hxg6 31. Rxg6 {My opponent resigned here.} *

