1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 7. Nb3 Be7 8. f3 Be6 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O a5 11. a4 Nc6 12. g4 Nb4 13. Kb1 Rc8 {I always felt that the English Attack was not properly named. At least in the Najdorf with 6.Be3 e5 7.Nb3 , I find it very rare that White gets any real attacking chances. Rather, it strikes me as a positional opening. Black has weakened the d5 -square, and the best way to fight for it is to push g4 - g5 to bully the f6 -knight away. If White wants to do this, obviously he should castle long. In my opinion, White's next move showed some misunderstanding of the middlegame.} *

