{White's top priority should be to immediately kick the knight away from the defense of the d5 -square. I assume that White played h4 because he wanted h4-h5 to prevent Black from being able to meet g5 with ...Nh5 , but this was way too optimistic. After g5! Nh5 Bb5± , Black's position is a strategic trainwreck.} 14. h4 {Now, time to use the Shankland Method!} d5 {This is clearly the break Black wants to make. It looks like a bad idea because we will lose a pawn after g5 . But always ask... What happens if I do it anyway?} 15. g5 Ne8 16. exd5 (16. Nxd5 Bxd5 17. exd5 Rxc2 {and Black is clearly taking over.}) 16... Bf5 {With ... Nd6 coming next, Black's pieces look very menacing, and he has obvious compensation for the missing pawn.} *

