1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 7. Nb3 Be7 8. f3 Be6 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O a5 11. a4 Nc6 12. g4 Nb4 13. Kb1 Rc8 {I always felt that the English Attack was not properly named. At least in the Najdorf with 6.Be3 e5 7.Nb3 , I find it very rare that White gets any real attacking chances. Rather, it strikes me as a positional opening. Black has weakened the d5 -square, and the best way to fight for it is to push g4 - g5 to bully the f6 -knight away. If White wants to do this, obviously he should castle long. In my opinion, White's next move showed some misunderstanding of the middlegame.} 14. h4 {Now, time to use the Shankland Method!} (14. g5 $1 {White's top priority should be to immediately kick the knight away from the defense of the d5 -square. I assume that White played h4 instead because he wanted h4 - h5 to prevent Black from being able to meet g5 with ... Nh5 , but this was way too optimistic.} Nh5 15. Bb5 {Black's position is a strategic trainwreck.}) 14... d5 {This is clearly the break Black wants to make. It looks like a bad idea because we will lose a pawn after g5 . But always ask... What happens if I do it anyway?} 15. g5 Ne8 16. exd5 (16. Nxd5 Bxd5 17. exd5 Rxc2 {and Black is clearly taking over.}) 16... Bf5 {With ... Nd6 coming next, Black's pieces look very menacing, and he has obvious compensation for the missing pawn.} 17. Rc1 b6 {An excellent choice from Morozevich. Bringing the knight to d6 immediately would have allowed White to regroup with Nb3 - c5 - e4 .} (17... Nd6 $2 18. Nc5 $1 {Once the knight comes to e4 , Black's attack will be out of steam.}) 18. Bh3 Bxh3 19. Rxh3 Nd6 20. Rd1 {Shankland Method time again.} b5 {This is clearly the move Black wants to make, opening lines around White's king. It looks like the pawn is underdefended, but we ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} 21. Qe2 (21. axb5 a4 $1 22. Nxa4 {A sad necessity.} Nxc2 {Black's pieces are jumping to life and White's king is rapidly losing defenders.}) (21. Nxb5 Rxc2) (21. h5 bxa4 22. Nc5 Nc4) 21... Nxc2 ({Better than} 21... bxa4 22. Nc5 {and the queen is covering the c4 -square.}) 22. axb5 (22. Qxc2 {This was the last chance. After} b4 {Black will get his piece back and I don't like White's position, but the computer still thinks it is fairly balanced.}) 22... Na3+ (22... Rxc3 $1 {This was even stronger.} 23. bxc3 Nxe3 24. Qxe3 Qd7 {Black wins a tempo against the h3 -rook, and is ready for ... Nc4 , ... Qxb5 , and ... Rb8 to come soon.}) 23. Ka1 a4 24. Nc5 Ndc4 25. Bg1 Bxc5 {The rest was easy for Morozevich.} 26. Bxc5 Rxc5 27. bxa3 Qc8 28. Ne4 Rxb5 29. Rh2 Rb3 30. Rd3 Qb7 31. Nc5 Rb1+ 32. Ka2 Qb5 33. Nxa4 Rb8 34. Qc2 Nxa3 35. Nc3 Qc4+ *

