1. e4 {I had already locked up my first GM norm with a round to spare going into this game, and only needed a draw with white to clinch clear first in the tournament as well. I lost a very painful game to only finish in shared first, and my opponent beat me by using the Shankland Method extremely well. While I was the one who screamed about the "What happens if I do it anyway" question the most and it ended up being named after me, it clearly was still relevant and used long before I became a relevant presence in the chess world.} c5 2. Nf3 g6 3. d4 Bg7 4. c3 cxd4 5. cxd4 d5 6. exd5 (6. e5 {is known to be more ambitious.}) 6... Nf6 7. Bc4 O-O 8. Nc3 Nbd7 9. O-O Nb6 10. Bb3 Nbxd5 11. Re1 e6 12. Bg5 Nxc3 13. bxc3 h6 14. Bh4 b6 15. Qd3 (15. Ne5 {This would have been more incisive.}) 15... Bb7 16. Rad1 Rc8 17. c4 Qd6 {Around here, I started to play too defensively instead of trying to increase my initiative.} 18. Bg3 (18. d5 $1 {This looks very dangerous for Black.} exd5 19. cxd5) 18... Qb4 19. a3 Qa5 20. Bd6 Rfd8 21. Be7 Rd7 22. Bb4 Qf5 23. Qxf5 gxf5 24. Ne5 Rdd8 {Now the position is fairly balanced. The next two moves saw both me and my opponent using the Shankland Method, but once of us did it better than the other...} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 2"]
[Black "Shankland-Kacheishvili Exerci"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2rr2k1/pb3pb1/1p2pn1p/4Np2/1BPP4/PB6/5PPP/3RR1K1 w - - 0 25"]
[PlyCount "12"]

25. d5 {This is clearly the move I want to make, blowing up the position for my active bishops and rooks. It looks like it loses a pawn on d5 , but what happens if I do it anyway? I asked this question and got the wrong answer.} exd5 ({Black clearly wants to take the pawn. It looks like this walks right into Nxf7 . But, what happens if I do it anyway? Black is at least fine after} 25... Ne4 {but the text is better.}) 26. Nxf7 {Continuing down the wrong path, though White's position was already difficult anyway.} Kxf7 27. Re7+ Kg6 28. Rxb7 dxc4 {Here, I realized, to my horror, that my intended Rxd8 followed by Bxc4 would not work.} 29. Rxd8 (29. Bc2 Rxd1+ 30. Bxd1 Bf8 {I probably could have offered more resistance than I did, but White looks very lost to me.}) 29... cxb3 {Oops! The pawn is going through.} (29... Rxd8 30. Bxc4 {This would have been equal, resulted in a draw, and clear first for me.}) 30. Rxc8 (30. Rd1 b2 31. Bd2 Rd8 {was the cleanest of many winning moves.}) 30... b2 {I can't stop the pawn.} *

