1. e4 {I had already locked up my first GM norm with a round to spare going into this game, and only needed a draw with white to clinch clear first in the tournament as well. I lost a very painful game to only finish in shared first, and my opponent beat me by using the Shankland Method extremely well. While I was the one who screamed about the 'What happens if I do it anyway' question the most and it ended up being named after me, it clearly was still relevant and used long before I became a relevant presence in the chess world.} c5 2. Nf3 g6 3. d4 Bg7 4. c3 cxd4 5. cxd4 d5 6. exd5 (6. e5 {is known to be more ambitious.}) 6... Nf6 7. Bc4 O-O 8. Nc3 Nbd7 9. O-O Nb6 10. Bb3 Nbxd5 11. Re1 e6 12. Bg5 Nxc3 13. bxc3 h6 14. Bh4 b6 15. Qd3 (15. Ne5 {This would have been more incisive.}) 15... Bb7 16. Rad1 Rc8 17. c4 Qd6 {Around here, I started to play too defensively instead of trying to increase my initiative.} 18. Bg3 (18. d5 $1 {This looks very dangerous for Black.} exd5 19. cxd5) 18... Qb4 19. a3 Qa5 20. Bd6 Rfd8 21. Be7 Rd7 22. Bb4 Qf5 23. Qxf5 gxf5 24. Ne5 Rdd8 {Now the position is fairly balanced. The next two moves saw both me and my opponent using the Shankland Method, but once of us did it better than the other...} 25. d5 {This is clearly the move I want to make, blowing up the position for my active bishops and rooks. It looks like it loses a pawn on d5 , but what happens if I do it anyway? I asked this question and got the wrong answer.} exd5 {Black clearly wants to take the pawn. It looks like this walks right into Nxf7 . But, what happens if I do it anyway?} ({Black is at least fine after} 25... Ne4 {but the text is better.}) 26. Nxf7 {Continuing down the wrong path, though White's position was already difficult anyway.} Kxf7 27. Re7+ Kg6 28. Rxb7 dxc4 {Here, I realized, to my horror, that my intended Rd1xd8 followed by Bb3xc4 would not work.} 29. Bc2 (29. Rxd8 Rxd8 30. Bxc4 {would have been equal, resulted in a draw, and clear first for me. But... cxb3! Oops! The pawn is going through.} Rc8 {b2-+ and I can't stop the pawn. Or Rd1 b2} 31. Bd2 Rd8 {The cleanest of many winning moves.}) 29... Rxd1+ 30. Bxd1 Bf8 {I probably could have offered more resistance than I did, but White looks very lost to me.} 31. Bxf8 Rxf8 32. Rxa7 Rd8 33. Be2 b5 34. Rc7 (34. Kf1 $1 Rd2 35. Ra6 $1 {Black still has some work to do.}) 34... Ra8 {Well calculated.} 35. Rc5 Rxa3 36. f3 (36. Rxb5 Ra1+ 37. Bf1 Ne4 $1 {and ... Nd2 next will be decisive.} 38. Rd5 c3) 36... Rb3 {The rest was easy for Black.} 37. Kf2 h5 38. Kg3 Rb2 39. Bf1 h4+ 40. Kh3 (40. Kxh4 Rf2 {wins the bishop, and something like} 41. Bxc4 bxc4 42. Rxc4 Rxg2 {is dead lost.}) 40... Rf2 41. Bxc4 bxc4 42. Rxc4 Nh5 43. Rxh4 Kg5 44. g3 (44. Ra4 Nf4+ 45. Kg3 Rxg2#) 44... Rxf3 45. Kg2 Ra3 46. Rb4 Ra2+ 47. Kg1 Nf6 {The rest is not noteworthy.} 48. h3 Ne4 49. Rb3 Rc2 50. Ra3 Kf6 51. Ra6+ Ke5 52. Ra5+ Nc5 53. Rb5 Kd5 54. Rb4 Nd3 55. Ra4 Nf2 56. Ra5+ Ke4 57. Kg2 Nd1+ 58. Kh1 Ne3 59. Ra3 Kf3 60. Rb3 Re2 *

