{And after b5 , we should stop and use the Shankland Method to find White's best.} 1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 e6 6. g3 Qb6 7. Nb3 Ne5 8. e4 Bb4 9. Qe2 d6 10. Bd2 a5 11. f4 Nc6 12. Be3 Qc7 13. Bg2 {Morozevich was always one to play enterprising chess and was willing to take risks. Sooner or later, his pawn structure will be badly crippled once Black takes on c3 , and long term the queenside will be doomed. But White will get attacking chances.} a4 14. Nd2 Bxc3 15. bxc3 b6 16. Rb1 Nd7 17. Qf2 Rb8 18. O-O O-O 19. g4 {The computer does not love this move, but I think it is clearly best. White cannot sit and wait around for ... Ba6 and ... Na5 to come and collect his c4 -pawn, and Morozevich starts the attack.} Ba6 20. Qh4 {Not subtle. The rook is lifting to h3 next.} Rfe8 21. Rf3 Nf8 22. Rh3 *

