[PlyCount "14"]

22... b5 {What should White play?} 23. e5 {The Shankland Method works wonders here. White clearly wants to make this move to open up the g2 -bishop and get access to the e4 -square. It looks like he loses a pawn, but... what happens if I do it anyway?} (23. f5 {This would be much less effective. After} Ne5 $1 24. f6 Neg6 $1 {Black's knights defend his king quite nicely, and White cannot easily bring more pieces into the attack.}) 23... dxe5 {This is not Black's best move, but it is the only one White should be worried about. If you think that Black will not take on e5 , you push e4 - e5 and hit the clock. You can figure out the rest later.} 24. Be4 h6 25. f5 (25. g5 $1 {The machine claims this was even stronger, but I take no issue with the move Morozevich played in the game.}) 25... f6 26. Bxh6 gxh6 27. Qxh6 Qa7+ (27... Qg7 {This saves the king...} 28. Qxg7+ Kxg7 29. Bxc6 {But not the game. White takes back the lost piece, remains a pawn up, will soon win a second, and has a totally winning endgame.}) 28. Kg2 Qg7 29. Bxc6 {And White's good to go.} *

