1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 e6 6. g3 Qb6 7. Nb3 Ne5 8. e4 Bb4 9. Qe2 d6 10. Bd2 a5 11. f4 Nc6 12. Be3 Qc7 13. Bg2 {Morozevich was always one to play enterprising chess and was willing to take risks. Sooner or later, his pawn structure will be badly crippled once Black takes on c3 , and long term the queenside will be doomed. But White will get attacking chances.} a4 14. Nd2 Bxc3 15. bxc3 b6 16. Rb1 Nd7 17. Qf2 Rb8 18. O-O O-O 19. g4 {The computer does not love this move, but I think it is clearly best. White cannot sit and wait around for ... Ba6 and ... Na5 to come and collect his c4 -pawn, and Morozevich starts the attack.} Ba6 20. Qh4 {Not subtle. The rook is lifting to h3 next.} Rfe8 21. Rf3 Nf8 22. Rh3 b5 {What should White play?} 23. e5 {The Shankland Method works wonders here. White clearly wants to make this move to open up the g2 -bishop and get access to the e4 -square. It looks like he loses a pawn, but... what happens if I do it anyway?} (23. f5 {This would be much less effective. After} Ne5 $1 24. f6 Neg6 $1 {Black's knights defend his king quite nicely, and White cannot easily bring more pieces into the attack.}) 23... dxe5 {This is not Black's best move, but it is the only one White should be worried about. If you think that Black will not take on e5 , you push e4 - e5 and hit the clock. You can figure out the rest later.} 24. Be4 h6 25. f5 (25. g5 $1 {The machine claims this was even stronger, but I take no issue with the move Morozevich played in the game.}) 25... f6 26. Bxh6 gxh6 27. Qxh6 Qa7+ (27... Qg7 {This saves the king...} 28. Qxg7+ Kxg7 29. Bxc6 {But not the game. White takes back the lost piece, remains a pawn up, will soon win a second, and has a totally winning endgame.}) 28. Kg2 Qg7 29. Bxc6 {And White's good to go.} Qxh6 30. Rxh6 Red8 31. Ne4 {On another day, Leko might have resigned here. The game is clearly finished.} Kg7 32. Rxf6 Rdc8 {Now, anything wins, but I like Morozevich's choice, because he used the Shankland Method!} 33. cxb5 {It looks like this loses a piece to ... Rxc6 . But it's clearly the move White would most like to play, so the question needs to be asked.} Rxc6 34. bxc6 Rxb1 35. fxe6 {The e -pawn is immediately decisive.} Bd3 (35... Rb8 36. c7 {hitting the rook, and e7 is coming next.}) 36. e7 Bxe4+ 37. Kg3 Ng6 ({If} 37... Rb8 {, not} 38. exf8=Q+ Rxf8 {but} 39. c7 Rc8 {exf8=Q+} 40. Rf8 Rxf8 {.}) 38. e8=Q Kxf6 39. Qd8+ Kf7 40. c7 Rb2 41. Qd7+ (41. c8=Q Rg2+ 42. Kh3 Nf4+ 43. Kh4 Rxh2+ 44. Kg5) 41... Kf6 (41... Kf8 42. c8=Q#) 42. g5+ {Leko had seen enough.} Kxg5 43. Qg4+ *

