[FEN "rnb1kb1r/1p2pppp/pq1p1n2/8/3NP3/2N2P2/PPP1B1PP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 0 9"]
[PlyCount "14"]

9... Nc6 {Now, what should White do?} 10. Be3 {Use the Shankland Method! Clearly, this is the move White would like to play. He develops a new piece, defends d4 , and looks for discoveries against the queen on b6 . It looks like it drops a pawn on b2 . But, we have to ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} Qxb2 (10... e5 {was also possible. After} 11. Nf5 Qxb2 12. Na4 Qa3 13. c3 {, White has great compensation here as well. I got this exact position some years later against Debashis Das and had to try very hard not to win the game. Ultimately, I succeeded in making a draw, but Black's position is really tough.}) 11. Na4 Qb4+ (11... Qa3 $2 {It's important to have calculated this move well.} 12. c3 $1 Nxd4 {Now, always be wary of automatic moves...} 13. Bc1 $1 {White wins the queen, and the game, but not Bxd4?} e5 {, and Black is absolutely fine.}) 12. c3 Qa5 13. Nxc6 bxc6 {White has more than one good move here, but I do like the one I played in the game.} 14. Nb6 {It looks like this move hangs the pawn on c3 , but always ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} Rb8 (14... Qxc3+ 15. Bd2 {White wins the rook in the corner.}) 15. Nxc8 Rxc8 16. Qb3 {Black has a difficult position. Mainly, the c8 -rook may get into trouble.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 2"]
[Black "Shankland-Safarli Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r3k2r/1R2ppbp/p1pp1np1/8/4P3/2P1BP2/P3BKPP/1R6 b k - 0 21"]
[PlyCount "8"]

21... e6 {Now, Black is one move away from castling, which would solve a lot of his problems. White is totally winning if he finds the right way. The move I played was not bad, but there was a much better one.} 22. R1b6 {This is clearly the move White wants to play, bringing in the last piece and forking the pawns on c6 and a6 . I rejected it because Black will be able to advance ... d5 , and I won't be able to respond with e5 because after ... Nd7 , the b6 -rook will be hanging. But always ask... what happens if I do it anyway?} (22. Rc7 $2 d5 23. e5 Nd7 24. f4 {clearly favors White, but} O-O {and, although after} 25. Rxc6 {d5 e5 Nd7 f4 , White is still much better I really gave Black more defensive chances than I should have.}) (22. Rb8+ $2 Rxb8 23. Rxb8+ Ke7 {Black is more or less okay here.}) 22... d5 (22... O-O {wouldn't work now due to} 23. Rxc6 d5 24. e5 {and the knight can't retreat to d7 .} Ne8 25. f4 {.}) 23. Rb8+ {Now and only now. Black's problem is that since he pushed ... d5 , White will have a check on c5 available.} (23. Rxc6 {This works too, but I prefer the text for its simplicity and direct nature.} dxe4 24. Bc5 $1 Bf8 25. Bxf8 Rxf8 26. fxe4) (23. e5 {is met by} Nd7 {.}) 23... Rxb8 24. Rxb8+ Ke7 25. Bc5+ {Since White provoked Black into advancing ... d5 , this move is now available, and Black loses immediately.} *

