[White "Level 2"]
[Black "Shankland-Safarli Exercise #3"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2r3k1/3n1pbp/p3p1p1/3pP3/3B1P2/2P5/P3BKPP/1R6 b - - 0 27"]
[PlyCount "4"]

27... f6 {Now, I should have used the Shankland Method properly to find the best move.} 28. Bg4 {This should have been preferred. Black is forced to advance ... f5 to avoid immediate disaster.} (28. Bxa6 $2 {Always remember, the Shankland Method is to ask, 'What happens if I do it anyway?', not 'Do it anyway regardless of whether it works or not!'. This is clearly the move White would like to play, grabbing a pawn and hitting the rook. It looks like ... Ra8 will be an issue, hitting the bishop and going after a2 . I asked if I could do it anyway, and concluded that yes I could, and White would win. This was a miscalculation.}) 28... f5 29. Be2 {By provoking the f -pawn up to f5 , White's center is secure and no longer under assault, and the g7 -bishop leaves a lot to be desired. Black has a very difficult position, and I'd expect him to lose under normal circumstances.} *

