[PlyCount "79"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 Ng4 7. Bc1 Nf6 {After repeating moves once, I decided not to make a draw so soon.} 8. f3 Qb6 {This move does not enjoy the best reputation now, but at the time, it was considered a decent try to avoid the mainline English Attack. In the same Olympiad just a few days prior, Dominguez demolished Mamedyarov in this line in the Cuba-Azerbaijan match. Perhaps it was part of team Azerbaijan's preparation, as Safarli then chose it against me in the critical final round.} 9. Be2 {This move was extremely rare at the time, but I had an idea in mind.} Nc6 {Now, what should White do?} 10. Be3 {Use the Shankland Method! Clearly, this is the move White would like to play. He develops a new piece, defends d4 , and looks for discoveries against the queen on b6 . It looks like it drops a pawn on b2 . But, we have to ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} Qxb2 (10... e5 {This was also possible. After} 11. Nf5 Qxb2 12. Na4 Qa3 13. c3 {White has great compensation here as well. I got this exact position some years later against Debashis Das and had to try very hard not to win the game. Ultimately, I succeeded in making a draw, but Black's position is really tough.}) 11. Na4 Qb4+ (11... Qa3 $2 {It's important to have calculated this move well.} 12. c3 $1 Nxd4 {Now, always be wary of automatic moves...} 13. Bc1 $1 {White wins the queen, and the game, but not Bxd4?} e5 {, and Black is absolutely fine.}) 12. c3 Qa5 13. Nxc6 bxc6 {White has more than one good move here, but I do like the one I played in the game.} 14. Nb6 {It looks like this move hangs the pawn on c3 , but always ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} Rb8 (14... Qxc3+ 15. Bd2 {White wins the rook in the corner.}) 15. Nxc8 Rxc8 16. Qb3 {Black has a difficult position. Mainly, the c8 -rook may get into trouble.} Qc7 17. Rb1 Ra8 18. Qb7 Qxb7 19. Rxb7 {Despite the pawn less, White is doing very well in this endgame thanks to his big lead in development.} g6 ({Black should have tried} 19... e6 {.}) 20. Kf2 Bg7 21. Rhb1 e6 {Now, Black is one move away from castling, which would solve a lot of his problems. White is totally winning if he finds the right way. The move I played was not bad, but there was a much better one.} 22. Rc7 (22. R1b6 $1 {This is clearly the move White wants to play, bringing in the last piece and forking the pawns on c6 and a6 . I rejected it because Black will be able to advance ... d5 , and I won't be able to respond with e5 because after ... Nd7 , the b6 -rook will be hanging. But always ask... what happens if I do it anyway?} O-O {wouldn't work now due to} 23. Rxc6 d5 24. e5 {and the knight can't retreat to d7 .} Ne8 25. f4 {. Black would have to play d5 and now e5 can be met by Nd7 , but White has stronger moves.  A)} Rb8 $1 {Now and only now. Black's problem is that since he pushed ... d5 , White will have a check on c5 available.} 26. Rxb8 {Rxb8+ Ke7 Bc5+! Since White provoked Black into advancing ... d5 , this move is now available, and Black loses immediately.  B) Rxc6 This works too, but I prefer Rb8 for its simplicity and direct nature. dxe4 Bc5! Bf8 Bxf8 Rxf8 fxe4+-}) (22. Rb8+ $2 Rxb8 23. Rxb8+ Ke7 {Black is more or less okay here.}) 22... O-O (22... d5 23. e5 Nd7 24. f4) 23. Rxc6 d5 24. e5 Nd7 25. f4 {White is still much better here, but I really gave Black more defensive chances than I should have.} Rfc8 26. Rxc8+ Rxc8 27. Bd4 f6 {Now, I should have used the Shankland Method properly to find the best move.} 28. Bxa6 {Always remember, the Shankland Method is to ask, 'What happens if I do it anyway?', not 'Do it anyway regardless of whether it works or not!'. This is clearly the move White would like to play, grabbing a pawn and hitting the rook. It looks like ... Ra8 will be an issue, hitting the bishop and going after a2 . I asked if I could do it anyway, and concluded that yes I could, and White would win. This was a miscalculation.} (28. Bg4 $1 {This should have been preferred. Black is forced to advance f6 - f5 to avoid immediate disaster.} f5 29. Be2 {By provoking the f -pawn up to f5 , White's center is secure and no longer under assault, and the g7 -bishop leaves a lot to be desired. Black has a very difficult position, and I'd expect him to lose under normal circumstances.}) 28... Ra8 29. Bb5 Rxa2+ 30. Kg3 fxe5 31. Bxd7 exd4 32. Bxe6+ Kf8 (32... Kh8 33. Rb8+) 33. Rb7 {I had calculated the position to here and sort of just assumed White is winning. Black cannot stop a check on f7 , and then he will be forced into allowing a deadly discovery. Unfortunately, this was way too optimistic, and I had not properly considered his defensive resources.} Re2 (33... dxc3 {I had focused all of my efforts on this move, thinking that Black was doomed to lose to the discovered check and that he should only be looking for counterplay. Indeed, White does win here.} 34. Rf7+ Ke8 {Only move.} 35. Rxg7 {and now} c2 36. Rc7 {is trivial. Instead,} Ra3+ {looked a bit scary, but White does win the race.} 37. Kh4 $1 {(Not Rc7? d4 when the pawns are coming, and Black is good to go.)} d4 38. Kg5 {c2 Kf6! Black will be mated before the queen can matter.} Kd8 {Rg8+ Kc7} 39. Rc8+ {White takes on c2 , and wins.}) 34. Rf7+ Kg8 35. Bxd5 Re3+ 36. Kf2 Rxc3 37. Rd7+ {Black managed to put all of his pieces on defended squares, and I can't do anything with the discovered check.} Kf8 38. g4 Rc2+ 39. Kf3 Rc3+ 40. Ke2 {I was definitely annoyed with myself for letting a half point slip, especially as Azerbaijan won the match 2.5-1.5. Still, this meant I only scored 9.0/10 instead of 9.5, and I won the gold medal all the same, so the disappointment was short lived!} *

