[Result "*"]
[ECO "C48"]
[PlyCount "86"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. Nc3 Nd4 5. Nxd4 exd4 6. e5 dxc3 7. exf6 Qxf6 8. dxc3 Bc5 9. Qe2+ Qe6 10. O-O O-O 11. Qf3 d6 12. Bg5 {The game is fairly quiet, and one would expect a draw to result soon. Still, Black should be a bit careful since his development is awkward. The queen on e6 will lose a tempo once White brings a rook to e1 , and the bishop on c8 is passive and stuck. Kramnik found the easiest way to neutralize the pressure.} Qf5 {This seems like a very clean solution. Black offers a queen exchange, landing his bishop on f5 as well. It looks like this blunders Be7 , grabbing an exchange. But... What happens if I do it anyway?} (12... Qg4 {was fine too, with the same idea.}) 13. Be7 (13. Qxf5 {White should have traded the queens and acquiesced to a very dry position.} Bxf5 14. Bd3 {Anything other than a draw would be shocking.}) 13... Qxf3 14. gxf3 a6 {Well calculated by Kramnik. He realizes that White's bishop will be marooned if he takes the rook.} 15. Ba4 {This only makes matters worse.} (15. Bxf8 axb5 16. Be7 f6 $1 {White's bishop is trapped and will be taken.}) (15. Bd3 $1 Re8 16. Rfe1 Be6 17. Bg5 {White will probably feel a little silly for the last moves he has made, but he is only a little worse here and I'd expect him to make a draw fairly easily with good defense.}) 15... b5 16. b4 {Now, Kramnik found another important move.} Re8 (16... Bb6 $2 {Retreating the bishop seems obvious, but now White can go rook grabbing.} 17. Bxf8 bxa4 18. Be7 f6 19. c4 $1 {White is ready for c5 next to break the bishop out. Black is lucky not to be lost.}) 17. Rfe1 (17. bxc5 {This would not have helped.} Rxe7 18. cxd6 Re6 $1 19. Bb3 Rg6+ 20. Kh1 Bb7 {Black will take f3 and d6 , and he is totally winning.}) 17... Bb6 18. Bb3 Bb7 19. Kg2 d5 {White's b3 -bishop gets permanently marooned. All Black has to do to win the game is regroup his pieces and trade on the e -file.} 20. Re5 c6 21. Rae1 Bc7 22. R5e2 {Now, how should Black finish the game off?} Bc8 {This is clearly the move Black wants to play, regrouping the bishop to a square like d7 or e6 where it will be more active. However, this looks like it blunders the funny trick Bd8 . But we ask, what if I do it anyway?} 23. a4 (23. Bd8 Rxe2 24. Rxe2 Bh3+ $1 {This important resource seals White's fate. Black is not mated and maintains material parity.} 25. Kxh3 Rxd8 26. Re7 Rc8 $1 {Black is ready for ... Kf8 to kick the e7 -rook off of its active post, and then he will effectively be a piece up, thanks to the dead piece of wood on b3 .}) 23... Bd7 24. Bh4 Rxe2 25. Rxe2 Re8 {Once the rooks come off, Black can win the game on the kingside with his extra piece.} 26. Rxe8+ Bxe8 27. Bg3 Bd8 28. Be5 f6 29. Bb8 Bg6 30. axb5 axb5 31. Kf1 Kf7 32. Ke2 Ke6 33. Ke3 Bb6+ 34. Ke2 Bh5 35. Ba2 g5 36. Bb3 f5 37. Ba2 f4 38. Bb3 Kf5 39. Bd6 g4 40. Kf1 g3 41. fxg3 fxg3 42. Bxg3 Bxf3 43. Ba2 Be3 {Short resigned here. It's hard to play down a piece! ... Bd2 is coming next.} *

