1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 Ng4 7. Bc1 Nf6 8. Be3 Ng4 9. Bg5 h6 10. Bh4 g5 11. Bg3 Bg7 12. h3 (12. f3 {is wrong due to} Ne3) 12... Ne5 13. f3 Nbc6 14. Bf2 Be6 15. Qd2 Rc8 16. O-O-O Nxd4 17. Bxd4 Qa5 {This is one of the most important mainlines in the Najdorf. While 6...Ng4 has fallen out of favor in recent years, I have no clue why - I certainly can't find an edge for White, and whenever I've faced it, I've always seen Bc1 come in response. Gelfand had clearly done his homework well, and he handled the middlegame fantastically.} 18. a3 O-O 19. h4 g4 20. Qf2 {White makes the not-so-subtle threat of Bb6 . What should Black do?} ({White's main move is} 20. f4 Nc4 21. Bxc4 Bxd4 22. Qxd4 Rxc4 {with a roughly balanced position after, for example,} 23. Qe3 Rfc8 24. Rd3 b5 {.}) *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 2"]
[Black "Nakamura-Gelfand Exercise #1"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2r2rk1/1p2ppb1/p2pb2p/q3n3/3BP1pP/P1N2P2/1PPQ2P1/2KR1B1R w - - 0 20"]
[PlyCount "20"]

20. Qf2 {White makes the not-so-subtle threat of Bb6 . What should Black do?} Rc6 {This move looks incredibly obvious, but you have to realize that this does deprive the e5 -knight of a square on c6 . Once f4 comes, the knight could be in trouble. But Gelfand asked the right question that we know all too well.} 21. f4 Rfc8 {Bang. This is clearly the move that Black wants to play. It looks like it hangs the e5 -knight, but when we ask what would happen if we do it anyway, the variations are not too hard sort out.} (21... Nd7 $2 {A sad move like this one would justify White's play.} 22. f5 $1 Bc4 23. Bxc4 Rxc4 24. Nd5 $1 {Just like that, Black is falling apart.}) (21... Nc4 22. Bxg7) 22. fxe5 {Hikaru correctly chose not to capture the knight, but this has to be considered the critical move to evaluate when choosing to go for ... Rc6 and ... Rfc8 or not.} (22. Qg3 $2 Nd7 $1 {was the game continuation. Black exchanges off the d4 -bishop and prepares a winning sacrifice on c3 .}) (22. f5 {This was White's best chance to fight back, but after the simple retreat} Bd7 {Black is surely for choice.}) 22... dxe5 23. Be3 Rxc3 {Of course. Black's attack crashes through.} 24. bxc3 Qxa3+ 25. Kd2 (25. Kb1 Qa2+ 26. Kc1 Qa1+ 27. Kd2 Qxc3+) 25... Qxc3+ 26. Kc1 (26. Ke2 Bc4+ 27. Rd3 Qxc2+ {Black wins back his rook, and if by some miracle White avoids getting mated, he will be down 4 clean pawns.} 28. Ke1 Bxd3 29. Qxc2 Bxc2) 26... g3 {The most savage and aesthetically pleasing of many winning moves. White's queen runs out of squares to stay in touch with c2 .} 27. Qe2 (27. Qd2 Qa1#) 27... Bg4 28. Qd3 Qa1+ 29. Kd2 Qxd1# *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 2"]
[Black "Nakamura-Gelfand Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2r5/1p2ppk1/p2pbn1p/5P2/4P1pP/q1P3Q1/2PK2P1/3R1B1R w - - 0 27"]
[PlyCount "10"]

27. Qd3 {Now, Gelfand played a strong human move, but he could have ended the game on the spot with the Shankland Method.} Nxe4+ {This is the move Black really wants to play. It might look like White's king will escape, but we should always ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} (27... Rxc3 $2 {Don't be that guy.} 28. Qxc3 {And the f6 -knight is pinned, preventing the capture on e4 . Time to resign.}) (27... Bc4 {does win but made Gelfand's life harder after} 28. Qd4 d5 {. Here, Qa2 was the only winning move, according to the engine, which sounds weird.}) (27... Bd7 {is quite reasonable, intending to organize the black pieces with ... Rc5 -... Re5 and ... Bc6 .}) 28. Qxe4 Qxc3+ 29. Ke2 (29. Kc1 Qa1+ 30. Kd2 Qa5+ $1 {By getting the queen to f5 with tempo, Black is ready to play ... Bxf5 next, ending the game.}) 29... Rc4 {This is the important attacking resource that Gelfand probably missed.} 30. Qe3 Qxc2+ 31. Rd2 Qxf5 {White's king will not survive, and with 5 pawns for the rook, Black is not even down material!} *

