{White resigned, as he will lose one of his rooks in short order. E.g.: Kd2 Qxf5 .} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 Ng4 7. Bc1 Nf6 8. Be3 Ng4 9. Bg5 h6 10. Bh4 g5 11. Bg3 Bg7 12. h3 (12. f3 {is wrong due to} Ne3) 12... Ne5 13. f3 Nbc6 14. Bf2 Be6 15. Qd2 Rc8 16. O-O-O Nxd4 17. Bxd4 Qa5 {This is one of the most important mainlines in the Najdorf. While 6...Ng4 has fallen out of favor in recent years, and I have no clue why. I certainly can't find an edge for White, and whenever I've faced it, I've always seen Bc1 come in response. Gelfand had clearly done his homework well, and he handled the middlegame fantastically.} 18. a3 O-O 19. h4 g4 20. Qf2 {White makes the not-so-subtle threat of Bb6 . What should Black do?} ({White's main move is} 20. f4 Nc4 21. Bxc4 Bxd4 22. Qxd4 Rxc4 {with a roughly balanced position after, for example,} 23. Qe3 Rfc8 24. Rd3 b5 {.}) 20... Rc6 {This move looks incredibly obvious, but you have to realize that this does deprive the e5 -knight of a square on c6 . Once f3 - f4 comes, the knight could be in trouble. But Gelfand asked the right question that we know all too well.} 21. f4 Rfc8 {Bang. This is clearly the move that Black wants to play. It looks like it hangs the e5 -knight, but when we ask what would happen if we do it anyway, the variations are not too hard to sort out.} (21... Nd7 $2 {A sad move like this one would justify White's play.} 22. f5 $1 Bc4 23. Bxc4 Rxc4 24. Nd5 $1 {Just like that, Black is falling apart.}) (21... Nc4 22. Bxg7) 22. Qg3 (22. fxe5 {Hikaru correctly chose not to capture the knight, but this has to be considered the critical move to evaluate when choosing to go for ... Rc6 and ... Rfc8 or not.} dxe5 23. Be3 Rxc3 $1 {Of course. Black's attack crashes through.} 24. bxc3 Qxa3+ 25. Kb1 Qa2+ 26. Kc1 Qa1+ 27. Kd2 Qxc3+ {or Kd2 Qxc3+} 28. Kc1 ({(After} 28. Ke2 Bc4+ 29. Rd3 Qxc2+ {, Black wins back his rook, and if by some miracle White avoids getting mated, he will be down 4 clean pawns.} 30. Ke1 Bxd3 31. Qxc2 Bxc2 {)}) 28... g3 $1 {The most savage and aesthetically pleasing of many winning moves. White's queen runs out of squares to stay in touch with c2 .} 29. Qe2 Bg4 $1 30. Qd3 Qa1+ 31. Kd2 Qxd1#) (22. f5 {was White's best chance to fight back, but after the simple retreat} Bd7 {, Black is surely for choice.}) 22... Nd7 {Black exchanges off the d4 -bishop and prepares a winning sacrifice on c3 .} 23. Bxg7 Kxg7 24. f5 (24. Rd3 Nc5 25. Re3 Na4) 24... Rxc3 25. bxc3 Qxa3+ (25... Ne5 {The machine claims this was even stronger, but I take no issue with the move Gelfand chose, which feels much more human to me.} 26. fxe6 Rxc3 27. Bd3 Rxa3) 26. Kd2 Nf6 27. Qd3 {Now, Gelfand played a strong human move, but he could have ended the game on the spot with the Shankland Method.} (27. fxe6 Nxe4+) (27. Bd3 Qxc3+) 27... Bc4 (27... Rxc3 $2 {Don't be that guy.} 28. Qxc3 {And the f6 -knight is pinned, preventing the capture on e4 . Time to resign.}) (27... Nxe4+ $1 {This is the move Black really wants to play. It might look like White's king will escape, but we should always ask what happens if I do it anyway?} 28. Qxe4 Qxc3+ {and if} 29. Kc1 Qa1+ 30. Kd2 Qa5+ $1 {, by getting the queen to f5 with tempo, Black is ready to play ... Bxf5 next, ending the game. White's best is} 31. Ke2 Rc4 $1 {This is the important attacking resource that Gelfand probably missed.} 32. Qe3 {Qxc2+ Rd2} Qxf5 {White's king will not survive, and with 5 pawns for the rook, Black is not even down material!}) (27... Bd7 {is quite reasonable, intending to organize the black pieces with ... Rc5 -... Re5 and ... Bc6 .}) 28. Qd4 d5 (28... Qa2 {is the only winning move, according to the engine, which sounds weird.}) 29. exd5 Bxd5 30. Rg1 Be4 31. Bd3 {To his credit, Hikaru has defended very well up to here, and it's a game again. Gelfand did not get mad at himself for missing an earlier win that I'm sure he felt was there and kept playing strong moves.} Qa5 (31... Bxd3 32. Ra1) 32. Qb4 (32. Bxe4 Rd8) 32... Qc7 {Black uses the Shankland Method perfectly. This is clearly the move he wants to play, avoiding the queen exchange at all costs. It looks like it drops a piece on e4 , but... What if I do it anyway?} (32... Qxf5 $2 33. Rgf1 {White is ready to take on f6 next, and he is very much back in the game.}) 33. Bxe4 a5 (33... Qf4+ {This was fine immediately as well, but Gelfand's move was even stronger.}) 34. Qxb7 (34. Qd4 Rd8) 34... Qf4+ 35. Ke2 Rc7 36. Qb6 Nxe4 {White is definitely done for, but you still have to put him away. Gelfand did so in fine style.} 37. Qd4+ Kh7 38. c4 {Now, what is the cleanest finisher?} Rd7 {This is the best way to include the rook in the attack. It looks like it hangs, but what happens if we do it anyway?} 39. Qe3 (39. Qxd7 Qf2+ 40. Kd3 Nc5+ {Black wins the queen, and the game.}) 39... Ng3+ 40. Qxg3 Qxg3 41. Rxd7 Qe5+ *

