[FEN "r1b2rk1/2qn1pb1/p4npp/1p2p3/4P1PB/PNN2Q2/1PP4P/2KR1B1R b - - 0 15"]
[PlyCount "10"]

15... Rb8 {While this move is a serious error objectively, in human terms, it makes perfect sense. Any Najdorf player knows the idea of playing ... Rb8 before bringing the bishop to b7 so that Black can play ... b4 and open the b -file. Indeed, if Black was allowed to play ... b4 next, take back with the rook, and only then bring the bishop to b7 , his position would look fantastic. But White can immediately reach a nice position using the Shankland Method.} 16. g5 {This is the move White wants to play, exchanging off a key defender on Black's kingside and opening the h3 - c8 diagonal for the other bishop to abuse. It looks like this will allow Black to successfully execute his plan with ... b4 , but we ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} (16. Rg1 {is too slow:} b4 17. axb4 Rxb4 18. Rxd7 Nxd7 19. Nd5 {doesn't work well for White.} Qb8 {White can take the exchange, but there's not mate.}) 16... hxg5 17. Bxg5 {At this point, Black has a tough position no matter what he does, but he could have done himself a favor by using the Shankland Method.} b4 {Obviously, this was the move Black had in mind. Once I played g5 , he must have realized the tactical issue, but he should have pressed on all the same by asking what would happen if he did it anyway.} (17... Bb7 {This is an admission of failure. ... Rb8 serves no purpose if Black will not play ... b4 , and a tempo lost in such a dynamic middlegame is often decisive.}) 18. axb4 Rxb4 19. Rxd7 {Now, we see the point of g5 . By removing the h -pawn, Black will not be able to play ... Nxd7 due to Nd5 and mate on e7 .} ({The machine claims that} 19. h4 {is stronger.}) 19... Nxd7 (19... Bxd7 $1 20. Bxf6 Be6 $1 {White is surely for choice here, but Black is not without counterplay. His rooks will be pretty good.}) 20. Nd5 (20. Nd5 {Black is already up an exchange, and he should not be too worried about White taking it back on b4 . But... Ne7 , followed by Qh3 , is a bigger concern. White wins.} Qb7 {White doesn't even bother to win an exchange because} 21. Ne7+ {leads to mate.}) *

