1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 Nbd7 7. Qe2 h6 8. Bh4 g6 9. f4 e5 10. fxe5 dxe5 11. O-O-O Qc7 (11... exd4 12. e5 {is horrible for Black.}) 12. Nb3 b5 13. Qf3 Bg7 14. g4 O-O 15. a3 {In a sharp Najdorf middlegame, my opponent made a typical Grandmaster mistake.} Rb8 {While this move is a serious error objectively, in human terms, it makes perfect sense. Any Najdorf player knows the idea of playing ... Rb8 before bringing the bishop to b7 so that Black can play ... b4 and open the b -file. Indeed, if Black was allowed to play ... b4 next, take back with the rook, and only then bring the bishop to b7 , his position would look fantastic. But White can immediately reach a nice position using the Shankland Method.} (15... Bb7 $1 {Simple was prudent. Black should be okay here.} 16. g5 hxg5 17. Bxg5 Rac8 18. Bd3 Nh5 $1 {The knight will look nice on f4 . I don't see any major issues for Black. Note that Bh3? was crushing in the game with Black's rook on b8 , but with the rook on c8 , it is a very different story. Bxe4-+ Game over.}) 16. g5 {This is the move White wants to play, exchanging off a key defender on Black's kingside and opening the h3 - c8 diagonal for the other bishop to abuse. It looks like this will allow Black to successfully execute his plan with ... b4 , but we ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} (16. Rg1 {is too slow:} b4 17. axb4 Rxb4 18. Rxd7 Nxd7 19. Nd5 {doesn't work well for White.} Qb8 {White can take the exchange, but there's not mate.}) 16... hxg5 17. Bxg5 {At this point, Black has a tough position no matter what he does, but he could have done himself a favor by using the Shankland Method.} Bb7 {This is an admission of failure. ... Rb8 serves no purpose if Black will not play ... b4 , and a tempo lost in such a dynamic middlegame is often decisive.} (17... b4 $1 {Once I played g5 , he must have realized the tactical issue, but he should have pressed on all the same by asking what would happen if he did it anyway.} 18. axb4 Rxb4 {and here the strongest move, according to the engine, is} 19. h4 {, but White's point is Rxd7! Now, we see the idea of g5 . By removing the h -pawn, Black will not be able to play ... Nxd7 due to Nd5 and mate on e7 . Nxd7?} Nd5 {Black is already up an exchange, and he should not be too worried about White taking it back on b4 . But... Ne7 , followed by Qh3 , is a bigger concern. White wins. Qb7 White doesn't even bother to win an exchange because Ne7+ leads to mate. After Bxd7!} 20. Bf6 {Be6! , White is surely for choice here, but Black is not without counterplay. His rooks will be pretty good.}) 18. Bh3 {Now, Black's knights are horribly frozen, and since his rook is on b8 rather than c8 , he doesn't have any tactics available. The machine is claiming he is losing material on the spot.} Bc6 19. Bxd7 Nxd7 20. Be7 Rfe8 21. Bd6 Qb6 22. Bxb8 Rxb8 {The first rule of any exchange sacrifice is to always ask, 'How good are the rooks?'. If the rooks are good, the exchange sacrifice will just about always fail, regardless of whatever other compensation there may be. Here, White's rooks are excellent, and my machine offers +4.} 23. Rhf1 Rf8 24. h4 {There were other good moves, but I like this one the most. To some degree, one could argue that this move uses the Shankland Method. Clearly, we want h4 - h5 to open up Black's king, but it looks like this walks into ... Nf6 since the knight wanted to go to h5 and f4 anyway. But... we know what to ask.} a5 (24... Nf6 {If Black got to play ... Nh5 next, he would not have such a bad position. Thankfully, he will not make it in time.} 25. Rd6 $1 {White wins material on the spot.} Nh5 26. Nd5 {The c6 -bishop will be lost.}) 25. h5 {The rest was easy.} a4 26. Nd2 {The rational approach.} ({I spent some time calculating} 26. hxg6 fxg6 27. Qh3 axb3 28. Qe6+ Kh7 29. Rh1+ Bh6+ 30. Kb1 {and here I thought Black could go} bxc2+ 31. Kxc2 Bxe4+ 32. Nxe4 Qxe6 {missing I had} 33. Ng5+ {at the end of the line.}) 26... b4 27. axb4 Qxb4 28. hxg6 fxg6 29. Qh3 Re8 (29... Rb8 30. Qe6+ Kh7 31. Rh1+ Bh6 {isn't even check thanks to the knight.} 32. Qf7+) 30. Nd5 Qb5 31. Nc7 {Black couldn't save the game anyway, but this sped matters.} *

