1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Bd3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Bd3 a6 9. e4 c5 10. e5 cxd4 11. Nxb5 Ng4 (11... axb5 12. exf6 gxf6 {was discussed in the Anand-Kramnik Wch match.}) 12. Nbxd4 (12. Qa4 {I think this is better.}) 12... Bb7 (12... Bb4+ {is a clear way to equalize.}) 13. Qa4 Qb6 14. O-O Bc5 15. h3 Bxf3 16. hxg4 Bd5 17. Nf3 h5 {I was caught by surprise when my opponent played the Semi-Slav and had not reviewed the Meran at all. Up to here, the game has followed known paths, though neither of us was aware of it at the time. Black is currently down a pawn but has decent compensation, thanks to his active pieces.} (17... Bxf3 18. gxf3 {Black can get the pawn back, but I would get rid of that monster bishop.}) *

