18... O-O {How should White proceed?} 19. Be4 {White would absolutely love to trade off the monstrous bishop on d5 and get the e4 -square for the queen. This move looks like it blunders a trick, with ... Bxf2 + followed by ... Nc5 in the air. But we must ask... What if I do it anyway?} (19. Qxd7 $2 Bc6 $1 {White's queen is trapped, and he will have to immediately pitch back the extra piece with Bh7 + in order to save her. Black will then be better despite the pawn less, thanks to his activity and his particularly powerful light-squared bishop.}) 19... Bxf2+ {In order to play Be4 , we need to notice that this one does not work.} (19... Bxe4 20. Qxe4 {White has consolidated the extra pawn and is much better.}) 20. Rxf2 Nc5 21. Qd4 {The pin on the c5 -knight is decisive. Black cannot take with the knight on e4 , but taking with the bishop leaves him with too many hanging pieces.} Bxe4 (21... Nxe4 {is not possible due to the pin:} 22. Qxb6) 22. b4 Bxf3 23. bxc5 {White wins a piece and the game.} *

