1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Bd3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Bd3 a6 9. e4 c5 10. e5 cxd4 11. Nxb5 Ng4 (11... axb5 12. exf6 gxf6 {was discussed in the Anand-Kramnik Wch match.}) 12. Nbxd4 (12. Qa4 {I think this is better.}) 12... Bb7 (12... Bb4+ {is a clear way to equalize.}) 13. Qa4 Qb6 14. O-O Bc5 15. h3 Bxf3 16. hxg4 Bd5 17. Nf3 h5 (17... Bxf3 18. gxf3 {Black can get the pawn back, but I would get rid of that monster bishop.}) 18. g5 {I was caught by surprise when my opponent played the Semi-Slav and had not reviewed the Meran at all. Up to here, the game has followed known paths, though neither of us was aware of it at the time. Black is currently down a pawn but has decent compensation, thanks to his active pieces. Fortunately for me, my opponent's next move was a big mistake that allowed me to quickly reach a fantastic position.} ({White would love to play} 18. Be4 hxg4 19. Bxd5 {However, instead of the automatic recapture} exd5 20. Qxg4 {, the 'invisible move' g3! wins on the spot!}) 18... O-O {How should White proceed?} 19. Be4 {White would absolutely love to trade off the monstrous bishop on d5 and get the e4 -square for the queen. This move looks like it blunders a trick, with ... Bxf2 + followed by ... Nc5 in the air. But we must ask... What if I do it anyway?} (19. Qxd7 $2 Bc6 $1 {White's queen is trapped, and he will have to immediately pitch back the extra piece with Bh7 + in order to save her. Black will then be better despite the pawn less, thanks to his activity and his particularly powerful light-squared bishop.}) 19... Bxe4 (19... Bxf2+ {In order to play Be4 , we need to notice that this one does not work.} 20. Rxf2 Nc5 21. Qd4 $1 {The pin on the c5 -knight is decisive. Black cannot take with the knight on e4 , but taking with the bishop leaves him with too many hanging pieces.} Nxe4 {is not possible due to the pin} 22. Qxb6 {, and if Bxe4 b4 Bxf3 bxc5+- White wins a piece and the game.}) 20. Qxe4 {White has consolidated the extra pawn and is much better.} g6 21. b3 Rfd8 22. Bd2 a5 23. Rac1 Rac8 24. Rc4 Qa7 25. Qc2 {A powerful move, pinning the c5 -bishop and getting ready for Be3 next to neutralize the pressure.} Rc7 26. Be3 Rdc8 27. Rc1 Qa6 (27... Bxe3 28. Rxc7 Rxc7 29. Qxc7 {is winning for White. Compare with the note to my 28th move.}) 28. Rd1 {The cleanest way to force a victory. Black has nothing to say about Rxd7 coming next.} (28. a4 {is not as effective:} Bxe3 29. Rxc7 Rxc7 30. Qxc7 {Here Black's queen is not attacked, so} Bxc1 31. Qxc1 Qd3 {and the game goes on.}) 28... Nb6 (28... Bxe3 29. Rxc7 Rxc7 30. Qxc7 {My rook is safe on d1 .}) (28... Bb6 29. Rxd7) 29. Rxc5 Rxc5 30. Bxc5 Qb5 31. a4 Rxc5 (31... Qxc5 32. Rd8+ $1 {White can force a knight endgame.} Kg7 33. Rxc8 Qxc8 34. Qxc8 Nxc8 35. Nd2 {Black is very much done for.}) 32. axb5 Rxc2 33. Rd6 Nd5 34. b6 Rc8 35. Nd2 (35. b7 {gives Black some saving chances:} Rb8 36. Rd7 Kf8 37. Nd4 Ke8 38. Nc6 Kxd7 39. Nxb8+ Kc7 40. Nc6 Kxb7 {After collecting the b-pawn, Black definitely has drawing chances.}) 35... Kf8 36. Nc4 Rb8 37. g3 *

