13. Rb1 {At this point, Black is under unpleasant pressure. It is not so easy to complete development, but I was able to successfully use the Shankland Method to good effect.} b6 ({This is the move Black wants to play, getting ready for ...Nc4 next, as the immediate} 13... Nc4 {runs into} 14. Rxb7 {. It looks like it walks straight into Ba6 , which will greatly hinder Black's development. But we ask... What if I do it anyway?} ({Be7 A tame move like this one won't do.} 14. Nf3 {Black still cannot castle due to the hanging pawn on h7 , and his position definitely feels a bit uncomfortable.})) 14. Ba6 Nc4 {What happens if I do it anyway? As it turns out, Black will surprisingly take over the initiative very quickly if White grabs the exchange.} (14... Ra8 {This would be sad. After} 15. Nf3 {Black can never really hope to bring the knight to c4 since he will then have to take back with the pawn, and White is pleasantly better.}) 15. Rb3 (15. Bxc8 Qxc8 {It is quite annoying to deal with the threat of Nc4 - a3 , and the computer thinks that White should be careful to hang on. It's quite easy to go wrong.  A )} 16. Rd1 Qa6 {and Black is ready for ... Ba4 .} 17. Qd3 Bb5 {or} 18. Qe2 {Bb5 are very uncomfortable for White.  B ) Qd3 and Qa6!? is fully playable: Nf3 Bb5 Black has obvious compensation for the missing exchange. Even stronger is} Na3 {Rb3 Black has any number of decent moves here, but I like using the Shankland Method again. Bb5!? What happens if I do it anyway? Rxb5 Qa6! Black wins back his lost rook and has a very comfortable position.}) 15... Ba4 16. Bxc8 Qxc8 {Black will eventually get the exchange back with a fine position.} *

