f4 19. Nxf4 {Nothing too notable happened in the next phase of the game, but I did manage to outplay my opponent to some degree to create another chance to use the Shankland Method.} O-O 20. Qe2 Bxb3 21. axb3 Na5 22. Qb2 Qd7 23. Ra1 Rc8 24. Nd3 Qb5 25. Nb4 g6 26. Kh2 Rc7 27. Ra4 h5 28. h4 Kg7 29. g3 {This position looks as dry as it can be, and indeed, the machine has screamed 0.00 for all of the preceding moves. But here, I missed a chance.} Nc4 {This move is not too hard to spot, and I did play it, but I had completely missed my opponent's response.} 30. Qc2 (30. Qa2 {I had only considered this move for some reason, threatening both the c4 -knight and the a7 -pawn, and here I did see the way. Funnily enough, the machine thinks the simple move} Nd6 {is even stronger than Nxe3! What happens if I do it anyway? fxe3 a5 White's knight is hit and will be lost, and he has a totally lost position thanks to Black's superior activity and chances to hunt down the white king. E.g.:} 31. Nc2 Qe2) 30... Na5 {My plan was to repeat the position and find something better, but this was obviously foolish. The game was drawn just a couple moves later. I don't know how I managed to not bring the knight to d6 instead.} (30... Nd6 $1 31. Nd3 {and here,} Qc6 32. Ne5 {is annoying, but after} a5 {, White is surprisingly powerless to stop ... Qc6 , winning a pawn, and Black has excellent winning chances.} 33. Kg2 {Qc6 b4} Qxc3 34. Qxc3 Rxc3 {bxa5 Rxd3 a6 Nc8 a7 Nxa7 Rxa7 is the computer line. Hard to say if it's winning or not, but Black definitely has winning chances.}) 31. Kg2 Nxb3 32. Qxb3 a5 33. Kg1 axb4 34. Qxb4 Qxb4 35. Rxb4 Rxc3 *

