1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. d4 Nxe4 4. Bd3 d5 5. Nxe5 Nd7 6. Nxd7 Bxd7 7. O-O Bd6 8. Qh5 {This was topical Petroff theory at the time. Nowadays, it is well known to be harmless for Black.} Nf6 (8... Qf6 $1 {This is the cleanest equalizer, as Yu Yangyi played against me once.} 9. Nc3 Qxd4 $1 10. Be3 Qe5 11. Qxe5+ Bxe5 12. Nxd5 Nf6 13. Rae1 Nxd5 14. Bd4 O-O-O 15. Bxe5 Nb4 $1 16. Bxg7 Nxd3 17. cxd3 Rhg8 18. Be5 Bc6 $1 {Black takes back his lost pawn on d3 , and White's advantage is symbolic at best.}) 9. Re1+ Kf8 10. Qe2 Ng4 11. h3 Qh4 12. Qf3 {Now remember, the Shankland Method says to always ask 'What happens if I do it anyway?' It does not say 'Do it regardless of the answer to that question!} Bh2+ (12... Nxf2 $2 {Tempting as this may be, it loses immediately.} 13. Qxf2 $2 Bg3 {would justify Black's play, but White has} 14. Rf1 {instead.}) (12... Nf6 $1 {Retreating was prudent. Black is a little worse, but the game goes on.}) 13. Kf1 Nxf2 {This starts a forces sequence that works out very nicely for White.} 14. Qxf2 Bg3 15. Qd2 Qf6+ 16. Kg1 Bxe1 (16... Qxd4+ {is simply met by} 17. Qe3) 17. Qxe1 Qxd4+ 18. Kh2 Re8 19. Qg3 Qe5 20. Qxe5 Rxe5 {Now, White should be more or less winning no matter what he does, but Magnus used the Shankland Method very nicely to find the cleanest way by far.} 21. Bf4 {White goes after the c7 -pawn. It looks like a bad idea because he will be pinned along the first rank. But... what happens if I do it anyway?} Re1 22. Bxc7 {It turns out White can regroup his bishop to d2 just in time to kick Black's rook off the first rank.} a6 23. Ba5 Rd1 24. a4 {No ... Bb5 for you!} Ke7 25. Bd2 {White is ready for Be2 , and that's all she wrote.} Rc8 26. c3 {Patience. Be2 was not going anywhere.} (26. Be2 $2 Rxd2 $1 27. Nxd2 Rxc2 28. Rd1 Rxb2 {Black has a lot of pawns, and the tables are turning. ... Ba4 is on the way.}) 26... d4 27. c4 g6 28. Be2 {It is time. I'm a little surprised Wang kept playing here. For our purposes, this is over.} Bxa4 29. Bb4+ Ke6 30. Bxd1 Bxd1 31. Nd2 Be2 32. b3 f5 33. Kg3 Rd8 34. Kf2 d3 35. Bc3 Kf7 36. Nf3 f4 37. Ng5+ Kg8 38. Ne6 *

