[PlyCount "6"]

17... Bd7 {At this point, Kramnik clearly should be trying to use his lead in development. The Shankland Method would come in handy.} 18. Ng5 {This was a more challenging try for Black to meet - the knight looks very menacing.} (18. f5 $6 {This was the right attitude to take, but not the best execution. Obviously,} exf5 19. Qb3 {is not an option. After gxf5 Qd2 , how should Black continue his development? That's our next exercise!}) 18... Rc8 {I imagine this would be the move White would be concerned with. But, now it's time for the Shankland Method.} (18... Qc7 19. Nb5) (18... Qe8 19. Nb5 Rc8 20. Ne4) 19. Nxf7 {What happens if I do it anyway?} Kxf7 20. f5 {Black gets crushed.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 3"]
[Black "Kramnik-Carlsen Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r2q2k1/pp1bbp1p/2n1p3/5p2/2B5/P1N2NP1/1PQ2P1P/3R2K1 w - - 0 19"]
[PlyCount "6"]

19. Qd2 {How should Black continue his development?} Qb6 {This looks like it hangs the bishop... but what happens if I do it anyway? A little calculation needs to be done, but Black is definitely fine.} 20. Na4 {This move looks very scary. Black's queen is hit, and should she retreat to her only safe square on c7 , White will safely grab the loose bishop. Luckily, Black has a key resource.} (20. Qxd7 $2 Rd8 {White loses his queen. This much is easy to see.}) (20. Qh6 {was the game continuation.}) 20... Nd4 (20... Qc7 21. Qxd7 {and ... Rd8 no longer works.}) (20... Qa5 {drops a piece.} 21. Qxa5 Nxa5 22. Rxd7) 21. Nxb6 Nxf3+ {Black gets the queen back, and he is totally fine.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 3"]
[Black "Kramnik-Carlsen Exercise #3"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r3bk2/pp3p1p/1qn1p3/5pQ1/2B5/P1N3P1/1P3P1P/3R2K1 w - - 0 25"]
[PlyCount "6"]

25. Rd6 {Now, Black's position looks very dangerous, and he is one bad decision away from disaster. Magnus himself blundered the game away here, and the best moves are not easy to find! But, Black can hold in 2 different ways, both by using the Shankland Method.} Rd8 {This strikes me as the most human way to hold. Black would absolutely love to activate his last piece and trade off the menacing rook on d6 . I'm sure Magnus saw this move and rejected it because of Rxe6 . But we ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} (25... Qc7 $2 26. Qh6+ $1) (25... Qc5 {This move looks extremely desirable. Black forks the rook and the bishop on c4 , seemingly winning a piece. I'm sure Magnus saw this and rejected it because of Ne4 - Nf6 when it looks like he should be mated. But always ask: What happens if I do it anyway?} 26. Ne4 Qxc4 27. Nf6 {Now, rather than resigning, a surprising move saves the game.} Ke7 $1 {This looks very scary, but White has nothing better than a draw.}) 26. Qh6+ (26. Rxe6 Qd4 $1 {is similar.}) 26... Kg8 27. Rxe6 {It looks like Black is crushed here.} Qd4 {This move is extremely important, and Black saves the game by bringing his queen back to the defense.} (27... fxe6 $2 28. Qxe6+ Kg7 29. Qg8+ Kf6 30. Qf8+ {Black's king is chased into the center, and he will be mated.}) *

