1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 5. Bf4 O-O 6. e3 c5 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. a3 Nc6 9. Qc2 Qa5 10. Rd1 Be7 11. Be2 dxc4 12. Bxc4 Nh5 13. O-O Nxf4 14. exf4 g6 15. g3 Rd8 16. Rxd8+ Qxd8 17. Rd1 Bd7 {At this point, Kramnik clearly should be trying to use his lead in development. Eventually, the bishop pair and White's slightly compromised pawn structure could matter, but for the moment, Black is playing without his a8 -rook, and the queen is in the line of fire.} 18. f5 {This was the right attitude to take, but not the best execution.} (18. Ng5 $1 {This was a more challenging try for Black to meet - the knight looks very menacing.} Qc7 19. Nb5 {or Qe8 Nb5} Rc8 20. Ne4 {are fantastic for White. I imagine Rc8 would be the move White would be concerned with. But, now it's time for the Shankland Method. Nxf7! What happens if I do it anyway? Kxf7} f5 $1 {Black gets crushed.}) 18... gxf5 (18... exf5 19. Qb3 {is out of the question.}) 19. Qd2 {How should Black continue his development?} Qb6 {This looks like it hangs the bishop... but what happens if I do it anyway? A little calculation needs to be done, but Black is definitely fine.} 20. Qh6 (20. Qxd7 $2 Rd8 {White loses his queen. This much is easy to see.}) (20. Na4 {This move looks very scary. Black's queen is hit, and should she retreat to her only safe square on c7 , White will safely grab the loose bishop.} Qc7 21. Qxd7 {and ... Rd8 no longer works, while} Qa5 {drops a piece to Qxa5 Nxa5 Rxd7 . Luckily, Black has a key resource.} 22. Nd4 $1 {Nxb6 Nxf3+ Black gets the queen back, and he is totally fine.}) 20... Be8 21. Ng5 Bxg5 22. Qxg5+ Kf8 23. Qh6+ Kg8 24. Qg5+ Kf8 25. Rd6 {Now, Black's position looks very dangerous, and he is one bad decision away from disaster. Magnus himself blundered the game away here, and the best moves are not easy to find! But, Black can hold in 2 different ways, both by using the Shankland Method.} Qc7 (25... Rd8 $1 {This strikes me as the most human way to hold. Black would absolutely love to activate his last piece and trade off the menacing rook on d6 . I'm sure Magnus saw this move and rejected it because of Rd6xe6 . But we ask what happens if I do it anyway?} 26. Qh6+ ({(} 26. Rxe6 Qd4 $1 {is similar.)}) 26... Kg8 27. Rxe6 {It looks like Black is crushed here.} Qd4 $1 {This move is extremely important, and Black saves the game by bringing his queen back to the defense. If instead fxe6? Qxe6+ Kg7 Qg8+ Kf6} 28. Qf8+ {, Black's king is chased into the center, and he will be mated.}) (25... Qc5 {This move looks extremely desirable. Black forks the rook and the bishop on c4 , seemingly winning a piece. I'm sure Magnus saw this and rejected it because of Ne4 - Nf6 when it looks like he should be mated. But always ask: What happens if I do it anyway?} 26. Ne4 Qxc4 27. Nf6 {Now, rather than resigning, a surprising move saves the game.} Ke7 $1 {This looks very scary, but White has nothing better than a draw.}) 26. Qh6+ Ke7 (26... Kg8 {would not have saved Black either.} 27. Rxe6 $1 fxe6 28. Bxe6+ Bf7 29. Nd5) 27. Qh4+ Kf8 (27... Kxd6 28. Nb5+) 28. Qh6+ Ke7 29. Nb5 {No draw today. Black's queen is kicked away from the defense, and the king will perish.} Qa5 30. b4 Nxb4 31. Rxe6+ fxe6 32. Qxe6+ Kd8 (32... Kf8 33. Qf6+) 33. Qf6+ Kc8 34. Qxf5+ Kd8 35. Qf6+ Kc8 36. axb4 {Magnus had seen enough.} Qxb4 37. Be6+ Bd7 38. Qh8+ *

