11. Nf3 {White makes the not-so-subtle threat of e5 . The move I played here certainly was not terrible, but I could have found a better one using the Shankland Method.} e5 ({Clearly, this is the move Black would really like to play. He gains space in the center and stops White's threat at the same time. I rejected it because, after exd5 cxd5 , White can grab the pawn on e5 thanks to the pin on the e -file. But always ask... What happens if I do it anyway?} 11... dxe4 12. Bxe4 Nxe4 13. Qxe4 O-O {The position is fairly balanced now, and Wang outplayed me across the next phase of the middlegame.}) 12. exd5 O-O {It transpires that Black does not need to take back on d5 right away and can simply save the e5 -pawn instead. Now, obviously, Black will be very happy if he can play ... cxd5 next, so the only concerning move is White taking on c6 .} (12... cxd5 $2 13. Nxe5 {would indeed leave Black in terrible shape.}) 13. dxc6 Bxc6 {Here, we note that ... e4 is coming, and Black will, at the very least, win back his lost pawn on h2 . The active pieces and strong center leave him with a clear advantage.} *

