[Black "Wang-Shankland Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2r5/5ppk/3Qp1b1/p1p4p/Pq6/1P2NP1P/2P3PK/3R4 w - - 0 33"]
[PlyCount "8"]

33. Kg1 {I had been defending a solid but unpleasant position for some time, and this was the first moment where I could have changed the nature of the game.} c4 {Looking back at this course now in 2024, it's really amazing to me how many half points I left on the table by not using the Shankland Method effectively enough. I was a decently strong player and clearly knew the idea (it is even named after me now!) but no matter how strong you get, you will always make mistakes. This is clearly the move Black wants to make, liquidating a weakness. It looks impossible because White wins a pawn but always ask... What happens if I do it anyway?} (33... Rb8 {was my choice in the game.}) 34. Qxb4 axb4 35. bxc4 {This looks horrible, but with a little effort, Black can equalize directly.} Ra8 36. Ra1 Ra5 {White's pawns are stopped in their tracks, his pieces are stuck in defensive positions, and he cannot stop ... f6 followed by ... Be8 . Black is absolutely fine.} *

