[PlyCount "6"]

41. Qd4 {This was the first move that came after time control and a spot where I colossally failed at using the Shankland Method, even with a bunch of added time on the clock. Clearly, the move I would like to play is ... Qc6 , hitting the a4 -pawn. It looks like it fails to Qd7 , defending the pawn and offering a queen exchange. I asked what would happen if I did it anyway and came to the wrong conclusion.} e5 {This should have been preferred, using the Shankland Method in an alternate way. Black mobilizes his central pawn majority. It looks like a bad idea because White can force the queens off, but always ask... What if I do it anyway?} (41... Qc6 $2 42. Qd7 $1 {My opponent used the Shankland Method better than I did. It looks like this move blunders ... Bxc2 , which is what I had been banking on. But what happens if I do it anyway?} Qc5 {This was not a great move, but the position was lost in any case. I was not able to offer any meaningful resistance. After Bxc2? Nxc2 Qxc4+} 43. Qd3 $1 {, in my calculations, I had not realized that this move comes with check and that I cannot take on c2 . White remains a piece up and wins.}) 42. Qd6 Qxd6 43. Rxd6 f5 {White cannot stop ... f4 , and his pawns will start dropping quickly. If I were White here, I would be thinking about how to avoid being worse, although the machine insists on absolute equality.} *

