 using the Shankland Method effectively enough. I was a decently strong player and clearly knew the idea (it is even named after me now!) but no matter how strong you get, you will always make mistakes. This is clearly the move Black wants to make, liquidating a weakness. It looks impossible because White wins a pawn but always ask... What happens if I do it anyway?} 34. Qxb4 axb4 35. bxc4 {This looks horrible, but with a little effort, Black can equalize directly.} Ra8 $1 36. Ra1 Ra5 {White's pawns are stopped in their tracks, his pieces are stuck in defensive positions, and he cannot stop ... f6 followed by ... Be8 . Black is absolutely fine.}) 34. Kf2 Rc8 35. Qd2 Qh4+ 36. Kg1 Qd4 37. Kf2 (37. Qxd4 cxd4 38. Rxd4 Bxc2 {and Black is surely equalizing.}) 37... Qh4+ 38. Ke2 c4 {At least once in this game, I did use the Shankland Method correctly. But one time out of four was not enough to get on the scoreboard.} 39. Qc3 (39. Nxc4 Rxc4 $1 40. bxc4 Qxc4+ 41. Kf2 Qxa4 {Black definitely should not lose here.}) 39... Qg3 40. bxc4 Qc7 41. Qd4 {This was the first move that came after time control and a spot where I colossally failed at using the Shankland Method, even with a bunch of added time on the clock. Clearly, the move I would like to play is ... Qc6 , hitting the a4 -pawn. It looks like it fails to Qd7 , defending the pawn and offering a queen exchange. I asked what would happen if I did it anyway and came to the wrong conclusion.} Qc6 (41... e5 $1 {This should have been preferred, using the Shankland Method in an alternate way. Black mobilizes his central pawn majority. It looks like a bad idea because White can force the queens off, but always ask... What if I do it anyway?} 42. Qd6 Qxd6 43. Rxd6 f5 {White cannot stop ... f4 , and his pawns will start dropping quickly. If I were White here, I would be thinking about how to avoid being worse, although the machine insists on absolute equality.}) 42. Qd7 {My opponent used the Shankland Method better than I did. It looks like this move blunders ... Bxc2 , which is what I had been banking on. But what happens if I do it anyway?} Qc5 {This was not a great move, but the position was lost in any case. I was not able to offer any meaningful resistance.} (42... Bxc2 $2 43. Nxc2 Qxc4+ 44. Qd3+ $1 {In my calculations, I had not realized that this move comes with check and that I cannot take on c2 . White remains a piece up and wins.}) 43. Rb1 Rc7 44. Qb5 Qd4 45. Rd1 Qc3 46. Rd8 Re7 47. Qc5 Qf6 48. Qc8 Kh6 49. Rh8+ Kg5 50. Qc5+ Kh4 51. Kf2 *

