[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 3"]
[Black "Shankland-Nyzhnyk Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/pppn2bp/3p2n1/2PPp1p1/4Pp2/2NN1P2/PP1BB1PP/2RQ1RK1 b - - 0 14"]
[PlyCount "6"]

14... Nf6 {Now, ideally, we would like to slam cxd6 and Nb5 to invade the queenside, but this allows a check on b6 . How can we use the Shankland Method?} 15. Nb5 {It looks like there is a problem with this move order- Black can play ... a6 , and the knight is unable to reach c7 . But... Shankland Method!} (15. cxd6 {This move order is less accurate.} cxd6 16. Nb5 Rf7 $1 {White is missing Ba5 . This is better than a6} 17. Nc7) 15... a6 (15... Rf7 {Again, Black took me at my word. How can we use the Shankland Method again?}) 16. cxd6 {What happens if I do it anyway?} axb5 17. dxc7 {White's monster pawns more than make up for the missing piece.} *

