[FEN "r1bq1rk1/ppp3bp/3p1nn1/1NPPp1p1/4Pp2/3N1P2/PP1BB1PP/2RQ1RK1 b - - 0 15"]
[PlyCount "8"]

15... Rf7 {Again, Black took me at my word. How can we use the Shankland Method again?} 16. Ba5 {This pressures the c7 -pawn, all but forcing ... b6 . This looks less than ideal, as after a future cxb6  axb6 , Black is able to preserve the c -pawn to keep the c -file half closed, and a2 might even hang. But, what happens if I do it anyway?} (16. cxd6 cxd6 17. Bb4 {is less effective. White doesn't get the c6 -square for the rook, and Black has} Qb6+ {.}) 16... b6 17. cxd6 {The Shankland Method strikes again! White would love to take this way to prevent Black from recapturing with the a-pawn. It looks like it hangs a piece, but what happens if I do it anyway?} (17. cxb6 $2 axb6 $1 {Black preserves his structure. White is unable to effectively target the d6 -pawn, and ... g4 comes next, with a lot of counterplay.}) 17... bxa5 (17... cxd6 {Once more, my opponent took me at my word.} 18. Bb4 $1 {Now, since Black had to take with the c -pawn on d6 , the d6 -pawn is weak, requires protection, and his pieces are beaten backwards. Note that by provoking Black's pawn to b6 , I am not only avoiding an annoying check from the queen but also, c6 has been weakened.}) 18. dxc7 Qf8 19. Nc5 {With d6 and Bc4 on the way, the pawns are crashing through.} *

