26... Qg5 {Now, time to use the Shankland method once more!} 27. Nc7 {This is a very important move. Not only does it help White crash through on the queenside, but it also helps him by trading off the e8 -knight, which would have liked to go to f6 to try to pry open the g -file. It looks impossible because now ... Bxc6 can work. But what happens if I do it anyway?} Bxc6 (27... Bxg4 28. fxg4 f3 29. gxf3 ({(} 29. Bxf3 Rxf3 30. gxf3 Qf4 {is messy.)}) 29... Qf4 30. Be1 $1 {and White has everything under control.}) (27... Nf6 {allows} 28. Ne6 {.}) 28. Ne6 ({An important in-between move.} 28. dxc6 $2 Nxc7 {is bad news for White.}) 28... Bxa4 29. Qxa4 {Despite the exchange less, White is totally winning. His pieces might be nominally smaller than Black's, but they are so much better. Consider the knight on e6 , the bishop on b4 , and rook on c1 . Now compare them to the rooks on f7 and a8 and the knight on e8 . Which 3 pieces are more effective? Black's queenside will soon collapse.} *

