60... Qa1 {Now, White is winning in more ways than one, but I really like the one I chose.} 61. Kb5 {The only problem White is facing, the only reason Black is not resigning yet, is that White's king is out in the open. If we could drop the king on any square, where would he be safest? a8 comes to mind. Can we run that way? Well, Kb5 allows a check on b1 , and the rook will be lost. But... What happens if I do it anyway?} (61. Qd8+ Kg7 62. Qd7+ Kg8 63. d6 {The computer finds this one, controlling the a4 -square. Of course, it wins, but I find my choice to be more human, and more consistent with this course.}) 61... Qb1+ 62. Ka6 Qxc2 63. Qxe5+ {The point. White takes a rook, too.} Kg8 64. Qxg3 Qxe2+ 65. Kxa7 {White's d -pawn will be decisive.} *

