{Moving on a bit deeper into my career, I got to demonstrate the Shankland Method in action in one of my favorite games I've ever played.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. f3 f4 12. Bd2 g5 13. Rc1 Ng6 {The battle lines have been drawn. White clearly wants to break through on the queenside, while Black tries to give mate on the kingside. The break I want to make is c5 .} 14. c5 {Shankland Method! What happens if I do it anyway? In the game, Black took me at my word and did not take the pawn.} (14. b4 $6 {This move guarantees c5 will come next, but it is slow and will not help White win the game on the queenside once c5 comes. As such, it is wasted time.} Nf6 15. c5 h5 {and ... g4 is coming quickly. I would be worried with White here.}) 14... Nf6 {Now, ideally, we would like to slam cxd6 and Nb5 to invade the queenside, but this allows a check on b6 . How can we use the Shankland Method?} (14... dxc5 {Obviously, we should know what to do if Black takes the pawn before we push c4 - c5 . With energetic play, White retains a clear edge.} 15. b4 $1 {This is the best way to tear open the queenside. It looks like it loses a pawn, but what happens if I do it anyway?} cxb4 16. Nb5 $1 {Despite the two pawns up, Black is in bad shape. White will easily take back the lost material, and the queenside is being blasted open.}) (14... Nxc5 {This was possible, but after} 15. Nxc5 dxc5 {, instead of} 16. b4 cxb4 17. Nb5 Rf7 {and Black can protect the 7th rank, Na4! b6 b4! cxb4} 18. Bxb4 {where I prefer White's position despite the pawn less. He has successfully opened the queenside, and his pieces are very active.}) 15. Nb5 {It looks like there is a problem with this move order- Black can play ... a6 , and the knight is unable to reach c7 . But... Shankland Method!} (15. cxd6 {This move order is less accurate.} cxd6 16. Nb5 Rf7 $1 {White is missing Ba5 . This is better than a6} 17. Nc7) 15... Rf7 {Again, Black took me at my word. How can we use the Shankland Method again?} (15... a6 16. cxd6 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway?} axb5 17. dxc7 {White's monster pawns more than make up for the missing piece.}) 16. Ba5 {This pressures the c7 -pawn, all but forcing b7 - b6 . This looks less than ideal, as after cxb6  axb6 , Black is able to preserve the c-pawn to keep the c-file half closed, and a2 might even hang. But, what happens if I do it anyway?} (16. cxd6 cxd6 17. Bb4 {is less effective. White doesn't get the c6 -square for the rook, and Black has} Qb6+ {.}) 16... b6 17. cxd6 {The Shankland Method strikes again! White would love to take this way to prevent Black from recapturing with the a-pawn. It looks like it hangs a piece, but what happens if I do it anyway?} (17. cxb6 $2 axb6 $1 {Black preserves his structure. White is unable to effectively target the d6 -pawn, and ... g4 comes next, with a lot of counterplay.}) 17... cxd6 {Once more, my opponent took me at my word.} (17... bxa5 $2 18. dxc7 Qf8 19. Nc5 {With d6 and Bc4 on the way, the pawns are crashing through.}) 18. Bb4 {Now, since Black had to take with the c -pawn on d6 , the d6 -pawn is weak, requires protection, and his pieces are beaten backwards. Note that by provoking Black's pawn to b6 , I am not only avoiding an annoying check from the queen but also, c6 has been weakened.} Bf8 19. Rc6 Ne8 20. a4 h5 21. Nf2 Nh4 22. h3 {It will not be easy for Black to get ... g4 in!} Qf6 23. Qb3 Bd7 {Now what?} 24. Rfc1 {We want to develop the f1 rook, but obviously, we have to deal with the threat of ... Bxc6 . But... What happens if I do it anyway? This turns out to be very strong, and Black cannot take on c6 .} g4 {Black has to try. He has no other plan. He is still okay here but should be very precise.} (24... Bxc6 $2 25. dxc6 {With Bc4 and Nc3 - Nd5 on the way, White is totally winning despite the exchange less.}) 25. hxg4 (25. fxg4 {is not the right call. The c6 -rook won't be immune to capture forever!} f3 26. gxf3 Bxc6 27. dxc6 Nxf3+ {is not working for White.}) 25... hxg4 26. Nxg4 Qg5 ({Now, time to use the Shankland method once more!} 26... Qg6 {would have been better, avoiding the issue in the game.}) 27. Nc7 {This is a very important move. Not only does it help White crash through on the queenside, but it also helps him by trading off the e8 -knight, which would have liked to go to f6 to try to pry open the g-file. It looks impossible because now Bxc6 can work. But what happens if I do it anyway?} Bxg4 (27... Bxc6 28. dxc6 $2 Nxc7 {is bad news for White. Instead, Ne6! is an important in-between move. Bxa4 Qxa4+- Despite the exchange less, White is totally winning. His pieces might be nominally smaller than Black's, but they are so much better. Consider the knight on e6 , the bishop on b4 , and rook on c1 . Now compare them to the rooks on f7 and a8 and the knight on e8 . Which 3 pieces are more effective? Black's queenside will soon collapse.}) (27... Nf6 {allows} 28. Ne6 {.}) 28. fxg4 f3 29. gxf3 (29. Bxf3 Rxf3 30. gxf3 Qf4 {is messy.}) 29... Qf4 30. Be1 Nxc7 (30... Nxf3+ 31. Qxf3 {It's important that my bishop is still on the board.}) 31. Rxc7 Rh7 32. R1c2 {Protecting h2 in advance, anticipating a move like ... Nxf3 +.} (32. Rxh7 $2 Qxc1) 32... Be7 (32... Nxf3+ 33. Bxf3) 33. Rxe7 Rxe7 34. Bxh4 Rh7 35. Bf2 Rf8 36. Qe3 Qh2+ 37. Kf1 Qh1+ 38. Bg1 Rh2 39. Ke1 {While this move does win, there was a much easier solution.} (39. Rc1 $1 {White prepares for Ke1 - d2 next. The point is that now Rg2 will fail routinely.} Rg2 40. Qg5+ $1 Kh8 41. Qh5+) 39... Rg2 40. Kd2 Qxg1 {With time control reached, at this point, I realized that I missed a detail in my calculations. Luckily, White still wins.} 41. Qg5+ Kh8 42. Rc7 Qd4+ 43. Kc1 Rg1+ (43... Qg1+ 44. Bd1) 44. Kc2 Qxa4+ 45. Kc3 Qd4+ (45... Qa5+ 46. Kb3) 46. Kb3 {When I played 39.Ke1 , I had seen up to here and thought that Black had no checks, and the game was over. But... he does have one. Luckily, once time control was reached, I realized it was not a huge concern.} Rxf3+ 47. Bxf3 Qd3+ 48. Rc3 {It is not too hard for White to escape the checks.} Qb5+ 49. Kc2 Qa4+ 50. b3 Qa2+ 51. Kd3 Qb1+ 52. Rc2 Qxb3+ 53. Kd2 Qb4+ 54. Ke2 Qe1+ 55. Kd3 Qb1 (55... Qf1+ 56. Be2 {No more checks.} Rg3+ 57. Kd2) 56. Qh6+ Kg8 57. Qg6+ Kh8 58. Qxd6 Qf1+ 59. Be2 Rg3+ 60. Kc4 Qa1 {Now, White is winning in more ways than one, but I really like the one I chose.} 61. Kb5 {The only problem White is facing, the only reason Black is not resigning yet, is that White's king is out in the open. If we could drop the king on any square, where would he be safest? a8 comes to mind. Can we run that way? Well, Kb5 allows a check on b1 , and the rook will be lost. But... What happens if I do it anyway?} (61. Qd8+ Kg7 62. Qd7+ Kg8 63. d6 {The computer finds this one, controlling the a4 -square. Of course, it wins, but I find my choice to be more human, and more consistent with this course.}) 61... Qb1+ 62. Ka6 Qxc2 63. Qxe5+ {The point. White takes a rook, too.} Kg8 64. Qxg3 Qxe2+ 65. Kxa7 {White's d -pawn will be decisive.} Qxe4 66. Qb8+ Kf7 67. Qc7+ Kg6 68. d6 Qa4+ 69. Kb7 {The final important detail. White keeps the b6 -pawn alive to shield his king on the b -file. The d -pawn goes through, and Black resigned here.} Qe4+ 70. Qc6 *

