12. Nd2 {At this point, the move I played was not bad, but I could have maintained the tension a bit better by using the Shankland Method.} Rc8 {Clearly, this is the move Black wants to play, bringing out another piece and fighting for the c4 -square. I rejected it because of Nd5 when I was worried for the safety of my knight on a5 . But always ask... What happens if I do it anyway?} (12... d5 $6 13. exd5 Nxd5 14. Nxd5 Qxd5 15. Qxd5 Bxd5 {Black is definitely under a little pressure in this endgame. It's not so bad, but I did give my opponent one chance to cash in.}) 13. Nd5 Nxd5 14. exd5 Bd7 15. a4 {It looks like b4 is coming next, and Black will lose a piece. But Black is not out of resources.} ({The immediate} 15. b4 {run into} Bb5 {.}) 15... f5 16. b4 e4 {The counterplay is coming.} 17. Qa3 f4 18. Bd4 {Now things look bad... but the attack keeps coming.} (18. Bxf4 $1 {White should prefer this one, going into damage control mode. After} Rxf4 19. bxa5 Qxa5 20. Nc4 Qc7 {I prefer Black, but the game goes on.}) 18... f3 19. gxf3 exf3 20. Nxf3 (20. Bd3 Bf6 {White will soon be mated once the queen reaches g5 .}) 20... Nc4 {By distracting the d2 -knight, Black gained the c4 -square, and he is totally winning.} *

