{A final accurate move seals Black's fate. The a7 -rook and the e7 -knight are hanging, and Rxc5 is met by Nxc5 with a back-rank mate threat.} 25... Bb2 {Now, White could have won the game by using the Shankland Method.} 26. g4 {White absolutely would love to play this move, kicking Black's bishop away from the defense of the d7 -square. This might look dumb because it ignores the threat to the rook on c1 but always ask... What happens if I do it anyway?} (26. Na4 $2 {This lets a chance slip, and I equalized immediately.} Bxc1 27. Rxc1 Ne5 $1 {I had access to this move specifically because White brought his knight to a4 rather than d7 . Bd7 would allow} 28. Nb6 {.}) 26... Bxc1 27. Rxc1 Bg6 28. Nd7 {Now, since White got to put the knight on d7 rather than a4 as he did in the game, Black's knight on c6 does not have access to the e5 -square. He is totally lost.} Ne7 29. Bc5 Ra7 30. Rd1 *

