ng Black's bishop away from the defense of the d7 -square. This might look dumb because it ignores the threat to the rook on c1 but always ask... What happens if I do it anyway?} Bxc1 27. Rxc1 Bg6 28. Nd7 {Now, since White got to put the knight on d7 rather than a4 as he did in the game, Black's knight on c6 does not have access to the e5 -square. He is totally lost.} Ne7 29. Bc5 $1 Ra7 30. Rd1 $1 {A final accurate move seals Black's fate. The a7 -rook and the e7 -knight are hanging, and} Rxc5 {is met by} 31. Nxc5 {with a back-rank mate threat.}) 26... Bxc1 27. Rxc1 Ne5 {I had access to this move specifically because White brought his knight to a4 rather than d7 .} (27... Bd7 {would allow} 28. Nb6 {.}) 28. Bxa8 Rxa8 29. Rc5 Re8 30. Bd4 Bd7 {A final accurate move saves the game for Black.} (30... f6 $2 31. Bxe5 {Black has a tough and probably unsuccessful defense ahead.}) 31. Rxe5 ({But not} 31. Nb6 $2 Nd3) *

