1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c6 4. e3 Nf6 5. Nf3 Nbd7 6. Bd3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Bd3 a6 9. a4 ({More challenging is} 9. e4 c5 10. e5 cxd4 11. Nxb5 axb5 12. exf6 gxf6 {.}) 9... b4 10. Ne4 Bb7 11. Nxf6+ Nxf6 ({I toyed with the idea of} 11... gxf6 12. e4 e5) 12. Qe2 c5 13. dxc5 Bxc5 14. e4 O-O 15. O-O Qc7 16. Bg5 a5 17. Rac1 Rac8 18. Be3 Qd6 ({Right after I moved, it occurred to me that I underestimated an important idea.} 18... Qe7 $2 19. e5 {and Bxh7 is coming.} Bxf3 20. exf6 Bxe2 21. fxe7) (18... Qb6 $1 {Cold blooded and not fearing the pin. Black may even be more comfortable. I saw it, but somehow could not bring myself to play it.} 19. Rxc5 Rxc5 20. e5 Bxf3 21. Qxf3 Nd5 {.}) 19. Bxc5 Rxc5 {How should White proceed?} 20. e5 {This looks ill-advised because Black can take on f3 . But, she should ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} Bxf3 21. exd6 Bxe2 22. Rxc5 {This endgame is extremely dangerous for Black. White's rooks will be great, a5 is loose, and the d-pawn is coming.} Bxd3 23. Rd1 (23. Rfc1 $1 {This one looked stronger to me during the game. Computer claims White is only slightly better, but it looks super dangerous to me}) 23... Be4 24. Rxa5 (24. d7 $2 Bd5 {Oops!}) 24... Rd8 {I spent a long time on this move. I calculated a super long line, but I missed a key idea. Still, Black should be more or less okay} 25. Rb5 Bc2 (25... Bc6 26. Rxb4 Nd5 27. Rc4 Rxd6 {This looked very problematic to me but the computer thinks Black is just about holding}) 26. Ra1 Nd5 {White is winning here, but she should be extremely accurate.} 27. Rc5 (27. a5 $1 {This is definitely the move White wants to make. It looks like it will not work once Black plays ... Bd3 because the white rook will be marooned. But always ask... what happens if I do it anyway?} Bd3 28. Rb7 Ba6 29. Ra7 Bb5 30. a6 Kf8 {This position is not so easy for White because the a7 -rook cannot get out of the way without some effort. How should she proceed?} 31. Rb7 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway?} Bc6 32. Rc1 $1 Bxb7 33. axb7 {Obviously, White is looking for Rc8 to come next, but Black can prevent it.} Nb6 34. Rc8 $1 {I missed this move from afar when I was calculating the consequences of 25...Bc2 .} Rxc8 35. bxc8=Q+ Nxc8 36. d7 {or Nxc8 b8=Q Ke8 Qxb4} Nd6 {Black's fortress chances are not zero, but White should probably win with enough effort and accuracy.}) {I had calculated something like Rc6 Nd7 Rc8 Ke8 Rc7 Nb8 Re7+ Kf8 Rc7} 27... Bd3 28. Rc6 (28. a5 {was still best.}) 28... Kf8 {Now Black is firmly in command, and I won in short order.} 29. Rd1 (29. a5 Bb5 30. Rc5 Ba6 31. Rc6 Bb7 32. Rc5 Rxd6) 29... Be4 30. Rc4 (30. Rd4 Nc3 $1 {wins nicely.}) 30... Bg6 31. a5 Ke8 (31... Rxd6 $2 32. Rxb4 {And White is very much alive. Black did not need to take the d -pawn so soon, it is not going anywhere.}) 32. a6 Ra8 33. d7+ (33. Ra1 {Black can even play} Bd3 {but Kd7} 34. a7 {Kxd6 is easy enough.}) 33... Kxd7 34. Rxb4 Rxa6 35. Rb8 Rc6 36. h3 Rc2 37. Kf1 Ke7 38. Rd4 Kf6 39. Ke1 Ke5 40. Rd2 Rc1+ 41. Rd1 Rc4 42. b3 Rc3 43. b4 Rb3 44. b5 h5 45. Rb7 Nf4 46. g3 Nd3+ 47. Ke2 Nc5 48. Rb8 Bd3+ 49. Ke1 Bxb5 50. Ra1 Ke4 51. Ra7 Nd3+ 52. Kd2 Ne5 53. Rab7 Rd3+ 54. Kc1 Bc4 55. f4 Nc6 56. Rf8 e5 57. fxe5 Nxe5 *

