{One of my most memorable tournament victories was the 2016 Edmonton International. My good friend Surya Ganguly came to visit me in California for a training camp the week before. We trained well and were both on fire. I came into this game with 6.5/7... and in second place! I needed to win the game to take over the tournament lead, and I managed to use the Shankland Method twice to do so successfully.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 c5 7. Rb1 Be7 8. Bc4 O-O 9. Ne2 {At the time, this move was a novelty. The Bc4 / Ne2 setup had been well-known against the Grunfeld for a very long time, but it had not yet been seen in the Semi-Tarrasch.} Nc6 10. O-O b6 11. Be3 Bb7 {So far, Surya has played just fine for Black and has developed his pieces the same way you would in a Grunfeld. But now, we see a key difference with the bishop being on e7 rather than g7 .} 12. Nf4 {This move is possible without hanging the d4 -pawn! White has serious ideas to either sacrifice on e6 or push d4 - d5 . Black is surely fine with best play, but it's not an easy position to face for an unprepared human, even a very strong one like Surya.} cxd4 13. cxd4 Rc8 14. Nxe6 fxe6 15. Bxe6+ Kh8 16. Bxc8 Qxc8 {Now, the battle lines are drawn. White has a rook and two very menacing central pawns for two pieces. White's goal should be to consolidate the center and get the rooks to open files.} *

