[Black "Shankland-Ganguly Exercise #1"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2q2r1k/pb2b1pp/1pn5/8/3PP3/4B3/P4PPP/1R1Q1RK1 w - - 0 17"]
[PlyCount "10"]

17. Qa4 {Now, Black is in a tough spot. Rfc1 could be coming next, d5 is in the air, and the queen cannot go to e6 . I don't believe Black can equalize here, but Surya definitely found the best move to keep the disadvantage to a minimum.} b5 {Black uses the Shankland Method. This is clearly the move he would like to play, expelling my active queen. It looks like it hangs a pawn, but always ask, what happens if I do it anyway? It turns out that the tactics favor Black.} (17... Qe6 $2 {It might be tempting to think that this is the Shankland Method in action, but it is not. After} 18. d5 Qg6 {, while it is true that White cannot safely take on c6 , the queen going to g6 did not help much, and White's pawn center is mobilizing.} 19. f4 $1 {Black looks very lost to me.}) ({Black couldn't prepare this move with} 17... a6 {due to} 18. Rxb6 {.}) 18. Rxb5 {At this point, I realized that Black would successfully grab an exchange back here, but I asked, what if I do it anyway? I ultimately concluded that it was worth it and that I preferred the 3 pawns vs a piece position.} (18. Qb3 {The computer thinks this is a move of equal merit, but I disagree. After} Na5 19. Qd3 Ba6 {Black's position appeals much more to me here than it did in the game.}) 18... Ba6 19. Rc1 {Sadly, this move does not work.} (19. Rfb1 Bxb5 20. Qxb5 {White is surely better here, but it's far from over. There were some mutual mistakes in the coming moves, but the game took its decisive turn once I was able to use the Shankland Method.}) 19... Bxb5 20. Qxb5 Nxd4 (20... Rf6 21. d5) 21. Qd3 (21. Qa4 $2 Ne2+) (21. Rxc8 Nxb5) (21. Qb2 Nc6) 21... Nc6 {Black is ready for ... Rf6 as needed; he saves his knight and remains a piece up.} *

