[White "Level 3"]
[Black "Shankland-Ganguly Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "5r2/6pk/p1R4p/4P1b1/3P4/P2nB1KP/5PP1/8 b - - 0 29"]
[PlyCount "6"]

29... Bf4+ {This looks so tempting, but now, the Shankland Method strikes.} 30. Kf3 {White clearly wants to play this move to get his king to the center. It looks outrageous- Black has any number of discoveries available, he can grab the e5 -pawn, he can play ... Ne1 and take on g2 ... But we ask, what happens if I do it anyway? It turns out nothing can hurt White here, and he successfully gets the king to e4 to push the central pawns through.} Ne1+ (30... Bxe5+ 31. Ke4 $1 {Black loses a piece, and White wins with the extra pawn(s).} Bxd4 32. Kxd3 Bxe3 33. Kxe3 Ra8 34. a4) (30... Bxe3+ 31. Kxe3 Nxf2 32. Rc1) (30... Bg5+ 31. Ke2 Nf4+ 32. Bxf4 Rxf4 33. Rc4) 31. Ke4 Nxg2 32. d5 {White lost the g2 -pawn, but it is of no significance. The king got to the center, and the center pawns roll.} *

