{One of my most memorable tournament victories was the 2016 Edmonton International. My good friend Surya Ganguly came to visit me in California for a training camp the week before. We trained well and were both on fire. I came into this game with 6.5/7... and in second place! I needed to win the game to take over the tournament lead, and I managed to use the Shankland Method twice to do so successfully.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 c5 7. Rb1 Be7 8. Bc4 O-O 9. Ne2 {At the time, this move was a novelty. The Bc4 / Ne2 setup had been well-known against the Grunfeld for a very long time, but it had not yet been seen in the Semi-Tarrasch.} Nc6 10. O-O b6 11. Be3 Bb7 {So far, Surya has played just fine for Black and has developed his pieces the same way you would in a Grunfeld. But now, we see a key difference with the bishop being on e7 rather than g7 .} 12. Nf4 {This move is possible without hanging the d4 -pawn! White has serious ideas to either sacrifice on e6 or push d4 - d5 . Black is surely fine with best play, but it's not an easy position to face for an unprepared human, even a very strong one like Surya.} cxd4 13. cxd4 Rc8 14. Nxe6 fxe6 15. Bxe6+ Kh8 16. Bxc8 Qxc8 {Now, the battle lines are drawn. White has a rook and two very menacing central pawns for two pieces. White's goal should be to consolidate the center and get the rooks to open files.} 17. Qa4 {Now, Black is in a tough spot. Rfc1 could be coming next, d4 - d5 is in the air, and the queen cannot go to e6 . I don't believe Black can equalize here, but Surya definitely found the best move to keep the disadvantage to a minimum.} b5 {Black uses the Shankland Method. This is clearly the move he would like to play, expelling my active queen. It looks like it hangs a pawn, but always ask, what happens if I do it anyway? It turns out that the tactics favor Black.} (17... Qe6 $2 {It might be tempting to think that this is the Shankland Method in action, but it is not. After} 18. d5 Qg6 {, while it is true that White cannot safely take on c6 , the queen going to g6 did not help much, and White's pawn center is mobilizing.} 19. f4 $1 {Black looks very lost to me.}) ({Black couldn't prepare this move with} 17... a6 {due to} 18. Rxb6 {.}) 18. Rxb5 {At this point, I realized that Black would successfully grab an exchange back here, but I asked, what if I do it anyway? I ultimately concluded that it was worth it and that I preferred the 3 pawns vs a piece position.} (18. Qb3 {The computer thinks this is a move of equal merit, but I disagree. After} Na5 19. Qd3 Ba6 {Black's position appeals much more to me here than it did in the game.}) 18... Ba6 19. Rfb1 ({Sadly,} 19. Rc1 $2 {does not work.} Bxb5 $1 20. Qxb5 {and here,} Rf6 {runs into} 21. d5 {but} Nd4 $1 22. Qd3 Nc6 {and Black is ready for ... Rf6 as needed; he saves his knight and remains a piece up. Same after Qb2 Nc6 , whereas Qa4? would run into Ne2+ and Rxc8 Nxb5 leaves Black again a piece up.}) 19... Bxb5 20. Qxb5 {White is surely better here, but it's far from over. There were some mutual mistakes in the coming moves, but the game took its decisive turn once I was able to use the Shankland Method.} h6 21. h3 a6 22. Qb7 Qxb7 23. Rxb7 Bg5 24. Rc7 (24. Rb6 {I'm not entirely sure why I did not play this one.} Bxe3 25. fxe3 Na5) 24... Nb4 25. a3 Nd3 26. e5 Rb8 27. Kh2 Kh7 28. Kg3 Rf8 29. Rc6 {Black's position is still difficult in human practice, but the machine insists on dead equality and that more than one move holds on. Luckily for me, Surya erred.} (29. e6 Nxf2 30. Bxf2 Bf4+) 29... Bf4+ {This looks so tempting, but now, the Shankland Method strikes.} 30. Kf3 {White clearly wants to play this move to get his king to the center. It looks outrageous- Black has any number of discoveries available, he can grab the e5 -pawn, he can play Ne1 and take on g2 . But we ask, what happens if I do it anyway? It turns out nothing can hurt White here, and he successfully gets the king to e4 to push the central pawns through.} Ne1+ (30... Bxe5+ 31. Ke4 $1 {Black loses a piece, and White wins with the extra pawn(s).} Bxd4 32. Kxd3 Bxe3 33. Kxe3 Ra8 34. a4) (30... Bxe3+ 31. Kxe3 Nxf2 32. Rc1) (30... Bg5+ 31. Ke2 Nf4+ 32. Bxf4 Rxf4 33. Rc4) 31. Ke4 Nxg2 {White lost the g2 -pawn, but it is of no significance. The king got to the center, and the center pawns roll.} 32. d5 Nxe3 33. fxe3 Bg3 34. d6 Re8 {What should we do here?} 35. d7 {What happens if I do it anyway?} (35. Rc5 $2 Kg6 {The pawns are stopped in their tracks, and Black is right back in the game.}) 35... Rd8 (35... Rxe5+ 36. Kd4 {White makes a queen.}) 36. e6 Bh4 37. Kd5 Kg8 (37... Be7 38. Rc8) 38. Rxa6 (38. Kd6 Kf8) 38... Kf8 39. Ra4 Bf6 40. Rc4 {The last move before time control seals Black's fate. The rook will come to c8 , jailing the defending rook on d8 , and then the a -pawn decides the game.} Ke7 41. Rc8 Bg5 42. a4 Bxe3 43. a5 h5 44. Kc6 {Surya resigned. He beat Shirov in the final round while I only managed to make a draw, and we shared first with 8.0/9. Not a bad result after our training camp!} ({Black's only try for counterplay fails: g5} 44. Rxd8 Kxd8 45. e7+ Kxe7 {Kc7}) *

