60... Rh5 {White has not played too well in the preceding moves, and now he has to be very accurate to win the game. How would you continue?} 61. e4 {This is the move White really wants to play to mobilize his pawns, as we saw at an earlier moment. Here, it looks ill-advised because after Black takes on e4 , White will not be ready for f4 - f5 . But we have to ask: What if I do it anyway?} (61. Nh2 $2 {Now Black holds.} Kc5 {Black is out of danger. The king gets back in time.}) (61. Ng3 Rh4 62. e4 {is also a fine move order.}) 61... fxe4 62. Ng3 Rh4 63. f5 {This looks wrong. Black will take on f5 and White can push neither g6 nor h7 , and taking on f5 would allow a check on g4 . But again... what if I do it anyway?} gxf5 64. Nxf5 Rg4+ 65. Ng3 {And it transpires that Black cannot safely take on g7 . White is threatening h7 , making a queen directly.} Rh4 (65... Rxg5 66. h7 {The pawn goes through.}) 66. Nxe4 ({White clips an important pawn, and the knight is immune from capture. There will still be some work to do once queen against rook is reached, but the position is definitely winning. E.g.: Rxe4} 66. h7 Rg4 67. Kf2 Rxg5 68. h8=Q Re5) *

