1. d4 e6 2. c4 Bb4+ 3. Bd2 a5 4. g3 Nc6 5. Bg2 d6 6. e3 e5 7. d5 (7. Ne2 {is more principled.}) 7... Bxd2+ 8. Qxd2 Nb8 9. Nc3 Na6 10. Nge2 Nf6 11. h3 Nc5 12. g4 Ng8 13. Ng3 Qh4 14. O-O-O Bd7 15. Nce4 Nxe4 16. Bxe4 g6 17. Bc2 Ne7 18. Rdg1 (18. c5 {is the move White wants to play, so what happens if I do it anyway?} dxc5 19. d6 {is crushing and after} O-O {cxd6 cxd6} 20. Ne4 {, Black has some problems to solve.}) 18... f5 19. f4 e4 20. Qc3 Rf8 21. Ne2 Qf6 22. Nd4 Kf7 23. g5 Qg7 24. h4 a4 25. h5 c5 26. dxc6 bxc6 {Black's position looks a bit suspect, but it is still fairly solid. How should White continue?} 27. Rd1 (27. c5 $1 {is definitely the move White wants to play. He prevents ... c5 , which fixes the c6 -pawn on a light square and solidifies the knight on d4 . I'm sure Ivanisevic saw this move and rejected it due to the ... Nd5 response. But we should always ask: What happens if I do it anyway?} Nd5 28. Qc4 $1 {Black cannot take on e3 yet because the knight is pinned.} dxc5 29. Qxc5 Nxe3 {Now, for the moment, it might look like things have gone wrong. But when one looks a bit closer, White has a crushing initiative.} 30. Qd6 $1 {Simple and totally crushing. Black can hardly defend the bishop in a reasonable way.} Rad8 31. Bxa4 {Black is done for.}) 27... c5 {White is definitely still better, but he missed his chance to end the game on the spot.} 28. Ne2 Ra6 29. Qxg7+ (29. Qa3 {would secure the advantage, but this is not an easy move to play.}) 29... Kxg7 30. Nc3 Rb8 31. Rd2 Be6 32. h6+ Kf7 33. Rhd1 Nc8 34. Bxa4 Bxc4 35. Bd7 Ke7 36. Bxc8 Rxc8 37. b3 Bd3 38. Kb2 Ke6 39. Rxd3 exd3 40. Rxd3 c4 41. Rd4 cxb3 42. axb3 {Black defended the unpleasant position well, and now it looks very balanced. But the balance was soon broken.} Rca8 43. Rc4 Ra1 44. Rc7 R8a7 {This was a surprising blunder from a strong player, but everyone is human. How should White proceed?} 45. Rxh7 {White just put the rook on c7 with the not-so-subtle intention of snacking on the h7 -pawn. Black just defended it. We know what to ask.} Rxh7 46. Kxa1 Rb7 47. Kb2 {Now White should definitely win, but it takes some precision, and Ivanisevic missed a couple of chances.} Rb8 48. Kc2 Rb7 49. Nb1 Kd5 50. Nd2 Rc7+ 51. Kd3 Rb7 52. Kc3 Rc7+ 53. Nc4 Rb7 {The position has been statically winning for a while, but White was struggling to break through. Here, he had a very clean opportunity to do so. How should he proceed?} 54. Nd2 (54. e4+ $1 {This is the pawn break that White will eventually have to make in order to get another passed pawn. It looks like the pawn hangs here in two ways, but what happens if we do it anyway?  A)} Kxe4 55. Nxd6+ {White wins the rook, and the game.  B) fxe4 f5! Black will not stop the connected passed pawns. gxf5 g6 and the pawns are unstoppable. Ke6 h7 Rb8 g7 White makes a queen.}) 54... Rc7+ 55. Kd3 Rc1 56. b4 Rd1 57. Ke2 Rh1 58. Nf1 Kc4 59. Kf2 Kxb4 60. Kg2 Rh5 {White has not played too well in the preceding moves, and now he has to be very accurate to win the game. How would you continue?} 61. Nh2 {Now Black holds.} (61. e4 $1 {This is the move White really wants to play to mobilize his pawns, as we saw at an earlier moment. Here, it looks ill-advised because after Black takes on e4 , White will not be ready for f4 - f5 . But we have to ask: What if I do it anyway?} fxe4 62. Ng3 $1 Rh4 63. f5 $1 {This looks wrong. Black will take on f5 and White can push neither g6 nor h7 , and taking on f5 would allow a check on g4 . But again... what if I do it anyway?} gxf5 64. Nxf5 $1 Rg4+ 65. Ng3 $1 {And it transpires that Black cannot safely take on g7 . White is threatening h7 , making a queen directly. If} Rxg5 66. h7 {, the pawn goes through. Rh4 Nxe4! White clips an important pawn, and the knight is immune from capture. There will still be some work to do once queen against rook is reached, but the position is definitely winning. Rxe4 h7} Rg4 67. Kf2 Rg5 68. h8=Q Re5) (61. Ng3 Rh4 62. e4 {is also a fine move order.}) 61... Kc5 {Black is out of danger. The king gets back in time.} 62. Nf3 Kd5 63. Kg3 Ke6 64. Nd4+ (64. Nh4 Kf7 $1 {Just in time.}) 64... Kf7 65. Nb5 Ke7 66. Kg2 Rh4 67. Nc7 Kf7 68. Nd5 Rg4+ 69. Kf3 Rg1 70. Ke2 Ra1 71. Nc3 Ra5 72. Kd3 Rc5 73. Kd4 Ra5 74. Kc4 *

