1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Nc6 4. Bxc6+ bxc6 5. O-O e5 6. c3 g5 {I always admired the aggression and fearlessness in Judit's play. Here she is, playing Black against the greatest player who ever lived; all of her pieces are on the back rank, and she just goes for it. That said... this is not a good move, and it can be punished by using the Shankland Method.} 7. d4 {When Black is so underdeveloped, White obviously wants to blow up the center. It looks like this move is ill-advised because once g5 - g4 comes, the knight will be kicked away, and White will lose a pawn. But we always ask, 'What happens if I do it anyway?'} g4 8. Nfd2 (8. Ne1 $2 Ba6 $1 {is a bit annoying.}) (8. Ng5 h6 {is not an option.}) 8... cxd4 9. cxd4 exd4 {Black won a central pawn, but the position is opening far faster than she would like.} 10. Nc4 c5 11. Re1 {Not a subtle move. White wants e4 - e5 next.} Be6 12. Nba3 Be7 13. e5 {It's time to strike.} d5 {Now, where should the knight go?} 14. Nd6+ ({Forward, of course! It looks like this loses a pawn, but we should ask, what happens if I do it anyway? Playing} 14. Nd2 {would lead White nowhere.}) 14... Kf8 {Judit correctly chose not to take on d6 . Her position is lost either way, but avoiding the capture certainly gives her more practical chances, as we will see later on. Now, how should White continue? Garry clearly saw the right idea since he played it on the following move, but he did not need to wait.} (14... Bxd6 15. exd6 Qxd6 16. b4 {again, what if I do it anyway? Black's pawn center crumbles, and she will be crushed after} Ne7 17. bxc5 Qxc5 18. Bb2 {or c4 Qxd4} f6 {.}) 15. Bf4 {This is second best.} (15. b4 $1 {White should play this while he still can.}) 15... h5 ({However, had Judit played} 15... Rb8 $1 {, her position is still quite bad according to the machine, but I think it will take a lot of work for White to finish her off when b4 does not come so easily.}) 16. b4 {This time, Garry did not need to be asked twice.} c4 17. Qxd4 h4 18. Nab5 Rh5 19. Qe3 Qd7 20. Nd4 Bd8 {Black is definitely lost here, but breaking the position down is not that easy since the center is still closed. Garry started to falter.} 21. Qd2 (21. f3 $1 {This looks like the best way to me. Tear open the f -file.}) ({The computer likes} 21. h3 gxh3 22. gxh3 Bxh3 23. Kh2 {.}) 21... Ne7 22. b5 Ng6 23. Nc6 {All of a sudden, things are not so clear.} c3 24. Qc1 g3 {This was a bit much.} (24... d4 $1 {A slower, buildup move was prudent.} 25. Nxd4 Bd5 {Black will be more prepared to push ... g3 once she has more pieces pointed towards White's kingside. The machine even claims she is a bit better here.}) 25. fxg3 hxg3 26. Bxg3 Bb6+ 27. Kh1 Kg7 28. Qxc3 Rah8 29. Rf1 Kg8 30. Rae1 {Now, Black is very, very lost. The machine gives +4. But Judit absolutely found the best move in practical terms.} ({I think Black can resign after} 30. Rf6 {.}) 30... f5 {This move looks suicidal. Black allows exf6 , winning a pawn, opening up her king, and opening the e -file for White's rook. But we ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} 31. exf6 Qh7 {All of a sudden, things are not so clear. White is surely winning with best play, but the position is very messy and has to be calculated well. With the clocks ticking down, Kasparov himself was unable to find the way cleanly.} 32. f7+ Bxf7 (32... Kf8 33. Qxh8+ Qxh8 34. Rxe6) 33. Qxh8+ {In human terms, this move would be fine if it was followed up correctly.} (33. h3 {fails to} Rxh3+ 34. gxh3 Qxh3+) (33. Bh4 $3 {was the only clean win, and it is an incredibly difficult move to find.} Rxh4 34. h3 $1 {By virtue of pitching the g3 -bishop, the queen defends against sacrifices on h3 , and White wins in the center. This is the kind of thing you can only find with quite a lot of time on your clock, even for a player of Kasparov's caliber.}) 33... Nxh8 34. Ne7+ Kf8 35. Nec8 (35. Nef5 $1 {This was the way, and now White is in good shape with Rc1 coming next.} Bd8 36. Rc1) 35... Qd3 (35... Bc5 $1 {It takes veins of ice to play a move like this one, but Black is indeed winning. You don't beat Kasparov without finding some very difficult moves!}) 36. Rf3 {Now Black is back in only-move territory and in a wildly complicated position to boot. Judit did not manage.} Qc2 (36... Qd2 37. Nxf7 Nxf7 {and} 38. Nd6 {runs into} Rxh2+ {but after Ref1 Bc5! , Black keeps kicking. This could be found in a classical game with a lot of time. In rapid, no chance.}) 37. Nxf7 Nxf7 38. Nd6 {The ping pong match is over; the point is in Kasparov's hands, and it is there to stay.} Qg6 39. Rxf7+ Qxf7 40. Nxf7 Kxf7 41. Re5 Rxe5 42. Bxe5 d4 43. Kg1 Ke6 44. Bb8 Kd5 45. Kf1 Kc4 46. Ke2 d3+ 47. Kd1 Kxb5 48. h4 a5 49. Be5 Bf2 50. h5 Be3 51. Bg7 *

