14... d5 {Not a subtle move. Black wants ... d4 . What should White play?} 15. g5 ({What happens if I do it anyway?} 15. Kb1 Rfd8 {leads to a more controlled position}) 15... hxg5 (15... Nh5 $1 {This was the only way for Black to keep his head above water. I had seen the move and thought it would not hold together, but the computer is a very tenacious defender.} 16. Qg4 {and if} hxg5 $2 17. Qxh5 gxh4 18. f6 $1 {, mate is on the way:} Bxf6 19. Nxd5 Qd8 20. Rg1) ({After} 15... d4 $1 16. gxh6 dxc3 17. hxg7 Rfd8 18. Bc4 {, I thought White has a huge attack, but the computer seems to think Black is more or less okay.}) 16. Bxg5 d4 {This is the move that should scare White, but I had realized that it was not a major concern and that I would not lose the a2 -pawn.} 17. Bxf6 Bxf6 18. Ne4 Qxa2 (18... Be5 {Also played immediately. I suspect my opponent missed after that after Qg5 ! Black does not have ... f6 available.} 19. Qg5 $1 {Here, I initially thought it was already over - f5 - f6 looks like the end of the world as Black knows it. Once he was thinking for 30 minutes, I saw some of Black's resources, and they looked very frightening... In our next exercises, I'll challenge you to refute the toughest tries.}) 19. Nxf6+ {Black gets mated.} gxf6 20. Rg1+ {And mate.} *

