19... Nb4 {The exclamation mark is more for bravery than objectivity. White clearly wants to push f6 to give mate, but I noticed my opponent had a dangerous idea. I spent half an hour calculating but ultimately came to the right conclusion. Should we use the Shankland Method here or not?} 20. f6 {At this point, I calculated for a very long time. I realized that this would be my only chance to play f6 before the knight gets back to d5 , but I also saw that after ... Bf4 +, Black would get a ton of counterplay. I asked what would happen if I did it anyway... I was not completely sure about the consequences, but I was confident enough to pull the trigger.} (20. a3 {This stops all threats, but also allows Black to bring the knight back to d5 , stopping f6 and leaving White with no relevant threats.} Nd5 {Black looks fine to me. If Qc7 , I can play} 21. Rd2 {.}) 20... Bf4+ ({Black pitches the bishop to distract my queen for a move, and then his own attack begins.} 20... g6 21. Bc4 Kh7 22. Qh4+ {is winning for White.}) 21. Qxf4 Rac8 22. Bd3 ({Now, Qa4 is the most challenging move. We'll continue in our next exercise.} 22. Rd2 Qxa2 {is more problematic.}) *

