22... Qa4 {The most challenging move. Now, how should White continue?} 23. c3 {This is the move White really wants to play, slowing Black down for a moment to get the queen to g5 next. I had seen it, thought it failed, and threw it away. But we should always ask... what happens if I do it anyway?} (23. Qf2 $6 {This was my plan when I played 20.f6 . It should be winning, but White had a better solution.} Rxc2+ $1 24. Bxc2 Rc8 {I was happy to find the only winning move here, which I had seen in advance during my long think before deciding to push f6 .}) (23. Rd2 Nxd3+) (23. Qd2 Rxc2+ 24. Bxc2 Rc8 {is a better version of the game for Black.}) ({We can't go} 23. Qf5 {due to the ... Rxc2 threat, same as the immediate Qg5?? fails to} Rxc2+ 24. Kb1 Qxa2#) (23. Nc3 $2 {Regrettably, this does not work.} dxc3 24. Qg5 {All I need is one move to give mate, but of course, Black will not oblige.} cxb2+ 25. Kd2 Rxc2+ $1 26. Ke3 Re8+ {The engine claims mate in 10 for Black. One look at White's king should be enough to realize that he will not survive.}) 23... dxc3 24. Qg5 Nxa2+ {This was the move that concerned me.} (24... Nxd3+ 25. Rxd3 cxb2+ 26. Kd2 {White's king escapes the checks surprisingly easily.} Rc2+ 27. Ke3 Re2+ 28. Kxe2 Qxe4+ 29. Kf2 {No more checks, and White wins with his extra rook.}) (24... cxb2+ 25. Kd2) 25. Kb1 c2+ 26. Bxc2 Qxc2+ {And here I realized Black would bring the queen back to a4 with check, then take e4 with check, then choose between making a draw or bringing the queen back to g6 . But always be wary of automatic moves...} 27. Ka1 {By leaving the a2 -knight on the board, White makes sure that ... Qa4 does not come with check. I am safe for one move, so Black is mated.} (27. Kxa2 Qa4+ 28. Kb1 Qxe4+ {Black has at least a draw but can even play for a win.}) *

