1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bg5 e6 7. Qd2 Be7 {This was sort of the main line.} (7... a6 {is my recommendation in my Classical Sicilian LTR.}) 8. O-O-O O-O 9. f4 h6 10. Bh4 e5 11. Nf5 {At this point, I realized I was about to play a wildly complicated game.} (11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. fxe5 dxe5 13. Qxd8 Rxd8 14. Rxd8+ Bxd8 15. Bc4 {This looked a bit vegetarian to me during the game. The engine likes White by some small margin, but even with this knowledge, I believe Black is pretty much okay}) 11... Bxf5 12. exf5 Qa5 {Now, I was a bit too aggressive with my use of the Shankland Method here. What should White play?} 13. g4 {This was too aggressive and unnecessary.} (13. Bc4 $1 {Developing a new piece and increasing the pressure on the d5 -square was simple, strong, and clearly best. This is now a very good bishop, and Black is quite a bit worse.}) 13... exf4 {This was not terrible, but Black had a much cleaner route to equality.} (13... Ne4 $1 14. Nxe4 Qxa2 $1 {and White should bail out into a balanced endgame. because} 15. Qe1 $2 {is way too optimistic.} Bxh4 16. Qxh4 Nb4 17. Qe1 Qa1+ 18. Kd2 Qxb2 19. Bd3 Rac8 {Things have gone badly wrong. After Qd5 Qxd5 Rxd5 Bxh4} 20. fxe5 dxe5 {c3 , White should be fine despite the pawn minus, but definitely not better.}) (13... Nxg4 $2 {would be way too optimistic:} 14. Bxe7 Nxe7 15. Rg1 {It's quite clear this is not a pawn Black wanted to grab.} Nf6 {is simply met by} 16. Qxd6 {and} h5 17. h3 Qc5 18. Bd3 {Nf2 f6 is crushing.}) 14. Qxf4 d5 {Not a subtle move. Black wants ... d4 . What should White play?} 15. g5 ({What happens if I do it anyway?} 15. Kb1 Rfd8 {leads to a more controlled position}) 15... hxg5 (15... Nh5 $1 {This was the only way for Black to keep his head above water. I had seen the move and thought it would not hold together, but the computer is a very tenacious defender.} 16. Qg4 {and if} hxg5 $2 17. Qxh5 gxh4 18. f6 $1 {, mate is on the way:} Bxf6 19. Nxd5 Qd8 20. Rg1) ({After} 15... d4 $1 16. gxh6 dxc3 17. hxg7 Rfd8 18. Bc4 {, I thought White has a huge attack, but the computer seems to think Black is more or less okay.}) 16. Bxg5 d4 {This is the move that should scare White, but I had realized that it was not a major concern and that I would not lose the a2 -pawn.} 17. Bxf6 Bxf6 18. Ne4 Be5 {Also played immediately. I suspect my opponent missed after that after Qg5 ! Black does not have ... f6 available.} (18... Qxa2 19. Nxf6+ {Black gets mated.} gxf6 20. Rg1+) 19. Qg5 {Here, I initially thought it was already over - f6 looks like the end of the world as Black knows it. Once he was thinking for 30 minutes, I saw some of Black's resources, and they looked very frightening...} Nb4 {The exclamation mark is more for bravery than objectivity. White clearly wants to push f6 to give mate, but I noticed my opponent had a dangerous idea. I spent half an hour calculating but ultimately came to the right conclusion. Should we use the Shankland Method here or not?} ({A)} 19... Qc7 {This was the other serious try. While it is objectively a stronger move, it leads to a really miserable and quite simple position. The game continuation was the fighter's way of resisting.} 20. Rd2 $1 {This is necessary so ... Nb4 does not come with tempo. It looks like a bad idea because it walks straight into ... Bf4 , but we should always ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} ({(} 20. Kb1 $6 Nb4 $1 {This is much less convincing. Black wins a tempo by threatening c2 , and he is ready to play ...f6 next. White is better, but the game goes on.)}) 20... Nb4 $2 {runs into} 21. f6 {, so Black has to go for} Bf4 {Nf6+! Kh8} 22. Qh5 Bh6 {Ng4! Black now has major problems with the king, and White is ready to take on h6 . Black can avoid mate but at a heavy cost. Qf4 Nxh6} 23. Qxh6 {Now, it should be pretty clear that the black king will survive. So, we cash in. Qxh6+!} gxh6 24. Bg2 {Black was able to exchange the queens, but he'll lose the d4 -pawn. White should have excellent winning chances in this rook ending.}) ({B)} 19... Qd8 $2 {A natural attempt to stop f6 , but imagination comes into play...} 20. f6 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway?} Bxf6 21. Nxf6+ Qxf6 22. Qxf6 gxf6 23. Rg1+ Kh7 24. Rd3 {Who needs queens to deliver checkmate?}) ({C)} 19... f6 $2 20. Bc4+ {.}) ({D)} 19... Qxa2 20. f6 $1 {Black is mated.}) 20. f6 {At this point, I calculated for a very long time. I realized that this would be my only chance to play f6 before the knight gets back to d5 , but I also saw that after ... Bf4 +, Black would get a ton of counterplay. I asked what would happen if I did it anyway... I was not completely sure about the consequences, but I was confident enough to pull the trigger.} (20. a3 {This stops all threats, but also allows Black to bring the knight back to d5 , stopping f6 and leaving White with no relevant threats.} Nd5 {Black looks fine to me. If Qc7 , I can play} 21. Rd2 {.}) 20... Bf4+ ({Black pitches the bishop to distract my queen for a move, and then his own attack begins.} 20... g6 21. Bc4 Kh7 22. Qh4+ {is winning for White.}) 21. Qxf4 Rac8 22. Bd3 (22. Rd2 Qxa2 {is more problematic.}) 22... Qa4 {The most challenging move. Now, how should White continue?} 23. Qf2 {This was my plan when I played 20.f6 . It should be winning, but White had a better solution.} ({A)} 23. c3 $1 {This is the move White really wants to play, slowing Black down for a moment to get the queen to g5 next. I had seen it, thought it failed, and threw it away. But we should always ask... what happens if I do it anyway?} dxc3 24. Qg5 $1 {and if} cxb2+ 25. Kd2 {, the white king is safe. Black has other options:}) ({A1) Nxd3+ Rxd3 cxb2+} 23. Kd2 {White's king escapes the checks surprisingly easily.} Rxc2+ {Ke3 Re2+ Kxe2 Qxe4+ Kf2 No more checks, and White wins with his extra rook.}) ({A2) Nxa2+ was the move that concerned me.} 23. Kb1 {c2+ Bxc2} Qxc2+ {And here I realized Black would bring the queen back to a4 with check, then take e4 with check, then choose between making a draw or bringing the queen back to g6 . But always be wary of automatic moves...} 24. Ka1 {By leaving the a2 -knight on the board, White makes sure that ... Qa4 does not come with check. I am safe for one move, so Black is mated. Kxa2} Qa4 25. Kb1 {Qxe4+ Black has at least a draw but can even play for a win.}) ({B)} 23. Qd2 Rxc2+ 24. Bxc2 Rc8 {is a better version of the game for Black.}) ({C) We can't go} 23. Qf5 {due to the ... Rxc2 threat, same as the immediate Qg5?? fails to} Rxc2+ 24. Kb1 Qxa2#) ({D)} 23. Nc3 $2 {Regrettably, this does not work.} dxc3 24. Qg5 {All I need is one move to give mate, but of course, Black will not oblige.} cxb2+ 25. Kd2 Rxc2+ $1 26. Ke3 Re8+ {The engine claims mate in 10 for Black. One look at White's king should be enough to realize that he will not survive.}) 23... Rxc2+ 24. Bxc2 Rc8 {I was happy to find the only winning move here, which I had seen in advance during my long think before deciding to push f5 - f6 .} 25. Rd2 (25. Nc3 $2 {It's very unfortunate that this does not work} Rxc3 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway?} 26. bxc3 Qa3+ {when} 27. Kb1 Qxa2+ 28. Kc1 Qa3+ {leads to a draw immediately.} 29. Kd2 Qxc3+ 30. Ke2 Qxc2+ {I managed to get away from the queenside, I have a massive material advantage, and Black is out of pieces to attack with... but somehow it is a draw anyway! White cannot escape the checks without losing a rook. For example,} 31. Rd2 Qe4+ $1 {wins the h1 -rook. Kf3 Qf5+ Kg2 Qg4+!} 32. Kf1 {Qxd1+ or Qg3} Qe2+ {Kh3 Qh5+ Qh4 Qf3+ Qg3 Qh5+} 33. Kg2 {Qe2+} Qxf2+ {Qg4+ etc. The issue is that White can't bring the king to the back rank due to the hanging rook on d1 . Black has a perpetual.}) 25... Qxa2 (25... Rxc2+ 26. Rxc2 Nd3+ 27. Kb1 Nxf2 {White has forced mate with Rc8 here, but I had only seen that I could simply take the knight on f2 , which is clearly good enough to win the game. White will have way too many pieces for the queen. The computer screams} 28. Rc8+ Kh7 29. fxg7 Kxg7 30. Rg1+ {is just winning.}) 26. Kd1 Qa1+ 27. Ke2 Qxh1 {The dust has settled, and I am still a piece up. For one move and one move only, I do not have to deal with any mating threats and can start looking for my own ideas.} 28. Qg3 {It is very important to first bring the queen to g3 before saving the bishop.} (28. Bd3 $2 {would be premature. Following} Re8 29. Rd1 $2 Nxd3 $1 {, it is a problem that the queen is loose on g3 .}) 28... g6 29. Bd3 {Now and only now, Bc2 - d3 comes. White is ready to trap the queen next with Rd2 - d1 .} Nxd3 (29... Re8 30. Rd1 $1 {It is quite nice that} Nxd3 {does not hit my queen! Rxe4+} 31. Kd2 {and White wins.}) 30. Kxd3 {White has consolidated, for some value of the word, and is not in much danger of facing any more threats. My conversion from here was far from perfect, but it got the job done.} (30. Qxd3 $2 Re8 {Oops!}) 30... Rd8 {Not the most practically challenging, in my opinion, but by this point, we were both getting pretty low on time.} (30... Qc1 $1 {This was Black's best chance, though still insufficient.} 31. Nd6 $1 Qf1+ 32. Kxd4 $1 Qxf6+ 33. Qe5 Qh4+ 34. Ke3 Rc7 {Black still has some practical chances, but he should lose against best play.}) 31. Qf4 b5 32. Ng3 (32. Kc2 {The computer prefers this by a wide margin, but my move was quite sufficient.}) (32. Qh6 Qf3+ {and the c4 -square is off-limits.} 33. Kc2 Qxe4+) 32... Qc6 (32... Qc1 33. Ke2 Re8+ 34. Kf2 d3 {I missed this strong move, but I think White should still win.}) 33. Ke2 {White brings the king back to the safe zone.} (33. Rc2 $2 Qd5 $1 {With ... Qb3 and ... d3 coming, it is suddenly much less clear} 34. Qh6 Qf3+ $1 35. Kd2 Qxf6) 33... Rd6 34. Ne4 Re6 35. Rxd4 Qc2+ 36. Kf3 Qb3+ 37. Nc3 {And Black is checkmated, ending a wild game. The Shankland Method surely proved useful many times!} *

