19. Rb1 {Now, Black has a very powerful idea. It's clear what we want to do, but we need to use the Shankland Method to make it work.} dxc4 {Clearly, this is the move Black wants to make. But... what do we do after Nc6 ? It looks like a problem.} 20. Nc6 {Shankland rule again!} (20. Bc3 {should have been preferred when White is only slightly worse.}) 20... Rxc6 {What happens if I do it anyway?} (20... Bb4 {holds the material but does not make use of an important chance.}) 21. Bxf6 {Now, it seems like all has gone wrong. But always examine all checks and captures...} Qxf2+ {Bang! Once you see this move, the details are pretty easy to work out.} 22. Kxf2 Bc5+ {Of course, White's king is not permitted to return to g1 and h1 .} 23. Kf3 (23. Kf1 c3+ $1 24. Re2 c2 $1 {The most brutal of many winning moves.}) 23... Rxf6+ 24. Kg4 {Now, once more, we need to use the Shankland Method.} Ne5+ {This is clearly the move Black wants to play to continue his attack. It looks like a blunder - after all, White can take on e5 , and then there is a problem with the back rank. But... What happens if I do it anyway?} 25. Rxe5 (25. Kg5 {This was played in the game, but now everything wins. The text requires an accurate move.} Rg6+ 26. Kh5 f6 27. Rxe5 Rxe5+ 28. Kh4 Bc8 {White resigned.}) 25... Bc8+ {White will be mated.} (25... Rxe5 $2 {would be far too impulsive and blunder a full point.} 26. Rb8+ $1 Bf8 27. Ne4 {Black is missing ... Bc8 , and he is out of steam.}) *

