[PlyCount "120"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 a6 5. e3 Bf5 6. Nh4 (6. Qb3 {is my recommendation in my Chessable 1.d4 LTR, and I stand by it.}) 6... Bg4 7. Qc2 e6 8. h3 Bh5 9. g4 Nfd7 10. Ng2 Bg6 11. Qb3 b5 12. c5 a5 13. Nf4 b4 14. Nxg6 hxg6 15. Na4 Na6 16. a3 Be7 17. Bd2 O-O 18. Bd3 e5 19. O-O Nc7 20. axb4 Rb8 21. Nb6 axb4 22. Nxd7 Qxd7 23. Ra7 Bf6 24. dxe5 Bxe5 25. Bxb4 Qd8 26. f4 Qh4 {I had played a less-than-stellar game to this point, but I sort of lucked into a good position. Now, White clearly has to play either Rf3 or Kg2 to keep things under control on the kingside. I chose the wrong one.} 27. Rf3 {Now, How should Black play?} (27. Kg2 $1 {This should have been preferred.} Ne6 28. Qa3 $1 {Black is missing the resource he has the way the game was played.}) 27... Ne6 {This is the move Black wants to make to bring more pieces into the attack. It ignores the threat to the bishop, but what happens if I do it anyway?} 28. Qa3 (28. fxe5 $2 Ng5 $1 {White will be mated.}) 28... Qf6 {Another strong move. Black retreats the queen to stop the threat of fxe5 , and now b2 is hanging as well.} 29. Rf2 {This was too ambitious. Now, how should Black respond?} (29. Kg2 Bxb2 30. Qa5 {Black is better, but the game goes on.}) 29... Nxf4 {I fell victim to my own method. Black clearly wants to take on f4 , but it looks like the response Bf1 would be a problem. He should ask: What if I do it anyway?} 30. Bf1 (30. exf4 $2 {This one is easier to disarm.} Bd4 {White collapses immediately.}) 30... Qh4 31. Rxf4 (31. exf4 Bd4 32. Be1 Rfe8 $1 {and Black wins.}) 31... Qg3+ 32. Bg2 {Now what?} Rxb4 (32... d4 $1 {This was a winner. White's rook is bullied off the f4 -square, and then the queen will reach h2 .} 33. Rf3 Qh2+ 34. Kf1 dxe3 $1 {Black is ready to follow up with ... e2 .} 35. Qxe3 {is met by} Rxb4 {and after Rxe3} 36. Qf4 $1 {, Black wins the bishop on b4 , allowing him to continue his attack with equal material. White is done for.}) 33. Rxb4 Qe1+ 34. Bf1 Qg3+ 35. Bg2 Qe1+ 36. Bf1 g5 {I was very surprised to see Black decline a draw here, but then I realized in horror what he had in mind. ...Bg3 is coming next, and I no longer have Rf4 ! The immediate} (36... Bg3 {was met by} 37. Rf4 {.}) 37. e4 (37. Ra8 $1 {This was the only convincing way to hold.} Rxa8 38. Qxa8+ Kh7 39. Qa3 $1 Bg3 40. Qd3+ $1 g6 41. Qe2 $1 Qxb4 42. Qf3 $1 {White takes on f7 and saves the game. This sequence was not easy to find!} Be5 43. Qxf7+ Bg7) (37. Qb3 {simply allows} Bg3 38. Qc2 Qxb4) 37... dxe4 (37... Bg3 $2 38. Qf3 {The point of White's e4 is revealed.} Qxb4 39. Qxg3) 38. Rc4 Qd2 {Well spotted. Black wins a tempo by threatening mate on h2 , and he stops my rook from reaching the c2 -square.} 39. Bg2 Qe1+ 40. Bf1 Qd2 41. Bg2 e3 42. Qa1 e2 43. Re4 {Now, Black is winning, but he should be precise. Using the Shankland Method could have done him a favor here.} Bxb2 {This was a big mistake. The idea was to force my queen to the undefended b1 -square to be able to give a check on d1 next, but I have an unexpected resource.} (43... Qd1+ $1 {This is the move Black wants to play, but it looks like I have things under control after Kf2 . But always ask, what if I do it anyway?} 44. Kf2 Bd4+ $1 45. Kg3 Bxc5 $1 {A silent but deadly killer. The twin threats of ... Bxa7 and ... Qd6 prove impossible to meet, and White is crushed after, for example,} 46. b4 Bxa7 {or Raa4 Qd6+} 47. Kf3 Qd3+ {.}) 44. Rd7 {Black clearly missed this resource, and now it's a game again.} (44. Qb1 $2 Qd1+ 45. Kf2 Qxb1) (44. Qa2 e1=Q+) 44... Qxd7 45. Qxb2 Qd1+ 46. Kf2 f5 47. Rxe2 fxg4+ 48. Ke3 {A sad necessity.} (48. Kg3 Qd3+ $1 {White will be mated. Some sample lines:} 49. Kh2 g3+ {or Kxg4 Rf4+ Kxg5 Qf5}) 48... Re8+ 49. Be4 (49. Kf2 g3+ 50. Kf3 Qd3+) 49... gxh3 50. Qa2+ Kh8 51. Qc4 Qg1+ 52. Kd2 {Now, I got lucky to see the next mistake from my opponent.} Rd8+ {What should White play?} 53. Bd3 {This is clearly the move I want to make, blocking the check and stopping it forever. It looks like this simply lets the h -pawn through, but the Shankland Method makes it work.} Qa1 {Now, after having suffered in a miserable position for hours on end, I saw my chance to make a draw and took it right away. This was foolish. I could have used the Shankland Method again and even won the game!} (53... h2 54. Qe4 $1 {And all of a sudden, Black cannot stop the twin mate threats of Qh7 and Qe8 . The machine claims it is forced mate.} h1=Q 55. Qe8+ Rxe8 56. Rxe8# {I would have so loved to reach this final position.}) 54. Qe4 (54. Re3 $1 {The rook going to h3 with mate is not a small threat!} g4 {Now} 55. Qe4 {wins, but it is not nearly as clean as the move that answersthe question, what does White really want to play? Rxh3+! Shankland Method! The queen reaches h4 , and White wins. gxh3 Qh4+ .}) 54... Qa5+ 55. Kd1 Qa1+ 56. Kc2 Qa2+ 57. Kc1 Qa3+ 58. Kd2 Qa5+ 59. Kd1 Qa1+ 60. Kd2 Qa5+ *

