{White had seen enough. ...Rxg3 is coming next, and Be3 would be met by Qxe3 .} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. h3 Bb7 9. d3 d5 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nbd2 Qd7 {Nowadays, this position is fairly well known, but it was not at back in 2015. Anand had taken on e5 and beaten Aronian, but Black could improve, as was shown in this game.} 12. Nxe5 Nxe5 13. Rxe5 Nf4 (13... Nf6 {was Aronian's choice, but it does not fully equalize, and Vishy won a nice game after} 14. Nf3 Bd6 15. Re1 {.}) 14. Nf3 {Now, Black has a couple of tempting options. What is the best one?} Nxg2 {This is definitely the one Black would like to play most, taking the most critical pawn around White's king and luring him to the long diagonal. It looks like Black's pieces cannot join the attack too fast, but what happens if I do it anyway?} 15. Kxg2 a5 {This is a very effective follow-up that leaves White in horrible shape. Black gains an incidental tempo by threatening ... a4 , taking the b3 bishop, and equalizing material when the monster on b7 will reign supreme. Once White parries this threat, ... Ra6 will come to smash the kingside.} 16. Rxe7 {If White plays slow, he will be crushed.} (16. a4 Ra6 {and the rook is joining the attack via g6 .}) 16... Qxe7 17. c3 (17. a4 $1 {According to the all-powerful computer, this move offers White some chance to save the game, but I still think a human would go down hard.}) 17... Ra6 {The rest was easy for Black.} 18. d4 Rf6 19. d5 (19. Bg5 Rg6 $1) 19... a4 20. Bc2 {Now, Black has to be careful - there is only one winning move, and he needs to use the Shankland Method to find it. What would you play?} Rd8 {This is clearly the move Black wants to play, bringing his last piece out and getting ready to take on d5 . It looks like this blunders Bg5 but always ask...} 21. Qe1 {The move Ivanchuk played in the game loses every which way, but his position was beyond salvation anyway.} Qd7 22. Ng5 h6 23. Ne4 Rg6+ 24. Kh2 f5 25. Ng3 Qxd5 26. Qg1 Qf3 *

