[FEN "rn1qkb1r/pb3p2/2p1p2p/1p2P1pn/P1pP4/2N2NB1/1P2BPPP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 0 11"]
[PlyCount "6"]

11... a6 {White has sacrificed a pawn and needs to look for attacking chances. How should he continue?} 12. Nxg5 {This is clearly the move White would like to play, grabbing a pawn and taking advantage of the hanging knight on h5 . It looks like White will lose a piece after ... Nxg3 . But as always, we must ask: What if I do it anyway?} (12. O-O $2 {A slow move like this one won't do.} Nd7 {There is no great way to continue the initiative, and Black is ready for ... Qb6 and ... O-O-O .}) 12... Nxg3 (12... hxg5 13. Bxh5 {with a crushing position for White.}) 13. Nxf7 Kxf7 14. fxg3 {All of a sudden, Black is facing a vicious attack. With O-O , Bg4 , and Ne4 on the way, White's pieces are mobilizing fast. The position is still messy and unclear, with the computer insisting on equality, but this is a lot better than being down a clean pawn! Soon, Anand made some mistakes and should have been crushed.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 4"]
[Black "Kramnik-Anand Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "b4rk1/3nq1r1/p1pNp3/1p2P1bQ/PPpP4/6PB/6PP/R4R1K w - - 0 25"]
[PlyCount "6"]

25. Ne4 {A wild back-and-forth affair ultimately reached this position, where Vishy made a fantastic decision. What should Black play?} c5 {The computer thinks that other moves are equally good, but in my opinion, this one is absolutely best in human practice. For the last several moves, Black has been facing two main problems. First, the e6 -pawn is dreadfully weak. Second, the a8 -bishop has been a piece of dead wood. Black would absolutely love to push ... c5 to break out the bishop. It looks like this loses to Nxg5 . But what if I do it anyway?} 26. Nxg5 Bd5 {Now we see the point. While White took back the lost piece, the knight on g5 now really is hanging since e6 is properly defended. If White saves his knight, it will mean retreating, and his attack will lose power, and Black will crush the queenside.} (26... Rxg5 $2 27. Bxe6+ $1 {and things are going wrong for Black.}) 27. Nf3 ({This was complacent.} 27. Nxe6 $1 {was an absolute must. After} Bxe6 28. bxc5 {, I would definitely prefer Black here, but the game goes on. White is not without counterplay, and his central pawns could start moving.}) 27... cxb4 {All of a sudden, Black's king is totally safe, and he has no weaknesses. His queenside pawn mass will rush through without facing any real resistance.} *

