1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. d4 c6 5. Bg5 h6 (5... dxc4 6. e4 b5 7. e5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Bg3 Nd5 {is the standard Botvinnik order.}) 6. Bh4 dxc4 7. e4 g5 8. Bg3 b5 9. Be2 Bb7 10. e5 Nh5 11. a4 a6 {White has sacrificed a pawn and needs to look for attacking chances. How should he continue?} 12. Nxg5 {This is clearly the move White would like to play, grabbing a pawn and taking advantage of the hanging knight on h5 . It looks like White will lose a piece after ... Nxg3 . But as always, we must ask: What if I do it anyway?} (12. O-O $2 {A slow move like this one won't do.} Nd7 {There is no great way to continue the initiative, and Black is ready for ... Qb6 and ... O-O-O .}) 12... Nxg3 (12... hxg5 13. Bxh5 {with a crushing position for White.}) 13. Nxf7 Kxf7 14. fxg3 {All of a sudden, Black is facing a vicious attack. With O-O , Bg4 , and Ne4 on the way, White's pieces are mobilizing fast. The position is still messy and unclear, with the computer insisting on equality, but this is a lot better than being down a clean pawn! Soon, Anand made some mistakes and should have been crushed.} Kg8 15. O-O Nd7 16. Bg4 Qe7 17. Ne4 Rh7 18. Nd6 Rb8 19. b4 {This was too sophisticated. A blunt solution was quite fine.} (19. axb5 $1 {This was a winner. I'm not sure why Kramnik did not do it - the move looks fairly obvious to me. If} axb5 $1 20. Ra7 $1 Ba8 21. Rf6 $1 {, Black is collapsing.} ({cxb5} 21. Nb7 $1 Rxb7 22. Ra6 Rb6 23. Rxb6 Nxb6 24. Rf6 {Here as well, Black falls apart.})) 19... h5 20. Bh3 Bh6 21. Kh1 Bg5 22. Qc2 Rg7 23. Qe2 Ba8 24. Qxh5 Rf8 25. Ne4 {A wild back-and-forth affair ultimately reached this position, where Vishy made a fantastic decision. What should Black play?} c5 {The computer thinks that other moves are equally good, but in my opinion, this one is absolutely best in human practice. For the last several moves, Black has been facing two main problems. First, the e6 -pawn is dreadfully weak. Second, the a8 -bishop has been a piece of dead wood. Black would absolutely love to push ... c5 to break out the bishop. It looks like this loses to Nxg5 . But what if I do it anyway?} 26. Nxg5 Bd5 {Now we see the point. While White took back the lost piece, the knight on g5 now really is hanging since e6 is properly defended. If White saves his knight, it will mean retreating, and his attack will lose power, and Black will crush the queenside.} (26... Rxg5 $2 27. Bxe6+ $1 {and things are going wrong for Black.}) 27. Nf3 ({This was complacent.} 27. Nxe6 $1 {was an absolute must. After} Bxe6 28. bxc5 {, I would definitely prefer Black here, but the game goes on. White is not without counterplay, and his central pawns could start moving.}) 27... cxb4 {All of a sudden, Black's king is totally safe, and he has no weaknesses. His queenside pawn mass will rush through without facing any real resistance.} 28. axb5 axb5 29. Nh4 Qg5 {Better safe than sorry. Black makes sure not to allow the knight to reach g6 .} 30. Rxf8+ Nxf8 31. Qe8 Rf7 {Safety first. No rooks coming to f1 today! White can't ever find a way to even give a check, much less make a serious attempt at giving mate.} 32. Nf3 Qg6 33. Qxb5 b3 34. Rf1 Qd3 35. Kg1 Qe3+ 36. Kh1 c3 37. Bxe6 Bxe6 38. d5 Rxf3 {The cleanest of many winning moves.} 39. gxf3 Bh3 40. Qc4 Bxf1 41. Qg4+ (41. Qxf1 c2 {is game over.}) 41... Kh7 42. e6 Ng6 *

