1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 d5 5. cxd5 {I never liked taking on d5 for White in the Classical Nimzo. It always feels like Black gets a ton of counterplay whenever he can open up the c8 - h3 diagonal for his light-squared bishop, and this game was a perfect example.} (5. a3 {I would definitely prefer this move, which I used to beat Sunilduth Narayanan in the final round of the 2022 Chennai Olympiad. Black is surely fine objectively, but I think White's position is a bit easier to handle, and you don't have to worry about the kind of thrashing that White received in the present encounter.}) 5... exd5 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bh4 c5 8. dxc5 O-O 9. e3 Be6 10. a3 Bxc3+ 11. Qxc3 g5 12. Bg3 Ne4 13. Qc2 Nc6 14. b4 f5 {Black's play has not been particularly subtle. Strategically, he is dead lost. White is a pawn up, has a healthy bishop pair, and a better structure. Black's hope is that he can smash White with his lead in development long before any long-term considerations can be taken into account.} 15. Ne2 Qf6 16. Rd1 {How should Black proceed?} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 4"]
[Black "Ali Marandi-Moiseenko Exercis"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r4rk1/pp6/2n1bq1p/2Pp1pp1/1P2n3/P3P1B1/2Q1NPPP/R3KB1R w KQ - 0 16"]
[PlyCount "16"]

16. Rd1 {How should Black proceed?} f4 {This is absolutely the move Black wants to play, tearing open in the center to reach the uncastled king. It looks like White has this square under control, but what happens if I do it anyway?} (16... Rae8 $2 {This looks like a normal move to allow the ... f4 break to happen next, but Black does not have time for such luxuries. White was ready to counter this with f3 .} 17. f3 $1 {The threat to the knight on e4 is extremely annoying, and Black is not fast enough to push ... f4 and get his bishop to f5 . The attack slows down, White is already better, and Black should be precise and energetic, not to lose immediately.} Nxg3 18. hxg3 {and White is totally safe and basically winning. Here, the king will be fine on f2 .}) 17. exf4 Bf5 18. Qc1 Rae8 {All of the pieces are in place to conduct the attack. White is clearly worse, even if he plays a long sequence of best moves, and playing the best moves is not easy! In the game, he did not even manage a single one. But the moves he did play forced Black to be very accurate to bring the point home. Moiseenko was up to the task.} 19. f3 Nc3 20. fxg5 hxg5 21. Rd2 d4 22. Kf2 {The last few moves have not been too hard, but the next one is a little trickier. Black needs to use the Shankland Method.} d3 {This is clearly the move Black wants to play. It looks like it hangs the knight on c3 , but we should ask: What if I do it anyway?} 23. Qxc3 (23. Nxc3 Qd4# {Mate on the board is a convincing end to any variation!}) (23. Bd6 {The rest was easy for Black. From here on, everything wins.}) 23... Rxe2+ ({Black wins the queen. Instead,} 23... dxe2 $2 {is tempting but poor.} 24. Qc4+ {picks up the e2 -pawn.}) *

