14. h4 {I clearly was in no mood to mess around this game. The machine is not too stoked about my attacking possibilities, but it does not seem like an easy position for Black to play. What would you do?} Bb7 {This should be the way. It is clearly the move Black wants to make, but it looks really dangerous not to contest the b3 -bishop and allow h5 to come. But Black should ask: What happens if I do it anyway?} (14... Nd5 $2 {With this move, Black was hoping to be able to bring the bishop to e6 next, neutralizing the pressure from the bishop on b3 . This is understandable enough, but it does not equalize.}) 15. h5 a4 {This is an important in-between move for Black to see and play.} (15... Bxg2 {I suspect Lenier only considered this one. Black is not worse here, but after} 16. Rg1 Bd5 $1 17. Nf4 $1 {The position looks very scary to a human eye.} Bxb3 18. Qxb3) 16. Ba2 (16. Bc2 {This was necessary to go into damage control mode, but it's obviously not a happy move for White and not one Black should be scared of.} Bxg2 17. Rg1 Bf3 {White's attack looks much less scary without the light-squared bishop on the a2 - g8 diagonal.}) 16... Bxg2 {Now and only now, Black takes on g2 .} 17. Rg1 Bd5 {We note that since the bishop was kicked back to the a2 -square, White will not be able to bring the queen to b3 while recapturing.} 18. Nf4 Bxa2 19. Rxa2 Nd5 {Black takes advantage of his access to the d5 -square, and he is totally winning.} *

