{I played board 1 for the United States in the 2015 World Team Championship- it was always a lifelong goal of mine to lead the US Team, and something I dreamed of for as long as I could remember. In the last-round match against Cuba, I had a fascinating game with Lenier Dominguez.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 Bg7 7. Bb5+ {This is a funky sideline, but nobody has anything against the Grunfeld anyway. So, people try stuff like this!} c6 8. Ba4 O-O 9. Ne2 b5 10. Bb3 a5 11. a4 Nd7 12. e5 Nb6 13. axb5 cxb5 14. h4 {I clearly was in no mood to mess around this game. The machine is not too stoked about my attacking possibilities, but it does not seem like an easy position for Black to play. What would you do?} Nd5 {With this move, Black was hoping to be able to bring the bishop to e6 next, neutralizing the pressure from the bishop on b3 . This is understandable enough, but it does not equalize.} (14... Bb7 $1 {This should be the way. It is clearly the move Black wants to make, but it looks really dangerous not to contest the b3 -bishop and allow h5 to come. But Black should ask: What happens if I do it anyway?} 15. h5 {I suspect Lenier only considered} Bxg2 {and Black is not worse here, but after} 16. Rg1 Bd5 $1 17. Nf4 $1 {, the position looks very scary to a human eye.} Bxb3 18. Qxb3) (14... a4 $1 {is an important in-between move for Black to see and play:  A)} 15. Ba2 {Bxg2! Now and only now, Black takes on g2 . Following Rg1 Bd5! We note that since the bishop was kicked back to the a2 -square, White will not be able to bring the queen to b3 while recapturing. Nf4 Bxa2 Rxa2} Nd5 $1 {Black takes advantage of his access to the d5 -square, and he is totally winning.  B) Bc2 This was necessary to go into damage control mode, but it's obviously not a happy move for White and not one Black should be scared of. Bxg2} 16. Rg1 {Bf3⩱ White's attack looks much less scary without the light-squared bishop on the a2 - g8 diagonal.}) 15. h5 Be6 16. Qd3 (16. h6 {In modern times, this move should be automatic. The A.I. engines have taught us the value of slamming a pawn all the way up to h6 . At the time, such moves looked weird, and I was not ready to give up on my plans of opening the h -file and giving mate.}) 16... Qc8 17. Qf3 {This was a bit too sophisticated. I was hoping to provoke the rook to d8 so that Black wouldn't open the f -file when I traded on g6 .} Rd8 18. hxg6 hxg6 19. Bd2 {This was very optimistic and kept more tension, but White certainly is not better.} (19. Qg3 {This was possible, but I calculated a variation that I did not like for White. Can you find the way for Black?} Nxc3 $1 {This is definitely the move Black wants to play. When you start calculating, it might look like there is a problematic tactical resource for White, but always ask... What if I do it anyway?} 20. Nxc3 {is simply met by} Bxb3 {, while Bxe6} 21. Ne2 $1 {Rh8+! looks like a major problem, but when one peels back the layers, we realize that Black is quite fine.} ({(} 21. Qxg6 $2 {fails to} Qe6 {)}) {Now not} 21... Bh8 $2 22. Qxg6+ {and White mates, but Kxh8 Qh3+ Kg8 Bxc8 Nxd4= Unfortunately, despite taking Black's queen, I am not better here. He will take a fair amount of material back.}) 19... b4 20. cxb4 axb4 21. Rc1 Nc3 {This ends my attack, but now I can at least hope to pick up a pawn. I managed to in the game, but I faltered at the finish line.} 22. Bxe6 Qxe6 23. Bxc3 bxc3 24. O-O Ra2 25. Rxc3 {Now, all I need to do is consolidate the pawn, but this is much easier said than done.} Qa6 26. Re1 (26. Rd3 {runs into} Bxe5 {.}) 26... Qa5 27. Kf1 Bh6 28. Rd3 Ra1 {An important defensive resource.} 29. Nc3 (29. Red1 {had been my original idea before I saw the issue with it.} Rxd1+ 30. Rxd1 Qxe5 $1 {Oops!}) 29... Rxe1+ 30. Kxe1 Bc1 31. Qb7 Qa1 32. Ke2 Qb2+ {This felt a bit too desperate. Black should hold the pawn down rook endgame, but it was not necessary to try to bail so fast.} (32... e6 {Something solid like this was fine too. I'm too uncoordinated to make good use of the extra pawn.}) 33. Qxb2 Bxb2 34. Ke3 Bxc3 {Of course, Black should exchange off the bishop. The knight will be a much better piece when all the pawns are clumped close together.} 35. Rxc3 e6 36. f4 Ra8 37. g4 Ra1 {Right before time control, I missed my one big chance. I analyze this endgame in great detail here in Theoretical Rook Endgames . What should White do?} 38. Rc8+ (38. f5 $1 {This was a winner. White's best hope of winning the rook endgame lies in advancing the pawn to f6 to fix f7 as a long-term weakness. It looks like Black can simply take the pawn, but we have to ask: What if I do it anyway?} gxf5 39. gxf5 exf5 40. d5 $1 {The answer is that White slams his pawn through to promotion, and Black can't stop it.} Kf8 41. d6 Ke8 42. Rc8+ Kd7 43. Rc7+ Ke8 44. Kf4 {and White wins, while} Rd1 {Rd3! is a winning pawn endgame for White.}) 38... Kg7 {The chance is gone, and now Black is making an easy draw.} 39. Rc7 Re1+ 40. Kf3 Rf1+ 41. Kg3 Kf8 42. Rd7 Ke8 43. Rd6 Kf8 44. d5 exd5 45. Rxd5 Kg7 46. Rd7 Kf8 47. Rd6 Kg7 48. Rf6 Rh1 49. Ra6 Rf1 50. Ra4 Rg1+ 51. Kh4 Rh1+ 52. Kg5 Re1 53. Ra7 Re4 54. Rb7 Re1 55. Re7 Re4 56. f5 Rxe5 57. Rxe5 f6+ 58. Kf4 fxe5+ 59. Kxe5 gxf5 60. Kxf5 Kf7 61. Ke4 *

