[PlyCount "16"]

23... b6 {This is quite a tough position for White to play and find the best way, and I burned half an hour here. I calculated very well, but not perfectly. I got to use the Shankland Method several times to make sure I was making the right decision.} 24. d6 {If Black gets one or two moves to blockade the d6 -pawn, he will be doing great, so energetic play is called for. White clearly wants to push d6 to wake up the bishop and make a dangerous passed pawn, but it looks like the pawn will be taken by ... Rd8 . But we ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} (24. Ne4 Nxe4 25. Rxd3 Nd6 26. Re3 Kf8 27. Kf1 Re8 28. Rxe8+ Kxe8 {The knight is a far superior piece, and White can't trade it off, so basically, he is lost.}) 24... Rd8 25. Nf3 {And now we see the first point: Black cannot take on d6 .} Nf4 {Time to use the Shankland Method again!} (25... Rxd6 26. Bf1) 26. Ne5 {It looks like this move should fail to ... Ne2 + followed by ... Nd4 , cutting off White's control of the d -file and rounding up the d6 -pawn. But what happens if I do it anyway?} Ne2+ {This looks very natural, but it doesn't solve Black's problems. I think he may just be in trouble.} 27. Kf1 Nd4 {At first, it might look like white is just losing a pawn, but he has a nice resource:} 28. d7 {Shankland Method at play again! White realizes the pawn cannot be safely taken, and it reaches all the way to the seventh rank. Bh3 will come next to bolster it further.} Nxd7 {As it turns out, Black should have bit the bullet and taken on d7 all the same.} (28... h5 $2 {This is a very tempting move and a very human mistake. Black takes control of the g4 -square and is ready for ... Ng4 to come next. However, White wins if he calculates correctly. More exercises for you!}) (28... g5 {I was expecting this one during the game, and Black still can offer some resistance.} 29. Bh3 {and} h5 {is met by} 30. f3 {. Black should play} g4 $1 31. Bxg4 Nxg4 32. Nxg4 Kg7 {when White's best chance is} 33. Ne3 Rxd7 34. Nf5+ Kf6 35. Nxd4 cxd4 36. Ke2 {Black has some saving chances here, though obviously, the ending is still very difficult for him. A mistake would be Rxd4 Rxd4 cxd4 Ke2 and the endgame is lost for Black.}) 29. Nc6 Re8 (29... Nxc6 30. Bxc6) 30. Nxd4 cxd4 31. Rxd4 {White was able to greatly activate his pieces, and his queenside pawn mass will quickly mobilize. The once dead g2 -bishop is now very strong. That said, Black is not without holding chances.} *

[Event "?"]
