[PlyCount "16"]

28... h5 {This is a very tempting move and a very human mistake. Black takes control of the g4 -square and is ready for ... Ng4 to come next. However, White wins if he calculates correctly.} 29. Bh3 {Slow moves won't cut it.} (29. h3 Kf8) (29. f3 Nxd7) 29... Ng4 {Losing immediately, but I suspect the game was beyond saving anyway.} (29... g5 {Tempting, and another exercise for you to calculate.}) 30. Bxg4 (30. Nxg4 hxg4 31. Bxg4 f5 {and Black is better.}) 30... hxg4 {Now, the last Shankland rule of this game.} 31. Rxd4 {What happens if I do it anyway?} (31. Nxg4 Kg7 {and Black picks up the d7 -pawn next.}) (31. b4 {had been my original intention when I first pushed d6 , but once the position after 30...hxg4 was on the board, I realized I had a better option.} f6 32. bxc5 bxc5 ({(Neither} 32... fxe5 33. Rxd4 bxc5 34. Rd5 {nor exd4 c6 are playable for Black.)}) 33. Rxd4 cxd4 34. c5 fxe5 35. c6 Kf7 36. c7 Ke7 37. c8=Q) {However, there is no reason to allow fxe5! White will win a pawn in the rook endgame and can keep pushing, but the position is probably holdable for Black. Rd5 Kf7 Rxe5 Rxd7 Rxc5 .} 31... cxd4 32. b4 {Black resigned. The pawns crash through.} f6 (32... d3 33. c5 d2 34. Ke2 f6 35. c6 fxe5 {Now, White needs to avoid one last trick.} 36. Kd1 $1 {See the annotated game for details.}) (32... Kf8 {Bringing the king to try to control c7 does not help, as Nxf7 is always in the air.} 33. c5 bxc5 34. bxc5 Ke7 35. c6 Kd6 36. Nxf7+ $1) 33. c5 {What happens if I do it anyway?} fxe5 34. c6 Kf7 35. c7 Ke7 36. c8=Q {And White wins.} *

