1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. Nf3 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nc6 6. c4 dxc4 7. Nxc6 Qxd1+ 8. Kxd1 bxc6 9. Bxc4 Bf5 10. Nc3 e6 11. Ke2 Ne7 12. Be3 Nd5 13. Nxd5 exd5 (13... cxd5 14. Bb5+ {would be a terrible decision for Black.}) 14. Bb3 O-O-O 15. Bxa7 Re8 16. f4 f6 17. Ba4 Kb7 18. Bd4 fxe5 19. fxe5 Bd6 20. Rac1 Bd7 21. Rhf1 Bxe5 {Up to here, the game has been very well played, and it never swayed from being balanced. While White is not better yet, I definitely found the best move to put my opponent under pressure.} 22. Rf7 {This is the move White wants to make, invading the seventh rank. It looks ludicrous because Black will take on d4 , grabbing a full bishop with a discovered check. But we have to ask: What happens if I do it anyway?} Bxd4+ (22... Bf4+ $2 {This does not work.} 23. Kd3 Bxc1 24. Rxd7+ Kc8 25. Ra7 {Black is up an exchange, but his rooks are frozen due to skewer threats on the back rank. He may well lose all four of his pawns in the upcoming moves.}) 23. Kd3 {And now we see the point. Black has a lot of threats to deal with! Rxd7 +, Kxd4 , and Bxc6 . He is still okay, but a lot of accuracy is needed. My opponent managed to a point.} Kb6 24. Rxd7 Re3+ {Black restores the balance.} 25. Kxd4 Re4+ 26. Kd3 Rxa4 27. a3 Rg4 {My opponent offered a draw here. I decided to continue.} 28. g3 {Even here, my computer screams draw. I'm sure it is right objectively, but how easy is it for Black? White has an obvious idea of Rf1 - Rff7 and a more subtle threat that my opponent missed.} h5 {The first move after offering a draw, Black makes a big mistake.} (28... Re8 29. Rd6 Rc4 30. Rxc4 dxc4+ {was Black's path to the draw.}) 29. Rd6 Rc8 30. Rxd5 {White wins a pawn. Black might be able to hold here with extremely good defense, but it will not be easy at all, and my opponent did not manage to offer much resistance.} h4 (30... cxd5 31. Rxc8 h4 {was Black's best try.}) 31. Rd4 Rxd4+ 32. Kxd4 hxg3 33. hxg3 c5+ 34. Ke4 Kb5 35. Rc3 Rf8 36. Rf3 Re8+ 37. Kf5 Re2 38. Rb3+ {A smart check.} (38. b3 Ra2 39. a4+ Kb4) 38... Kc4 39. Rb7 g6+ 40. Kf4 (40. Kg5 Re3 41. g4 Rb3 {is not what White wants.}) 40... Kd5 41. g4 c4 42. Kg5 Re6 43. Rb5+ Kc6 44. a4 Re4 45. Rb8 Kc5 46. a5 Re6 47. Rb6 *

