8. e3 {Black has won a pawn for the moment. But White is hoping to take back on c4 next and claim a slight edge thanks to his more active pieces. How should Black proceed?} b5 {This is an extremely good decision from Topalov. He would absolutely love to hang on to the c4 -pawn, but it looks like the b5 -square is not sufficiently well-defended. But we ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} 9. axb5 cxb5 10. Bxf6 gxf6 {This is important. Black should not leave the queen offside.} (10... Qxf6 $2 11. Nxb5 $1 {Now White can safely take on b5 .} axb5 12. Rxa8 Bb4+ 13. Nd2 {Since the queen has left d8 for f6 , Black can't play ... Bb7 or ... Nc6 , accelerating development. He has to do something sad instead, and White should be winning with the extra material.}) 11. Nxb5 axb5 {Again, what if I do it anyway?} 12. Rxa8 Bb4+ 13. Ke2 (13. Nd2 {This looks more natural to me, but after} Bb7 14. Ra1 e5 $1 {, White's position still looks very suspect.}) 13... Bb7 {Black has a very powerful initiative, and White's king on e2 does not inspire confidence. Positions this dynamic are very hard to play, and Topalov surely was not perfect, but he did well at the most critical moments.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
