1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 a6 5. a4 e6 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bh4 dxc4 8. e3 {Black has won a pawn for the moment. But White is hoping to take back on c4 next and claim a slight edge thanks to his more active pieces. How should Black proceed?} b5 {This is an extremely good decision from Topalov. He would absolutely love to hang on to the c4 -pawn, but it looks like the b5 -square is not sufficiently well-defended. But we ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} 9. axb5 cxb5 10. Bxf6 gxf6 {This is important. Black should not leave the queen offside.} (10... Qxf6 $2 11. Nxb5 $1 {Now White can safely take on b5 .} axb5 12. Rxa8 Bb4+ 13. Nd2 {Since the queen has left d8 for f6 , Black can't play ... Bb7 or ... Nc6 , accelerating development. He has to do something sad instead, and White should be winning with the extra material.}) 11. Nxb5 axb5 {Again, what if I do it anyway?} 12. Rxa8 Bb4+ 13. Ke2 (13. Nd2 {This looks more natural to me, but after} Bb7 14. Ra1 e5 $1 {White's position still looks very suspect.}) 13... Bb7 {Black has a very powerful initiative, and White's king on e2 does not inspire confidence. Positions this dynamic are very hard to play, and Topalov surely was not perfect, but he did well at the most critical moments.} 14. Ra1 f5 15. Ne5 Rg8 (15... Nc6 {This was a bit stronger according to the almighty engine.}) 16. f4 Nc6 17. Nf3 Na5 18. Kf2 Nb3 19. Ra7 Be4 20. Ra2 {This was a mistake, and now Black has a fantastic idea.} (20. g3 $1 {I am unsure why van Wely did not play this move. He must have seen some issue, but I don't know what it was.}) 20... e5 {This is the move Black wants to make to blow up the center and open lines to White's king. It looks outrageous- the pawn has no defenders, and 3 white pieces can take it. But... What if I do it anyway? It turns out White has no good captures available.} 21. fxe5 ({The d -pawn can't capture because now the white queen is unprotected.} 21. Nxe5 Qh4+ $1 22. g3 Rxg3 $1 {Black is crashing through.} 23. hxg3 Qxh1 {and ... Qh2 will be mate next. White's only defense to the immediate mate threat does not save the game.} 24. Nf3 Bxf3 $1 25. Qxf3 Be1+ $1 26. Ke2 Nc1+ $1 {Black wins the queen and the game.}) 21... f4 {Of course, the initiative continues.} 22. Be2 (22. exf4 {doesn't help either. Once Black's queen enters the game from the d4 -square, White will not survive.} Bxf3 23. Qxf3 Qxd4+ 24. Qe3 Qd5 $1 {and White can't do anything about ... Bc5 coming next. Or gxf3 Bd2! when ... Qxd4 is coming next, and it's all over but the crying.}) 22... fxe3+ 23. Kxe3 Qd5 24. g3 Nxd4 {This was not the most precise.} 25. Nxd4 {But White lets him off the hook.} (25. Ra8+ $1 Qxa8 26. Qxd4 {White distracted the queen from the d5 -square, and he does not have to worry about ... Bc5 . Black is better, but the game goes on.}) 25... Bxh1 26. Bf3 Qxe5+ 27. Kf2 Bc5 28. Bxh1 Bxd4+ 29. Kf1 {The dust has settled, and Black is winning with his extra pawn and safer king.} Rg5 30. Bf3 Kf8 31. Kg2 Qe3 32. Kh3 Kg7 33. b3 cxb3 34. Ra3 b4 35. Rxb3 Bc3 36. Qe2 Qc5 37. Qd3 Qc8+ 38. Kg2 Ra5 39. Qc2 Qe6 40. Qb1 Ra1 41. Qc2 Bd4 42. Bd1 (42. Rb1 {is no solution due to} Ra2 {.}) 42... Qe1 43. Bf3 Qf1# *

