[PlyCount "8"]

{Black's king is cut off from entering the position, and White should hold easily. If Black tries Bc3 to bring the king forward, Nf3 solves the problem.} 38... Nc3 {Up to this moment, I had defended well. Here, White has a big decision to make - do we use the Shankland Method or not?} 39. Bb1 ({Simple was prudent here. White stops the pawn and should be fine.} 39. Kxb2 $2 {Obviously, this is the move White would really like to make. It seems like it loses a piece, but I asked what would happen if I did it anyway. I came to the wrong conclusion, but fortunately, my opponent let me off the hook.} Ne2+ 40. Kc2 Nxg1 41. Bf7 $1 {White goes after the pawn chain.} g5 $1 {Without this one, White would make a draw immediately.} 42. fxg5 {When I took on b2 , I saw the position to here and thought I would make a draw with a Dvoretsky-style fortress. My king goes to g2 , and that is that. Once the position was on the board, I realized I had missed something.} Nh3 $2 {This move came quickly, and I breathed a big sigh of relief. White is now making a draw. Instead, Nf3-+ would have won. White's pawn is forced off of the h2 -square, and he will not be able to set up a fortress. h4 Be5-+ .}) 39... Nxb1 40. Kxb1 Kd5 41. Nf3 Bf6 42. Ng5 *

