1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. f3 c5 4. d5 d6 5. e4 e6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Bg5 h6 8. Be3 exd5 9. cxd5 O-O 10. Qd2 a6 11. a4 Re8 12. Nge2 Nbd7 {Paying no attention to the h6 -pawn, since it's not really threatened.} (12... Kh7 {is too slow and gives White time to play} 13. Ng3 {.}) 13. Bxh6 {White's last move was a mistake. I had seen what was coming but misevaluated the resulting position. How should Black continue?} Nxe4 {What happens if I do it anyway? Black pitches the knight to make room for his queen to reach h4 .} 14. Nxe4 Qh4+ 15. g3 Qxh6 16. Qxh6 Bxh6 17. Nxd6 Re5 {Black is absolutely fine in this endgame, and in fact, his position is even a bit more pleasant. I defended well to a point, but I allowed my opponent a big chance right before time control.} 18. f4 Rxd5 19. Nxc8 Rxc8 20. Bg2 Rd6 21. O-O c4 22. Rfd1 Rxd1+ 23. Rxd1 Nc5 24. Rd4 b5 (24... Nxa4 {allows} 25. Bxb7 {.}) 25. axb5 axb5 26. Rd6 Bg7 27. Bd5 Kf8 28. Rc6 Rxc6 29. Bxc6 b4 30. Bd5 Bxb2 31. Bxc4 b3 32. Kf2 Bg7 33. Ke3 Ke7 34. Kd2 Ne4+ 35. Kd3 b2 36. Ba2 f5 37. Kc2 Kd6 38. Ng1 Nc3 {Up to this moment, I had defended well. Here, White has a big decision to make - do we use the Shankland Method or not?} 39. Kxb2 {Obviously, this is the move White would really like to make. It seems like it loses a piece, but I asked what would happen if I did it anyway. I came to the wrong conclusion, but fortunately, my opponent let me off the hook.} (39. Bb1 $1 {Simple was prudent here. White stops the pawn and should be fine. For example, after} Nxb1 40. Kxb1 Kd5 41. Nf3 Bf6 42. Ng5 $1 {Black's king is cut off from entering the position, and White should hold easily. If Black tries 42... Bc3 to bring the king forward, 43.Nf3 solves the problem.}) 39... Ne2+ 40. Kc2 Nxg1 41. Bf7 {White goes after the pawn chain.} g5 {Without this one, White would make a draw immediately.} 42. fxg5 {When I took on b2 , I saw the position to here and thought I would make a draw with a Dvoretsky-style fortress. My king goes to g2 , and that is that. Once the position was on the board, I realized I had missed something.} Nh3 {This move came quickly, and I breathed a big sigh of relief. White is now making a draw.} (42... Nf3 {This would have won. White's pawn is forced off of the h2 -square, and he will not be able to set up a fortress.} 43. h4 Be5 {and there's no fortress.}) 43. g6 Ke5 44. Kd3 Kf6 45. Ke2 Bf8 46. Be8 Bd6 47. Kf3 Ng5+ 48. Kg2 {Now it is a very easy draw. There is no way Black will ever be able to challenge the base of White's pawn chain. My opponent tried for a while, but predictably without success.} Be5 49. Bb5 (49. h4 $2 {Had my pawn not been able to remain on h2 , the position would be immediately lost.} Ne4 {.}) 49... Kxg6 50. Bd3 Kf6 51. Bc2 Bd6 52. Bd3 Nf7 53. Bc2 Ne5 54. Bb1 Kg5 55. Bc2 Bc7 56. Bb1 Ng4 57. Bd3 Bd6 58. Be2 Bc5 59. Bd3 Kf6 60. Be2 Ne3+ 61. Kf3 Nc2 62. Ba6 Ke5 63. Bb7 Nd4+ 64. Kg2 Ne6 65. Bc8 Ng5 66. Ba6 Ke4 67. Bb7+ Ke3 68. Bc8 Ke4 69. Bb7+ Ke5 70. Ba6 Ba7 71. Bc8 Ne4 72. Kf3 Nd2+ 73. Kg2 Nb3 74. Bb7 Nd2 75. Bc8 Nc4 76. Kf3 Nb6 77. Ba6 Nd7 78. Bd3 Nf8 79. Bc2 Ne6 80. Bd3 Nd4+ 81. Kg2 Kd5 82. Kf2 Bb8 83. Ke3 Kc5 84. Ba6 Bd6 85. Bd3 Kd5 86. Kf2 Ba3 87. Ke3 Bc1+ 88. Kf2 Kc5 89. Ba6 Kb4 90. Bd3 Kc3 91. Ba6 Bh6 92. Bc8 Kd2 93. Bd7 Be3+ 94. Kf1 Bg5 95. Kf2 Be7 96. Bc8 Bd6 97. Bd7 Be5 98. Bc8 f4 99. gxf4 *

