[White "Level 5"]
[Black "Shankland-Saric Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "4rrk1/1bpn2pp/1p1pp2q/p4pN1/2PPPP2/P2B4/1P2Q1PP/4RRK1 b - - 0 21"]
[PlyCount "10"]

21... Qg6 {What should White do here?} 22. d5 {This uses the Shankland Method perfectly. White obviously would love to push d5 to gain the e6 -square for his knight, but it looks like it runs into a problematic pin on the e -file following ... exd5 . But... well, you know what to ask.} exd5 {While this was not played, it has to be considered the critical line.} (22... Nf6 23. exf5 exf5 24. Ne6 {The knight lands on e6 , and Black will not survive.}) (22... Nc5 {This one is also possible, but White is in good shape if he calculates well.} 23. exf5 $1 ({(} 23. Nxe6 {fails to} Rxe6 24. dxe6 Nxe4 {)}) 23... exf5 24. Bxf5 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway? The tactics favor White.  A)} Rxe2 25. Bxg6 Rxe1 26. Bxh7+ $1 {This in-between check leaves White two healthy pawns up.  B) Qxf5 Qxe8! h6 Qe7 hxg5 Without this move, Black will remain down a pawn and an exchange, but taking the knight will not save him either. fxg5 Qc8 g6 Black is mated.}) 23. exf5 Rxe2 24. fxg6 Rxb2 {This position is not actually that clear at first glance, but White can end the game immediately with good tactical vision.} 25. gxh7+ Kh8 26. Re8 {Well spotted! White wins.} *

