1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Bb4+ 4. Bd2 Qe7 5. Nc3 b6 6. e3 O-O 7. Bd3 Bb7 8. O-O ({This is more accurate than} 8. a3 Bxc3 9. Bxc3 Ne4 {.}) 8... a5 9. a3 Bxc3 10. Bxc3 Ne4 11. Be1 f5 12. Nd2 Ng5 13. f4 Nf7 14. e4 Nh6 15. Qe2 d6 16. Nf3 Nd7 {I got a very pleasant position straight out of the opening, but at this point, I missed the cleanest way through. Can you do better?} 17. Bh4 {Not bad, but definitely not best either.} (17. exf5 $1 {was a crusher. It is definitely the move White wants to play, tearing open the e -file while Black is forced to take back with a piece on f5 and leave himself with a weakness on e6 .} Nxf5 {But what now? The immediate} 18. Ng5 {is messy. g4! is the move White wants to make. Kick the knight, bully it back to h6 , and then Ng5 comes to win the house. It looks like it hangs the d4 -pawn. But... what if I do it anyway?} Nh6 {Ng5 is crushing. Bxf3} 19. Qf3 {Nh6 should be preferred, but after Bd2 with Rae1 to follow, Black is quite busted all the same. Therefore, Nxd4 comes, and I somehow missed Qe4+- in my calculations. White wins a piece with the twin threats on d4 and h7 .}) 17... Qe8 18. Rae1 Qh5 19. Bg5 Rae8 20. Bxh6 Qxh6 21. Ng5 {My play in the last few moves was quite lackluster, but when I got another chance, I jumped at it.} Qg6 {White should White do here?} 22. d5 {This uses the Shankland Method perfectly. White obviously would love to push d5 to gain the e6 -square for his knight, but it looks like it runs into a problematic pin on the e -file following ... exd5 . But... well, you know what to ask.} Nf6 (22... exd5 {While this was not played, it has to be considered the critical line.} 23. exf5 $1 Rxe2 24. fxg6 Rxb2 {This position is not actually that clear at first glance, but White can end the game immediately with good tactical vision.} 25. gxh7+ $1 Kh8 26. Re8 $1 {Well spotted! White wins.}) (22... Nc5 {This one is also possible, but White is in good shape if he calculates well.} 23. exf5 $1 ({(} 23. Nxe6 {fails to} Rxe6 24. dxe6 Nxe4 {)}) 23... exf5 24. Bxf5 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway? The tactics favor White.  A)} Rxe2 25. Bxg6 Rxe1 26. Bxh7+ $1 {This in-between check leaves White two healthy pawns up.  B) Qxf5 Qxe8! h6 Qe7 hxg5 Without this move, Black will remain down a pawn and an exchange, but taking the knight will not save him either. fxg5 Qc8 g6 Black is mated.}) 23. exf5 exf5 24. Ne6 {The knight lands on e6 , and Black will not survive.} Rf7 25. Qc2 Ng4 (25... Ne4 26. Rxe4 {Even better than trading!} fxe4 27. Bxe4 Qh6 28. Rf3) 26. h3 Nh6 27. Nd4 Rxe1 (27... Rff8 {to contest the e -file won't work due to the weakness of f5 .}) 28. Rxe1 Rf8 29. Qf2 {Prophylaxis.} Bc8 (29... Re8 30. Rxe8+ Qxe8 31. Nxf5 {and the black queen has no entry points.}) 30. Nb5 Qf7 31. Qh4 Re8 32. Nxc7 {Not the only winning move, but certainly the cleanest. It's a very simple case, but it is the Shankland Method all the same. The pawn was defended, but what happens if I take it anyway?} Rxe1+ (32... Qxc7 33. Rxe8+) 33. Qxe1 {The knight is immune thanks to the mate on e8 .} Bd7 (33... Qxc7 34. Qe8#) 34. Qe3 g5 35. fxg5 f4 36. Qd4 Bf5 37. gxh6 Bxd3 38. Qxd3 Qxc7 39. Qd4 Qf7 40. Kf2 ({Preparing the endgame. Ivan resigned here. This is cleaner than the immediate} 40. Qg7+ Qxg7 41. hxg7 Kxg7 42. Kf2 Kf6 43. Kf3 Kf5 {where there's still some work to do.}) *

