1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 9. Nc3 h6 10. h3 (10. Rd1+ {This would be the punishment, immediately forcing Black into a ... Ke8 plan. Whenever Black plays with the king on e8 , he would prefer to have his pawn on h5 instead of h6 . I cover this endgame in much more detail in my Berlin LTR here on Chessable.} Ke8 11. h3 {is the most principled way for White.}) 10... Bd7 11. b3 Ke8 12. Bb2 Rd8 13. Rad1 Ne7 14. Rfe1 Ng6 15. Ne4 Nf4 {Kasparov's inability to break down Kramnik's Berlin led to his defeat in the 2000 World Championship match. Now, 6 months later, he managed to do so at a less opportune moment.} *

