15... Nf4 {How did Kasparov break down Kramnik's Berlin 6 months after their 2000 World Championship match?} 16. e6 {This is absolutely the move White wants to play to open up his b2 -bishop and e1 -rook while Black is underdeveloped. It hangs the pawn in 3 different ways, but we should always ask, what happens if I do it anyway? We already saw this idea in the game Charbonneau-Yedidia, in Level 1.} Nxe6 (16... fxe6 17. Be5 {White takes on c7 , equalizing material, and has a crushing advantage thanks to the obvious difference in piece activity.}) (16... Bxe6 {loses to} 17. Nf6+ gxf6 18. Rxd8+ Kxd8 19. Bxf6+ {and the h8 -rook drops. Or Ke7 Ba3+ Rd6 Ne4 winning the exchange.}) 17. Be5 {Modern machines point out that this move was even stronger, aiming for Bxc7 next.} (17. Nd4 c5 {According to the all-seeing engine, Rh7 hangs on. Kramnik found the idea, but a move too late.}) 17... Be7 (17... Rc8 18. Nh4 $1 {and f5 will be decisive next.}) (17... a6 18. Bxc7 Nxc7 19. Nd6#) 18. Bxc7 {What happens if I do it anyway?} Nxc7 19. Nd6+ Kf8 20. Nxb7 {White takes the house.} *

