[Black "Garry Kasparov-Vladimir Kramn, Astana 2001"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "C67"]
[PlyCount "81"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 9. Nc3 h6 10. h3 (10. Rd1+ {This would be the punishment, immediately forcing Black into a ... Ke8 plan. Whenever Black plays with the king on e8 , he would prefer to have his pawn on h5 instead of h6 . I cover this endgame in much more detail in my Berlin LTR here on Chessable.} Ke8 11. h3 {is the most principled way for White.}) 10... Bd7 11. b3 Ke8 12. Bb2 Rd8 13. Rad1 Ne7 14. Rfe1 Ng6 15. Ne4 Nf4 {Kasparov's inability to break down Kramnik's Berlin led to his defeat in the 2000 World Championship match. Now 6 months later, he managed to do so at a less opportune moment.} 16. e6 {This is absolutely the move White wants to play to open up his b2 -bishop and e1 -rook while Black is underdeveloped. It hangs the pawn in 3 different ways, but we should always ask, what happens if I do it anyway? We already saw this idea in the game Charbonneau-Yedidia, in Level 1.} Nxe6 (16... fxe6 17. Be5 {White takes on c7 , equalizing material, and has a crushing advantage thanks to the obvious difference in piece activity.}) (16... Bxe6 {loses to} 17. Nf6+ gxf6 18. Rxd8+ Kxd8 19. Bxf6+ {and the h8 -rook drops. Or Ke7 Ba3+ Rd6 Ne4 winning the exchange.}) 17. Nd4 (17. Be5 $1 {Modern machines point out that this move was even stronger, aiming for Bxc7 next.  A)} Rc8 18. Nh4 $1 {and f5 will be decisive next.  B)} a6 19. Bxc7 Nxc7 20. Nd6+) ({C) Be7 Bxc7! What happens if I do it anyway? Nxc7} 17. Nd6+ {Kf8 Nxb7 White takes the house.}) 17... c5 ({According to the all-seeing engine,} 17... Rh7 {hangs on. Kramnik found the idea, but a move too late.}) 18. Nf5 Rh7 19. Bf6 {Well spotted.} Rc8 (19... gxf6 20. Nxf6#) 20. Bxg7 ({There was no need to release the tension right away. The machine suggests} 20. f4 $1 {and claims Black should lose. Still, I shouldn't criticize Garry's decision too much since it quickly simplified the position to a very pleasant Berlin Endgame for White.} g6 21. Bh4 {and Black can't move.}) 20... Bxg7 21. Nxg7+ Rxg7 22. Nf6+ Ke7 23. Nxd7 Rd8 24. Ne5 {Black definitely still has defensive chances here, but White got what he wanted. Black's bishop pair is gone, the e5 -pawn has been exchanged, Black has some kingside weaknesses, and White can hope to win with his superior structure.} Rxd1 25. Rxd1 Nf4 26. Kh1 {Well spotted. The g2 -pawn cannot be taken.} (26. g3 Nxh3+) 26... Rg5 (26... Nxg2 27. Rg1) (26... Rxg2 27. Nd3) 27. Ng4 {The machine claims this is still close to equal, but even the man who put the Berlin on the map was unable to hang on.} Rd5 28. Re1+ Kf8 29. Nxh6 Rd2 30. Re5 Rxf2 ({Kramnik missed the amazing resource} 30... Rxc2 $1 31. Rf5 Rxf2 32. Kg1 Rxg2+ 33. Kf1 Rg6 34. Nxf7 Nh5 $1 {Black can hang on by a thread if he finds this very difficult sequence.} 35. Ne5+ Rf6 36. Nd7+ Ke7 37. Nxf6 Ng3+ {.}) 31. Rf5 Kg7 32. Ng4 {Now it's all over.} Rxg2 (32... Rf1+ 33. Kh2) 33. Rxf4 Rxc2 34. Rf2 Rc3 35. Kg2 b5 36. h4 c4 37. h5 cxb3 38. axb3 Rc5 (38... Rxb3 39. h6+) 39. h6+ Kf8 40. Nf6 Rg5+ 41. Kh1 {Garry must have felt some redemption with this win.} *

