[White "Level 5"]
[Black "Carlsen-Gelfand Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r5k1/1p3ppp/2b1p3/7Q/1PPR4/2N5/2q2PPP/5BK1 b - - 0 27"]
[PlyCount "4"]

27... Qxc3 {It's easy to get this far in your calculation and then stop thinking that Black is fine. But... always look for candidate moves.} 28. Qa5 {Well spotted. Without this one, Black would be at least equal.} (28. Qd1 $2 {This has the same idea as the best move, aiming to abuse the d8 -square. But Black can use the Shankland Method.} Qxb4 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway? It turns out a rook coming to d8 is not checkmate.} 29. Rd8+ Rxd8 30. Qxd8+ Qf8 {and Black should win with his extra pawn.}) (28. Rh4 $2 {is an instructive mistake that highlights the usefulness of evaluating the size of threats. Menacing as White's position may seem, Qxh7 + is really just a pawn! After} Ra1 $1 29. Qxh7+ Kf8 {, the tables have turned, and White needs to be extremely accurate just to stay alive.}) 28... Rf8 29. Qb6 {White has consolidated the position, has advanced his queenside, is much more active, and b5 is coming. Magnus went on to win, although he did allow Black some defensive chances at some point.} *

