{This is probably my favorite Carlsen game of all time. And there are a ton that I really like!} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 e6 4. O-O Nge7 5. Re1 a6 6. Bf1 d5 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. d4 Nf6 9. Be3 cxd4 10. Nxd4 Bd7 11. c4 Nxd4 12. Bxd4 Bc6 13. Nc3 Be7 14. a3 a5 15. Qd3 O-O 16. Rad1 Qc7 17. Be5 Qb6 18. Qg3 Rfd8 19. Rxd8+ Qxd8 20. Rd1 Qb6 21. Bd4 Qb3 22. Rd3 Qc2 {Up to here, Magnus has matched modern engines for every single move. This is quite a feat since the engines at the time had other opinions! Here, White has a big chance, but finding the best move is twofold. It is figuring out what White wants and then using the Shankland Method to make it happen.} 23. b4 {This move can only be found with a deep understanding combined with strong calculation. At first glance, it might look like White's plan should be to attack Black's king, but this is not the case. The queen and bishop are well placed, but no other pieces can join. It comes down to understanding why White is better. The only source of his advantage is that his pieces are more active. The reason Black is not collapsing on the spot is that he has no weaknesses. There is nothing undefended in his position. When we realize that as soon as a weakness is made, his position collapses, b4 becomes easier to understand. White wants to push b4 - b5 , kicking the bishop off of c6 , and then he wins easily with a queen invasion to c7 . But b4 does not come easily! It puts the pawn on a hanging square and opens the a -file for Black's rook, which could be quite scary when it lands on a1 . But we must ask: What if I do it anyway? Magnus calculated well.} axb4 ({If Black plays something dumb, he'll be crushed. E.g.:} 23... h6 24. b5 Be8 25. Qc7 {.}) 24. axb4 Nh5 {The most testing move. Black hits White's queen, and he could potentially take on b4 next if White is not careful.} (24... Ra1 $2 {It's not actually that easy for a strong player to see White's winning move here because it is so positionally wrong, but it does win on the spot.} 25. Bxf6 $1 {White removes the knight, which was controlling e8 , and drags the e7 -bishop away from the f8 -square. Black is mated on the back rank.} Bxf6 26. Qb8+ {.}) (24... Bxb4 $2 25. Bxf6 {This much is easy to see.}) 25. Qe5 {Now, Black has two interesting tries here. One is ... Ra1 , and the other is ... Bf6 . White needs to be ready for both before pulling the trigger on b4 .} ({Nf6 is simply met by .} 25. Qc7 $2 Bxb4 $1 {Oops.}) (25. Qg4 {and Black could repeat with} Nf6 {, but why? Ra1} 26. Rg3 {Rxf1+ Kxf1 Nxg3+ and} Qc1 {seems to be even stronger.}) 25... Bf6 (25... Ra1 {This looks incredibly scary. Black invades the back rank and threatens to win a clean rook on d3 . White is good to go here, but only with some creative ideas.  A)} 26. Rg3 $2 {is tempting since the rook cannot be taken, but it does lose immediately.} Rxf1+ $1 27. Kxf1 Qc1+ $1 28. Ke2 Nf4+ {White loses the queen, or Qe1 Nxg3+ fxg3 Bxg2+! and it's all over.  B) White can start with Nd1 or insert Qb8+! Bf8 Now, in order to win, White needs to properly evaluate the size of threats. Bc5 is obviously a big idea, but Black can stop mate with ... h6 . So, Bc5 will only win a piece and potentially a pawn on f7 . In the meantime, Black is threatening to take on f1 followed by taking on d3 with check, which wins more than a piece. Rd1 works too, but I find Nd1! more forcing. White pitches 3 points of material to stop all threats along the back rank. Now, the rook is hanging on a1 as well. Rxd1 Rxd1 Qxd1 White's king is safe as can be, and now he executes his main idea. Bc5! h6 Qxf8+ Kh7 Now, be careful... Qd6+- White should win with a healthy bishop pair and his outside pawn mass. Don't fall for Qxf7? Be4! Oops! ... Bd3 incoming.}) (25... Bc5 $2 {This is too blunt. Rxf1+ Kxf1 Qxd3+ Kg1 h6 Qxf8+ Kh7-+ Both sides executed their threat and won a piece. But now Black ends up on top because the c3 -knight will fall.}) 26. Qxh5 Bxd4 27. Rxd4 Qxc3 {It's easy to get this far in your calculation and then stop, thinking that Black is fine. But... always look for candidate moves.} 28. Qa5 {Well spotted. Without this one, Black would be at least equal.} (28. Qd1 $2 {This has the same idea as the best move, aiming to abuse the d8 -square. But Black can use the Shankland Method.} Qxb4 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway? It turns out a rook coming to d8 is not checkmate.} 29. Rd8+ Rxd8 30. Qxd8+ Qf8 {and Black should win with his extra pawn.}) (28. Rh4 $2 {This is an instructive mistake that highlights the usefulness of evaluating the size of threats. Menacing as White's position may seem, Qxh7 + is really just a pawn!} Ra1 $1 29. Qxh7+ Kf8 {The tables have turned, and White needs to be extremely accurate just to stay alive.}) 28... Rf8 29. Qb6 {White has consolidated the position, has advanced his queenside, is much more active, and b5 is coming. Magnus went on to win, although he did allow Black some defensive chances at some point.} e5 30. Rd1 g6 31. b5 Be4 32. Qf6 h5 33. h4 (33. Rd7 {This was the way. The threats on the back rank are not a big deal.}) 33... Bf5 {Black stops the rook from reaching d7 , and the bishop will be very good on e6 .} 34. Rd5 Qc1 35. Qxe5 Be6 36. Rd4 Ra8 {Despite Magnus's hiccup and Gelfand's good defense, Black is still a pawn down and a long way from saving the game.} 37. Qe2 Kh7 {One innocent move and it's all over.} (37... Qc3 $1 38. Rd1 Rc8 $1 {This is not easy at all for a human to find.} 39. Qe4 Bxc4 40. Qxb7 Bxf1 41. Rxf1 Rc5 $1 {The machine claims zeroes here. But, even if Black gets this far, in human practice, he still has a lot of work to do.}) 38. Rd1 Qc3 39. Qe4 {Magnus is winning again.} Ra1 40. Rxa1 Qxa1 41. c5 Qc3 42. Qxb7 Qe1 43. b6 Bc4 44. Qf3 Qxf1+ 45. Kh2 Qb1 46. b7 Qb5 47. c6 Bd5 48. Qg3 *

