{Now, while there was nothing wrong with Nf4 , like Karjakin played, he could have found another move using the Shankland Method.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 5. c3 O-O 6. Bg5 {This move was my brainchild when I was helping Magnus for the 2016 match. I knew full well that Black could equalize in more ways than one, but my hope was that it would let White reach a playable position that is a little more unbalanced than what you normally get in the Berlin.} h6 7. Bh4 Be7 8. O-O d6 9. Nbd2 Nh5 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. Nc4 *

