[PlyCount "6"]

11. Nc4 {Now, while there was nothing wrong with 11...Nf4 , as Karjakin played, he could have found another move using the Shankland Method.} f5 {At the time, I remembered thinking that only this move would equalize for Black. Modern engines claim more or less anything is fine, but this one definitely is the cleanest. It looks like it is asking for trouble after Nfxe5 , but if Black has nerves of steel and asks what would happen if he does it anyway, the answer is nothing bad and that he easily equalizes.} 12. Nfxe5 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 (13. Qxh5 Nxd3) 13... Nf4 {And it transpires that White's aggressive lunges have left him dead lost in the face of an incoming attack.} (13... Qxe5 $2 {is tempting but poor.} 14. Bc4+ $1 Be6 15. Bxe6+ Qxe6 16. Qxh5 {Black is almost equalizing, but still suffering a little.}) *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 5"]
[Black "Carlsen-Karjakin Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r4rk1/1p4p1/2p1pqnp/p2pp3/P3P3/2PPN1Pn/1P1NQP1P/R4R1K w - - 0 21"]
[PlyCount "14"]

21. Qh5 {Black was a bit optimistic when he compromised his pawn structure, and he will face some definite long-term challenges. Here, ... Nxf2 is obviously the move he would like to play, but it looks impossible due to the pin on the f -file.} Nxf2+ {Had Karjakin successfully evaluated what would happen if he did it anyway, he would have made an immediate draw and remained a point ahead with 2 games to go. Of course, there is no guarantee that he would have won the match, but he would have been a favorite.} (21... Ng5 $2 22. h4 {Now Black is left with a lousy structure for no compensation. Eventually, Magnus ground out a win, leveling the score and putting him back in good position to defend his title.}) 22. Kg2 {Now, it looks like Black is in bad shape, with Ng4 coming next. However, with a little creativity, it can be found that Black could take on f2 anyway.} (22. Kg1 $2 Qg5 $1 {This is easy enough, and I'm sure Karjakin saw it.} 23. Qxg5 Nh3+ 24. Kg2 Nxg5 {Black is up a not so glorious pawn, but a pawn all the same.}) 22... Qf7 {An extremely important move, without which Black would be in big trouble. The threat of ... Nf4 + is annoying to meet.} 23. Kg1 Qf6 {And now back! It turns out White does not have a great way to stop the threat of ... Qg5 apart from returning with his king to g2 , repeating the position.} 24. Kg2 (24. Ng4 Qg5 25. Qxg5 Nh3+) 24... Qf7 25. Qe2 Nh4+ {This detail is also important to notice. The knight is immune from capture, and then Black gives a perpetual.} 26. Kg1 (26. gxh4 $2 Qg6+ {White has to pitch a piece back to avoid mate, and he is dead lost.}) 26... Nh3+ 27. Kh1 Nf2+ {Black makes a draw.} *

