[PlyCount "149"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 5. c3 O-O 6. Bg5 {This move was my brainchild when I was helping Magnus for the 2016 match. I knew full well that Black could equalize in more ways than one, but my hope was that it would let White reach a playable position that is a little more unbalanced than what you normally get in the Berlin.} h6 7. Bh4 Be7 8. O-O d6 9. Nbd2 Nh5 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. Nc4 {Now, while there was nothing wrong with Nf4 like Karjakin played, he could have found another move using the Shankland Method.} Nf4 (11... f5 $1 {At the time, I remembered thinking that only this move would equalize for Black. Modern engines claim more or less anything is fine, but this one definitely is the cleanest. It looks like it is asking for trouble after Nfxe5 , but if Black has nerves of steel and asks what would happen if he does it anyway, the answer is nothing bad and that he easily equalizes.} 12. Nfxe5 Nxe5 $1 13. Nxe5 $2 ({(White should bail out with} 13. Qxh5 Nxd3 {)}) 13... Nf4 $1 {and it transpires that White's aggressive lunges have left him dead lost in the face of an incoming attack. Instead, Qxe5? is tempting but poor.} 14. Bc4+ $1 Be6 15. Bxe6+ Qxe6 16. Qh5 {Black is almost equalizing, but still suffering a little.}) 12. Ne3 Qf6 13. g3 Nh3+ 14. Kh1 Ne7 {Nothing too notable happened in the upcoming moves until a proper use of the Shankland Method could have changed chess history.} 15. Bc4 c6 16. Bb3 Ng6 17. Qe2 a5 18. a4 Be6 19. Bxe6 fxe6 20. Nd2 d5 21. Qh5 {Black was a bit optimistic when he compromised his pawn structure, and he will face some definite long-term challenges. Here, ... Nxf2 is obviously the move he would like to play, but it looks impossible due to the pin on the f -file.} Ng5 (21... Nxf2+ $1 {Had Karjakin successfully evaluated what would happen if he did it anyway, he would have made an immediate draw and remained a point ahead with 2 games to go. Of course, there is no guarantee that he would have won the match, but he would have been a favorite.} 22. Kg1 $2 Qg5 $1 {is easy enough, and I'm sure Karjakin saw it.} 23. Qxg5 Nh3+ 24. Kg2 Nxg5 {Black is up a not so glorious pawn, but a pawn all the same. After Kg2 , it looks like Black is in bad shape, with ... Ng4 coming next. However, with a little creativity, it can be found that Black could take on f2 anyway. Qf7! An extremely important move, without which Black would be in big trouble. The threat of ... Nf4 + is annoying to meet.} 25. Kg1 {Qf6! And now back! It turns out White does not have a great way to stop the threat of ... Qg5 apart from returning with his king to g2 , repeating the position, as Ng4 run into Qg5 Qxg5} Nh3+ {. Therefore, White has to go} 26. Kg2 {Qf7 Qe2} Nh4+ $1 {This detail is also important to notice. The knight is immune from capture, and then Black gives a perpetual. If} 27. gxh4 $2 {Qg6+-+ , White has to pitch a piece back to avoid mate, and he is dead lost. Instead, Kg1 Nh3+! Kh1} Nf2 {and Black makes a draw.}) 22. h4 {Now Black is left with a lousy structure for no compensation. Eventually, Magnus ground out a win, leveling the score and putting him back in good position to defend his title.} Nf3 23. Nxf3 Qxf3+ 24. Qxf3 Rxf3 25. Kg2 Rf7 26. Rfe1 h5 27. Nf1 Kf8 28. Nd2 Ke7 29. Re2 Kd6 30. Nf3 Raf8 31. Ng5 Re7 32. Rae1 Rfe8 33. Nf3 Nh8 34. d4 exd4 35. Nxd4 g6 36. Re3 Nf7 37. e5+ Kd7 38. Rf3 Nh6 39. Rf6 Rg7 40. b4 axb4 41. cxb4 Ng8 42. Rf3 Nh6 43. a5 Nf5 44. Nb3 Kc7 45. Nc5 Kb8 46. Rb1 Ka7 47. Rd3 Rc7 48. Ra3 Nd4 49. Rd1 Nf5 50. Kh3 Nh6 51. f3 Rf7 52. Rd4 Nf5 53. Rd2 Rh7 54. Rb3 Ree7 55. Rdd3 Rh8 56. Rb1 Rhh7 57. b5 cxb5 58. Rxb5 d4 59. Rb6 Rc7 60. Nxe6 Rc3 61. Nf4 Rhc7 62. Nd5 Rxd3 63. Nxc7 Kb8 64. Nb5 Kc8 65. Rxg6 Rxf3 66. Kg2 ({The immediate} 66. Nd6+ {fails due to} Nxd6 67. Rxd6 Re3 68. Rxd4 Rxe5 {The problem for White is that the kingside structure is not winning, and Black has a good rook.}) ({White would like to play} 66. e6 {but} d3 67. Kg2 {Kc7 Rd7+ Kc6 Kf2 Rxe6 Rxd3 Re5 and again, Black gets an active rook.}) 66... Rb3 67. Nd6+ Nxd6 68. Rxd6 Re3 (68... d3 69. e6) 69. e6 Kc7 (69... d3 70. Kf2 {This comes with tempo.} Re2+ 71. Kf3 Kc7 72. Rxd3 Rxe6 73. Rd5 {Now White's rook is better than Black's, and White wins.}) 70. Rxd4 Rxe6 71. Rd5 Rh6 72. Kf3 Kb8 73. Kf4 Ka7 74. Kg5 Rh8 75. Kf6 *

