[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r2qr1k1/2p1bp2/p4np1/1p2PQ1p/1n4P1/5N1P/PP1N1P2/R1B1R1K1 w - - 0 19"]
[PlyCount "8"]

19. Qb1 {Now, Svidler's move was not horrible, but he missed a big chance. What should Black play?} hxg4 {This is clearly the move Black wants to make. It looks like exf6 in response is a big problem. But we know what to ask.} (19... Bc5 $2 {This allows White back into the game.} 20. Ne4 $1 {Well spotted by Dominguez. He eventually lost anyway, but here, White should not be worse. exf6 would be wrong due to} hxg4 {.}) 20. exf6 Bc5 {Without this move, Black would be losing material and probably the game. As is, White is collapsing. The rook on e1 is hit, the defending knight is kicked, and hxg4 does not work.} 21. hxg4 (21. Rxe8+ Qxe8 22. hxg4 Qe2 $1 {This is an important move, but also a very obvious one. White will be crushed.} 23. Qe4 Qxf2+ 24. Kh1 Rd8 25. Qe1 Rxd2 {and nothing works:} 26. Bxd2 Qxf3+ {or Nxd2 Qxe1+ are curtains, and after Qxf2 Rxf2 or Qe8+ Bf8 Nxd2 Nc2 Rb1 Ne3 , Black is crushing. E.g.: Qe4 Qe1+} 27. Kh2 Bd6+ {Kh3} 28. Qg3) 21... Qd7 {Some combination of ... Nd3 and/or ... Qxg4 + should end the game in short order. It is important to realize that White cannot hope to defend the g4 -pawn with Re4 because the rook can be destabilized.} 22. Re4 Nd3 {The queen is cut off from the defense of the rook, and White is unable to hold the g4 -pawn. He will lose.} *

