1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. d3 d6 7. c3 O-O 8. Nbd2 Re8 9. d4 b5 10. Bc2 exd4 11. cxd4 Bg4 12. h3 Bh5 13. g4 {Black has an important choice to make. Do we sacrifice on g4 or retreat to g6 ?} Bg6 {This was not terrible, but it was better to go for it.} (13... Nxg4 $1 14. hxg4 Bxg4 15. Qe1 {It's easy to get this far in your calculations and stop. White breaks the pin; he is ready for Qe3 next. He is a piece up, and it is hard to imagine the attack succeeding. But... always ask, what happens if I do it anyway!} Bg5 $1 {An important move, without which Black would be worse. Qe3 is prevented, and the attack rages on.} 16. Nxg5 {What else? White could barely move.} Nxd4 $1 {White is in trouble.}) {The machine prefers Nb3 , which looks weird to me, and Black has good compensation. I'm sure that Svidler was not worried about it, and concerned only with Qe1 , breaking the pin.} 14. Re1 {This is not a subtle move. White wants e4 - e5 . How should Black respond?} h5 {This is clearly the move Black wants to make to undermine White's kingside. It looks like ignoring e5 is asking for trouble, but... What happens if I do it anyway?} 15. e5 (15. g5 Nh7 16. h4 d5 17. e5 Nf8 {leads White nowhere.}) 15... Bxc2 16. Qxc2 Nb4 {Black saves the hanging knight with a gain of tempo; he will not lose material, and White is becoming overextended.} 17. Qf5 dxe5 18. dxe5 g6 19. Qb1 {Now, Svidler's move was not horrible, but he missed a big chance. What should Black play?} Bc5 {This allows White back into the game.} (19... hxg4 $1 {This is clearly the move Black wants to make. It looks like e5xf6 in response is a big problem. But we know what to ask.} 20. exf6 Bc5 $1 {Without this move, Black would be losing material and probably the game. As is, White is collapsing. The rook on e1 is hit, the defending knight is kicked, and h3xg4 does not work.  A)} 21. hxg4 Qd7 $1 {Some combination of ... Nd3 and/or ... Qxg4 + should end the game in short order. It is important to realize that White cannot hope to defend the g4 -pawn with Re4 because the rook can be destabilized.} 22. Re4 Nd3 $1 {The queen is cut off from the defense of the rook, and White is unable to hold the g4 -pawn. He will lose.  B)} 23. Rxe8+ Qxe8 {hxg4 Qe2! This is an important move, but also a very obvious one. White will be crushed. Qe4 Qxf2+} 24. Kh1 Rd8 {Qe1 Rxd2 and nothing works: Bxd2 Qxf3+ or Nxd2 Qxe1+ are curtains, and after Qxf2 Rxf2 or Qe8+ Bf8 Nxd2 Nc2 Rb1 Ne3 , Black is crushing. E.g.: Qe4 Qe1+} 25. Kh2 Bd6+ 26. Kh3 {Qg3}) 20. Ne4 {Well spotted by Dominguez. He eventually lost anyway, but here, White should not be worse.} (20. exf6 {would be wrong due to} hxg4 {.}) 20... Nxe4 21. Qxe4 hxg4 22. hxg4 Qd3 23. b3 Qxe4 24. Rxe4 Nd3 25. Be3 f5 26. gxf5 gxf5 27. Rh4 Nxe5 28. Nxe5 Rxe5 29. Bxc5 Rxc5 30. Rd1 Kf7 31. Rh7+ Kf6 32. Rdd7 Rg8+ 33. Kf1 Rc1+ 34. Ke2 Re8+ 35. Kf3 Rc3+ 36. Kg2 c6 37. Ra7 Rg8+ 38. Kf1 Rc1+ 39. Ke2 Rc2+ 40. Kf3 Rxa2 41. Rhc7 Rc2 42. Rxa6 Rc3+ 43. Ke2 Re8+ 44. Kd2 Rf3 45. Raxc6+ Kg5 46. Rg7+ Kf4 47. Rh6 Rxf2+ 48. Kc3 Rf3+ 49. Kb4 Ree3 50. Kxb5 Rxb3+ 51. Kc4 Rh3 52. Ra6 Rhc3+ 53. Kd5 Rb5+ 54. Kd4 Rbb3 55. Rf7 Rd3+ 56. Kc4 Rbc3+ 57. Kb4 Rb3+ 58. Kc4 Rdc3+ 59. Kd4 Re3 60. Ra1 Red3+ 61. Kc4 Ra3 62. Rh1 Rh3 63. Rd1 Rhe3 64. Rf1+ Rf3 65. Rd1 Ra4+ 66. Kb5 Re4 67. Rf8 Kg3 68. Rd5 Rb3+ 69. Kc5 f4 70. Rg5+ Kf2 71. Rgf5 f3 72. R5f7 Rg4 73. Rf6 Re3 74. R6f7 Kg2 75. Rf6 Rg3 76. Kd4 Re2 77. Kd3 Ra2 78. Kd4 *

