[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2kr1b1r/ppqn1p2/2p1p2p/3pP3/3P1nB1/1N1P4/PP4PP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 17"]
[PlyCount "4"]

17. Rxf4 {Now, how should Black proceed? I played the second-best move.} h5 {Using the Shankland Method would have led me to a far superior execution of the same idea that I ultimately played. This is definitely the move Black would like to make, destabilizing the g4 -bishop and opening the h6 -square. It looks like it hangs a pawn, but what happens if I do it anyway?} (17... f5 $6 18. Bh5 $1 {Unfortunately for me, White now is able to get his bishop to a far better square than he would have been able to if I had found the best move. Black is a little more comfortable, but it's not much, and Hikaru held on without much issue.} ({Other moves wouldn't cut it:} 18. exf6 Qxf4 {or} 19. Bh3 h5 {are fine for Black.})) 18. Bxh5 (18. Bh3 {This should be preferred, but now after} f5 {Black has a much better version of the position I got in the game because White's bishop ended up badly misplaced on g3 .} 19. Rh4 {White's center is not solid enough for this move to work.} Qb6 $1 20. Kh1 a5 $1 {White cannot take on h5 without losing d4 , and he should lose in just a few more moves.} 21. Rxh5 Rxh5 22. Qxh5 a4 {and White is in trouble, but after a4 Be7 , White can't still take on h5 because the b3 -knight is loose.}) 18... f6 {White's center will collapse, and Black is crushing.} *

