1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 Ne7 6. O-O Nd7 7. Nbd2 h6 8. Nb3 g5 9. Ne1 Qc7 10. Nd3 Ng6 11. Ne1 (11. g4 {doesn't scare Black due to} Bxd3 {.}) 11... Ne7 12. f4 {Gaining space on the kingside and the center and threatening to take on g5 . What should Black do?} O-O-O {Of course, this is the move Black wants to make. It looks like it loses the g5 -pawn, but we have to ask: What if I do it anyway?} 13. Nd3 {Hikaru wisely avoided taking the pawn.} (13. fxg5 $2 {Going pawn-grabbing is a bad idea.} hxg5 14. Bxg5 $2 f6 {The pawn is immune from capture thanks to h2 hanging. Black will take back his pawn, and White's kingside is collapsing.}) (13. g4 Be4 $1 {This is the best square for the bishop. It looks like it hangs f7 . But we have to ask, what if I do it anyway?} 14. fxg5 c5 $1 {White's center comes under direct assault. And once the center falls, Black's pieces will all converge on White's vulnerable king.} 15. c3 c4 $1 16. Nd2 Nxe5 $1 17. dxe5 hxg5 {Black's pieces rush into the attack, and White is done for.}) {My plan was hxg5 Rxf7 Nxe5 dxe5 Bg7 Rxg7 Qxe5 Also good for Black, but 14... c5 ! was stronger.} 13... Ng6 14. Bg4 Bxd3 15. cxd3 gxf4 16. Bxf4 {This was too optimistic.} Nxf4 17. Rxf4 {Now, how should Black proceed? I played the second-best move.} f5 (17... h5 $1 {Using the Shankland Method would have led me to a far superior execution of the same idea that I ultimately played. This is definitely the move Black would like to make, destabilizing the g4 -bishop and opening the h6 -square. It looks like it hangs a pawn, but what happens if I do it anyway?} 18. Bxh5 f6 {White's center will collapse, and Black is crushing. Bh3 This should be preferred, but now after f5-+ Black has a much better version of the position I got in the game because White's bishop ended up badly misplaced on g3 .} 19. Rh4 {White's center is not solid enough for this move to work.} Qb6 $1 20. Kh1 a5 $1 {White cannot take on h5 without losing d4 , and he should lose in just a few more moves. Rxh5 Rxh5 Qxh5} 21. a4 {and White is in trouble, but after a4} Be7 {, White can't still take on h5 because the b3 -knight is loose.}) 18. Bh5 {Unfortunately for me, White now is able to get his bishop to a far better square than he would have been able to if I had found the best move. Black is a little more comfortable, but it's not much, and Hikaru held on without much issue.} ({Other moves wouldn't cut it:} 18. exf6 Qxf4 {or} 19. Bh3 h5 {are fine for Black.}) 18... Nf6 19. Kh1 Nxh5 20. Qxh5 Qg7 21. Rf3 Rg8 22. Rg3 Qh7 23. Rxg8 Qxg8 24. Rf1 Qg5 25. Qxg5 hxg5 26. g4 fxg4 27. Rf7 Re8 28. Kg2 Be7 29. Kg3 b6 30. Kxg4 Kd7 31. Rf3 ({It's important that White can't play} 31. Nd2 Rh8 32. Nf3 Ke8 {. Hikaru offered a draw, and I accepted.}) *

