13. Bg5 {In the critical Norway-Armenia match at the 2015 European Team Championship, Magnus came up with an interesting setup in the d3 Berlin middlegame. It can only be disarmed by using the Shankland Method.} h6 {This is clearly the move Black wants to play, but it looks like it will drop the pawn on a5 . But we have to ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} 14. Bxf6 Qxf6 15. Nxa5 {Now what?} d5 {This is clearly the move Black wants, freezing the knight on a5 and taking more space in the center. It looks like it hangs a pawn, but what happens if I do it anyway?} 16. exd5 {Of course, Black should be ready in case White goes pawn-grabbing.} ({The less critical} 16. Bc2 dxe4 17. dxe4 Rd8 {was the game continuation.}) 16... cxd5 17. Bxd5 {Now, White is almost ready to bring the knight back and consolidate, but...} Qd8 {Black forks the pieces!} 18. Bxb7 {Otherwise, Black grabs a piece on the spot.} Bxb7 19. Nxb7 Qc7 {The knight ends up trapped.} 20. Qf3 Rab8 {Black should win.} *

