1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 5. O-O (5. c3 {is the standard move, controlling the d4 -square.}) 5... Nd4 6. Nxd4 Bxd4 7. c3 Bb6 8. Na3 c6 9. Ba4 d6 10. Bb3 a5 11. Nc4 Ba7 12. a4 O-O 13. Bg5 {In the critical Norway-Armenia match at the 2015 European Team Championship, Magnus came up with an interesting setup in the d3 Berlin middlegame. It can only be disarmed by using the Shankland Method.} h6 {This is clearly the move Black wants to play, but it looks like it will drop the pawn on a5 . But we have to ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} 14. Bxf6 Qxf6 15. Nxa5 {Now what?} d5 {This is clearly the move Black wants, freezing the knight on a5 and taking more space in the center. It looks like it hangs a pawn, but what happens if I do it anyway?} 16. Bc2 (16. exd5 {Of course, Black should be ready in case White goes pawn-grabbing.} cxd5 17. Bxd5 $2 {Now, White is almost ready to bring the knight back and consolidate, but...} Qd8 $1 {Black forks the pieces!} 18. Bxb7 {Otherwise, Black grabs a piece on the spot.} Bxb7 19. Nxb7 Qc7 $1 {The knight ends up trapped.} 20. Qf3 Rab8 {Black should win.}) 16... dxe4 17. dxe4 Rd8 18. Qe1 ({Less accurate is} 18. Qe2 Qg5 {.}) 18... Qg5 19. Kh1 Rd2 20. Bd1 Be6 21. b4 {Now, what?} Rad8 {Obviously, Black would like to bring his last piece into the game on the open file. It looks like this drops the b7 -pawn with tempo, but we know what to ask.} 22. Nxb7 Bc4 23. Nxd8 Bxf1 24. Qxf1 Rxf2 {Of course, Black does not settle for taking the knight on d8 and remaining two pawns down.} 25. Qg1 Ra2 {Black wins material.} 26. Rxa2 (26. Qxa7 Qxg2#) 26... Bxg1 27. Kxg1 Qc1 {The computer sees no difference, but I believe this is stronger than ... Qxd8 .} 28. Kf2 Qxd1 29. Nxc6 Qb3 30. Rd2 Qxc3 31. Rd6 Qb2+ 32. Ke3 Qa3+ 33. Kf2 Qxa4 {White would fare better with a bishop.} 34. Nxe5 Qc2+ 35. Kf3 f5 36. Rd3 (36. exf5 Qxf5+ {drops material.}) 36... fxe4+ 37. Kxe4 Qxg2+ 38. Nf3 Qg4+ 39. Ke3 g5 40. Kf2 Qf5 41. Rd8+ Kg7 42. Kg2 g4 43. Nd2 Qe6 44. Nf1 Qc6+ *

