12... d5 {This is the most resilient. Now, how should White proceed?} 13. Rh3 {This should be the best way to prepare g6 .} (13. Bh3 Ne5 {White does not have g6 anymore here, and Black is very comfortable, but not e5? due to} 14. exd5 {, whereas Nf5? d4⩱ is fine for Black.}) (13. g6 $2 {Unfortunately, here, full steam ahead does not work. White should ask what happens if he does it anyway, but in this case, he will not like the answer he receives.} hxg6 14. hxg6 Rxh1 15. gxf7+ Kxf7 ({(Again,} 15... Ke7 {is not the right call:} 16. Qg4 Kd6 17. Qxe6+ Kc7 18. Bf4+ Kc8 19. Nc6 {is crushing.)}) 16. Qf3+ Qf6 $1 17. Qxh1 e5 $1 {The point of ... d5 is revealed. Once ... e5 and ... d4 come, the king looks fantastic on f7 . White is lucky not to be totally lost.}) (13. Bg2 $2 Nc4 $1 {Black has reasonable counterplay. The highest and best use of White's light-squared bishop in most Sicilian middlegames is to take the black knight when it reaches c4 .}) (13. Rg1 {again gives Black a tempo to play} g6 {.}) (13. exd5 Nxd5 14. Nxd5 Bxd5 {is what Black wants.}) 13... dxe4 ({My opponent realized he couldn't stop g6 and played} 13... e5 {.}) (13... Ne5 $2 {will be featured in our next exercise.}) 14. g6 hxg6 (14... Nf6 15. gxf7+ Kxf7 16. Ndxb5 {and Black should play} Nbd5 {as taking the knight is met by axb5 Qxd8 Rxd8} 17. Bb6 {.}) 15. hxg6 Rxh3 16. gxf7+ {An important intermediate move.} Kxf7 17. Bxh3 {White is crushing.} *

