24... Nf5 {How should White proceed?} 25. Nf6 {This is the move we want to make. It looks like it hangs g7 . But... what happens if I do it anyway?} Rxg7 (25... Nxf6 26. Bxf6+ Kc7 {it's still not easy. White has to be precise. The rest of the game falls outside the scope of The Shankland Method, so I have not made this position into an exercise in the Movetrainer. That said, feel free to set it up on a board and try to find White's best move!}) 26. d6 {The point. The mate threat is a problem.} (26. Re8+ $2 Kc7 {and now} 27. d6+ {is simply met by} Nxd6 {but also insufficient is} 28. Rxa8 Bxa8 29. Ne8+ Kd8 {Oops! White has hanging pieces.}) (26. Bc3 {is fine too, but d6 is more forcing and clear, and more human.}) 26... Nxd6 ({Worse is} 26... Nxf6 27. Bxf6+ Kc8 28. Bh3 {.}) 27. Bxb7 Nxb7 28. Re8+ Kc7 29. Rxa8 {White emerges with an extra pawn.} *

