1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. h3 e6 7. g4 Nfd7 8. g5 b5 9. a3 Bb7 10. Be3 Nb6 11. h4 N8d7 12. h5 {Always ask, what happens if I do it anyway? This is clearly the move White wants to make.} (12. Qd2 {is too slow and met by} Ne5 {.}) (12. Bh3 {is also met by} Ne5 {.}) (12. Rh3 {is not bad here, but g6 is more to the point.}) (12. Rg1 {is also too slow and allows} g6 {.}) 12... d5 {This is the most resilient. Now, how should White proceed?} (12... Rc8 {This was not played in the game but is interesting and relevant all the same. How should White proceed? Qd2 is too slow and met by Ne5= . Bh3 is also met by Ne5 . Rh3 is not bad here, but g6 is more to the point. Rg1 is also too slow and allows g6 . g6! Always ask, what happens if I do it anyway? This is clearly the move White wants to make. fxg6 Nxe6 is not something Black wants to see. hxg6 hxg6 Rxh1 gxf7+ when it's critical to see that Ke7 fails to Qg4 and Nc5 is not possible due to Bg5+ . Black should play Kxf7 but he's definitely worse. Qf3+ Nf6 Qxh1 Rxc3 bxc3 Bxe4 Bg2± I don't buy this exchange sacrifice, but Black didn't have much of a choice. The first rule of any exchange sacrifice is to always ask, 'How good are the rooks?' Here, the rook will not be so terrible.}) 13. Rh3 {This should be the best way to prepare g6 .} (13. Bh3 Ne5 {White does not have g6 anymore here, and Black is very comfortable, but not e5? due to} 14. exd5 {, whereas Nf5? d4⩱ is fine for Black.}) (13. g6 $2 {Unfortunately, here, full steam ahead does not work. White should ask what happens if he does it anyway, but in this case, he will not like the answer he receives.} hxg6 14. hxg6 Rxh1 15. gxf7+ Kxf7 ({(Again,} 15... Ke7 {is not the right call:} 16. Qg4 Kd6 17. Qxe6+ Kc7 18. Bf4+ Kc8 19. Nc6 {is crushing.)}) 16. Qf3+ Qf6 $1 17. Qxh1 e5 $1 {The point of ... d5 is revealed. Once ... e5 and ... d4 come, the king looks fantastic on f7 . White is lucky not to be totally lost.}) (13. Bg2 $2 Nc4 $1 {Black has reasonable counterplay. The highest and best use of White's light-squared bishop in most Sicilian middlegames is to take the black knight when it reaches c4 .}) (13. Rg1 {again gives Black a tempo to play} g6 {.}) (13. exd5 Nxd5 14. Nxd5 Bxd5 {is what Black wants.}) 13... e5 (13... dxe4 14. g6 {and now:  A)} Nf6 15. gxf7+ Kxf7 16. Ndxb5 {and Black should play} Nbd5 {as taking the knight is met by axb5 Qxd8 Rxd8} 17. Bb6 {.}) ({B) hxg6 hxg6 Rxh3 gxf7+ An important intermediate. Kxf7 Bxh3} 13... Ne5 $2 14. g6 $1 {Again, what happens if I do it anyway?} hxg6 15. hxg6 {when} Rxh3 {is again met by the intermediate} 16. gxf7+ {and White wins. Nxg6 Rxh8 Nxh8 Qh5! Ng6 Nxe6+- Black collapses. But not Nxe6?? fxe6 Qh5+} Nxf7 {Don't be that guy!}) 14. exd5 (14. Ndxb5 $5 {This was not bad either.} axb5 15. Bxb6 $1 Qxb6 16. Nxd5 {.}) 14... exd4 15. Qxd4 Bd6 {Now, the move we really want to play is h6 . It looks like ... Be5 ruins our day, but we always ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} 16. h6 Be5 17. hxg7 {What happens if I do it anyway?} (17. Qd2 Qe7 {and Black is back in the game.}) 17... Bxd4 (17... Rg8 {is Black's best move, but hardly critical. If you believe that ... Rg8 is coming, you play h6 and hit the clock.} 18. Qd2 Rxg7 19. f4 {.}) 18. Bxd4 Rg8 19. Re3+ {Nice that the rook went to h3 !} Qe7 20. Rxe7+ Kxe7 21. Bg2 {Black has a miserable endgame. His pieces are super stuck. Still, White needs to be energetic to make use of the advantage. A rook is a rook, after all.} Nc4 22. O-O-O (22. Kf1 $2 {This is too slow. White denies himself Be4 thanks to a check on d2 .} Rae8 23. Bh3 Kd8 {Just like that, Black coordinates well and is good to go.}) 22... Nd6 (22... Rae8 23. b3 Nd6 24. Ne4) 23. Re1+ Kd8 {Now, Black is ready for ... Nf5 , ... Ne8 , or ... Kc7 and ... Rae8 . White should be very energetic to win the game.} 24. Ne4 {This is the move we want to play to maximize our activity. It looks like there are tactical problems. But always ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} (24. Bh3 $2 Ne8) 24... Nf5 (24... Kc7 25. Nf6 $1 {This looks like it hangs g7 . But... what happens if I do it anyway?} Rxg7 26. Re7 $1 {It's important to bring the rook in and not to capture with the knight on e7 . Black is in big trouble as} Rxg5 {runs into} 27. Rxd7+ {. After Rd8 , the computer is a savage and plays f4 here, but Nxd7 Rxg5 Nc5+} Kb8 28. Bf3 {is quite enough in human terms.}) {But not Nxd7 Rxg5 and Black is back in the game. After Nxf6 gxf6 Rae8 Rxe8 Rxe8 Be5! , Black can barely move, and White has endless threats. The simplest is b3 followed by c4 . Bxd5 is also met by Nf6! Bxg2 Nxg8+-} 25. Nf6 {This is the move we want to make. It looks like it hangs g7 . But... what happens if I do it anyway?} Nxf6 (25... Rxg7 26. d6 $1 {The point. The mate threat is a problem.} Nxd6 ({(Worse is} 26... Nxf6 27. Bxf6+ Kc8 28. Bh3 {.)}) 27. Bxb7 Nxb7 28. Re8+ Kc7 29. Rxa8 {White emerges with an extra pawn.}) (25... Re8 $2 {Kc7 and now} 26. d6 {is simply met by} Nxd6 {but also insufficient is Rxa8 Bxa8} 27. Nxe8 {Kd8∓ Oops! White has hanging pieces.}) {Bc3 is fine too, but d6 is more forcing and clear, and more human.} 26. Bxf6+ Kc7 {It's still not easy. White has to be precise. The rest of the game falls outside the scope of The Shankland Method, so I have not made this position into an exercise in the Movetrainer. That said, feel free to set it up on a board and try to find White's best move!} 27. Re5 {We need to kick the knight away from the defense of e7 .} (27. Bh3 $2 {This is not the right way to kick the knight.} Bc8 {White is missing d6 +.}) (27. d6+ $6 {White is not ready for this one.} Kb6) 27... Nh4 {This is the most testing. Black counterattacks the g2 -bishop, making sure that he can meet Re7 + with ... Kd6 . Now, White needs a couple more precise moves to bring the point home.} (27... Bc8 28. d6+) (27... Nxg7 28. Re7+ {This is very routine for White. After} Kd6 29. Rxb7 {, there's no hanging bishop. Kb8 Rxf7} Ne8 30. Be5+ {Ka7 Bd4+ Kb8 f4+- Stalemate. Who cares about the extra rook?}) 28. d6+ {Now and only now. Black's knight has been kicked away.} (28. Re7+ Kd6 29. Rxb7 Nxg2) 28... Kb6 29. Bxb7 Kxb7 {And now, one more good move finishes the game. The best way to find it is to first look at the most direct move, which is obviously d7 . It does not work, but once you figure out why it does not work, it is not too hard to spot the right move.} 30. Rc5 {White cuts the king off from c6 . Now, we are threatening both mate with Rc7 and d7 followed by Rd5 .} ({Instead, it's unclear what White is threatening after} 30. d7 $2 {I don't think he wants to make a queen next. The big idea should be Rd5 . Then Black loses immediately. As soon as we see that Rd5 is coming, it is not hard to see Black's response. White succeeds after} Nf3 31. Rd5 Kc7 32. d8=Q+ {or} Raxd8 33. Bxd8+ Rxd8 34. g8=Q) ({However, Kc6! prevents Rd5 , and White is forced to push the d -pawn through prematurely. Black should be fine. d8=Q Rgxd8} 30. Bd8 {Rxd8 Re7 Rg8 Rxf7 Kd5 with ... Ke5 and ... Nf5 to come, and White should think about how to make a draw.}) 30... Nf5 (30... Rac8 31. d7 $1 {and White wins after both} Rb8 32. Rd5 {and Rxc5 d8=Q Rxd8 Bxd8+-}) (30... Ng6 {intends to meet} 31. d7 {with} Nf4 {but after} 32. Rc7+ $1 Kb6 33. b4 a5 34. Bd4+ Ka6 {Rxf7+- , Black is forever stuck, and White can win with either Kc1 - b2 - b3 and c2 - c4 to give mate, or simply pushing his pawn to f7 .}) 31. Rxf5 {Ruifeng kept playing for a bit, but the result was obviously no longer in doubt.} Kc6 32. Bd4 Kxd6 33. Rxf7 *

