[PlyCount "78"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 7. Nb3 Be6 8. f3 Be7 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O Nbd7 11. g4 b5 12. g5 b4 (12... Nh5 {is Black's top choice nowadays.}) 13. Ne2 Ne8 14. f4 a5 15. f5 a4 16. Nbd4 {This move was all the rage at the time, but nowadays, it is not considered the most critical and rarely seen. I would not be surprised if this game had something to do with that trend!} (16. fxe6 $1 {This should be the most testing. Black is okay, but needs to be very well prepared. I played it once against Duda en route to winning the 2021 Prague Masters. He made one mistake, and the game was already more or less over.} axb3 17. cxb3 fxe6 18. Bh3 {and here} Rxa2 {is the only move, and play continues} 19. Bxe6+ {and so on. If instead Nc7 Kb1 , Black is busted.}) 16... exd4 17. Nxd4 b3 (17... Bxa2 {runs into} 18. Nc6 {.}) 18. Kb1 bxc2+ 19. Nxc2 Bb3 20. axb3 axb3 21. Na3 Ne5 22. h4 Ra5 23. Qc3 Qa8 24. Bg2 {How should Black proceed?} Nc7 {This is clearly the move Black wants to make. He gets ready for ... Nb5 , clears the back rank to get the f8 -rook into play, and launches a vicious attack. It looks bad because the knight hangs. But we have to ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} (24... Rxa3 25. bxa3 Qxa3 26. Rd2 {and White's king is totally safe.}) 25. Qxc7 Rc8 {In for a penny, in for a pound. Black will give mate.} 26. Qxe7 Nc4 (26... Rxa3 $2 {This is an easy trap to fall for.} 27. bxa3 Qxa3 28. Qa7 $1 {Oops!}) 27. g6 {Definitely the most testing. Now, Black should be accurate to bring the point home.} (27. Bc1 Nxa3+ 28. bxa3 Rxa3 29. Bxa3 Qxa3 {and White has no Qa7 .} 30. Rd2 Ra8) 27... hxg6 {This clears the h7 -square for the king, and then the win is not difficult anymore.} (27... Nxa3+ $2 {is too blunt.} 28. bxa3 Rxa3 {It looks like Black is mating. But remember, he sacrificed two full pieces for this attack! White can give some material back to distract the attackers, save his king, and remain with some material up all the same.} 29. gxf7+ $1 Kh8 30. f8=Q+ $1 Rxf8 31. Qxf8+ Qxf8 {For one key move, Black's queen is distracted. White consolidates and probably wins.} 32. Bd4 {The rook comes to d2 next, and Black will not give mate.} Qa8 33. Rd2 {White is very much alive and winning.}) 28. fxg6 Nxa3+ ({Now and only now, though} 28... Rxa3 {wins too.}) 29. bxa3 Rxa3 30. gxf7+ Kh7 31. f8=N+ Rxf8 32. Qxf8 Ra1+ {Taking the queen does win, but this is so much more satisfying.} 33. Kb2 Ra2+ 34. Kc3 (34. Kb1 Qxf8 35. Rd2 Rxd2 36. Bxd2 Qf2 {is game over.}) 34... Qa5+ 35. Kd3 Qb5+ 36. Kd4 Ra4+ 37. Kc3 Qc4+ {White is mated.} 38. Kd2 Ra2+ 39. Ke1 Qe2# *

