1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Bd3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Bd3 Bd6 9. O-O O-O 10. Qc2 Bb7 11. a3 Rc8 12. Ng5 {It is every Meran player's dream to play a game like this one. White has just played Ng5 , threatening the pawn on h7 . What should we do?} ({We have already seen} 12. b4 {where} a5 {is the move I recommended in my Semi-Slav LTR, and Kasimdzhanov dropped a novelty against Topalov.}) 12... c5 {The Shankland Method strikes again. Black seemingly could not advance c6 - c5 because it hangs b5 and ignores the threat to h7 , but always ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} 13. Nxh7 {White grabs a pawn with tempo.} (13. Bxh7+ {Modern theory suggests this is a better move, but Black still has good play, for example, after} Kh8 14. f4 cxd4 15. exd4 g6 {The game is very complicated. I covered this position and the entire opening line in more detail in my Semi-Slav course.}) 13... Ng4 {Shankland Method again! Black pays no need to the hanging rook on f8 and sends everything at White's kingside.} 14. f4 (14. Nxf8 Bxh2+ 15. Kh1 Qh4 {and mate.}) 14... cxd4 15. exd4 {The next move is one of the toughest ones, but once you put yourself in your dream world where anything is possible, you can ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} Bc5 {Black goes after the d4 -pawn.} 16. Be2 (16. dxc5 {For better or for worse, White had to take the bishop.} Nxc5 {and now:  A)} 17. Be2 {is bad due to} Qd4+ 18. Kh1 Nf2+ {and White has to go} 19. Rxf2 Qxf2 {because Kg1 leads to mate after Nh3+ Kh1 Qg1+ Rxg1 Nf2}) ({B)} 16. Nxf8 {Qd4+ Kh1 Nxd3 is good for Black.}) ({C) Best is} 16. h3 $1 {Qd4+ Kh1 Nxd3 Nxf8! Ndf2+ Rxf2} Nf2 17. Kh2 {Kxf8 when White's position is not fun, but the game goes on.}) 16... Nde5 {The most convincing and forceful of several winning moves. Black is ready to bring the queen in to d4 .} 17. Bxg4 (17. Nxf8 Qxd4+ 18. Kh1 Qg1+ $1 {would lead to a pretty finish.} 19. Rxg1 Nf2#) (17. fxe5 Qxd4+ 18. Kh1 Qg1+ {also leads to a Smothered Mate.}) (17. dxc5 Qd4+ 18. Kh1 Nf2+ 19. Rxf2 Qxf2 {is crushing.}) 17... Bxd4+ 18. Kh1 Nxg4 19. Nxf8 f5 {Black makes one last preparatory move before bringing the queen to h4 .} (19... Qh4 $2 20. Qh7+ $1 {White gets the queens off and survives.}) 20. Ng6 Qf6 21. h3 Qxg6 22. Qe2 Qh5 23. Qd3 (23. Rf3 {would save White from getting mated, but the game is lost anyway.} Bxf3 24. Qxf3 Nf2+ 25. Kh2 Qxf3 26. gxf3 Nd3 {.}) 23... Be3 {Vishy has played some amazing games of chess in his life, but this one was surely my favorite.} *

