1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 (4... Nf6 {is more popular nowadays, though I can't understand why. Bf5 makes a ton of sense.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Be7 13. Ne4 (13. Kb1 {is the most testing.}) 13... Nxe4 14. Qxe4 Nf6 15. Qd3 O-O 16. Kb1 c5 {This is a typical Mainline Caro-Kann middlegame. Nowadays, positions like this one are not too common- White players are normally playing the Advance variation if they are ambitious and fighting for an opening edge, or a sideline if they just want an easy position to play. This is something of a shame because I have always found these sharp, opposite-side castles' middlegames to be really enjoyable. Kamsky put on a clinic here, using the Shankland Method multiple times to score a quick win.} *

