[PlyCount "4"]

16... c5 {Kamsky put on a clinic here, using the Shankland Method multiple times to score a quick win. How did he start?} 17. g4 {This is clearly the move White wants to play. The whole goal of White's setup is to smash through with g4 - g5 to tear open Black's kingside. It looks like this move hangs a pawn, but we should ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} (17. dxc5 Qxd3 18. cxd3 Bxc5 {is not attractive.}) 17... Nxg4 (17... cxd4 $2 18. g5 {White should easily give mate.}) 18. Qe2 {This is a very clever move. White defends the f2 -pawn but also pressures the e6 -pawn. As such, once he plays a rook to g1 next, Black will not be able to safely play ... f5 in response. Black's position is very difficult here, but he can fight back using the Shankland Method. That's your next exercise!} (18. Rhg1 {The Shankland Method is all about asking, 'What happens if I do it anyway?' This does not mean 'Always do it anyway, regardless of whether it works or not!' This is clearly the move White wants to play, but there are two issues. First off, Black can get away with taking on f2 . Secondly, Black is able to bolster the knight with f7 - f5 .} f5 $1 {Black bolsters the knight, and he looks totally fine to me. I don't see a great way forward for White.} (18... Nxf2 19. Qe2 Nxd1 20. Bxh6 {This looks very scary, but after} Kh7 $1 21. Bxg7 Rg8 {White is a long way from giving mate and is lucky not to be lost.})) *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Level 5"]
[Black "Kamsky-Seirawan Exercise #2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/pp2bpp1/4p2p/2p4P/3P2n1/3Q1N2/PPPB1P2/1K1R3R w - - 0 18"]
[PlyCount "8"]

18. Qe2 {Black's position is very difficult here, but he can fight back using the Shankland Method. How?} cxd4 {What happens if I do it anyway?} (18... Kh8 $6 19. Rhg1 Nf6 20. dxc5 $1 {White is very happy to grab this pawn.}) 19. Rhg1 d3 {An important resource. Black shuts down the open d -file.} (19... f5 $2 {This is tempting, but if Black does not flick in d4 - d3 first, the d -file will be a huge problem.} 20. Bxh6 $1 gxh6 21. Qxe6+ Kh8 22. Rxd4 $1 {White's rook swings into the game with authority, and the attack is immediately decisive.} Qe8 23. Rdxg4 fxg4 24. Qxh6+ Kg8 25. Rxg4+ Kf7 26. Qg6#) 20. cxd3 f5 21. Qxe6+ (21. Bxh6 gxh6 22. Qxe6+ Kh8) 21... Kh8 {Black gave back both of the pawns he took, but his king looks fairly safe. The computer still gives White a slight edge, but the position does not look so bad to me.} *

