21... Bxc5 {Now, all the pieces are set up for the decisive penetration. White wins by using the Shankland rule.} 22. Bxh6 {This is the move we really want to play. It looks like a non-starter - after ... gxh6 , we don't get to take with the queen on h6 again. But always ask what happens if I do it anyway?} gxh6 23. Rd7 {Shankland rule at play once more. White slams the rook into Black's position. It looks suicidal since the rook can be taken, but... what happens if I do it anyway?} (23. Qd2 $2 {This is the right idea, but the wrong execution.} Qxe5 $1 24. Qxh6+ Nh7 {Black defends and wins with two pieces up.}) 23... Nxd7 (23... Qxd7 24. Nxd7 Nxd7 25. Qd2 $1 {The final important move. White wins by making twin threats of mate on h6 with the knight on d7 . The rest was not noteworthy, and Black could have resigned here.}) 24. Qd2 {Now, this works like a charm. Black is unable to defend h6 , and since his knight cannot make it back to h7 , he is checkmated.} Kh7 (24... Qxe5 25. Qxh6#) 25. Ng4 {Mate is coming.} *

