ry scary, but after} Kh7 $1 21. Bxg7 Rg8 {White is a long way from giving mate and is lucky not to be lost.})) 18... Kh8 (18... cxd4 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway?} 19. Rhg1 d3 $1 {An important resource. Black shuts down the open d -file.} 20. cxd3 f5 $1 21. Bxh6 gxh6 22. Qxe6+ Kh8 {or Qxe6+ Kh8 Black gave back both of the pawns he took, but his king looks fairly safe. The computer still gives White a slight edge, but the position does not look so bad to me.}) (18... f5 $2 {is tempting, but if Black does not flick in ... d3 first, the d -file will be a huge problem.} 19. Bxh6 $1 gxh6 20. Qxe6+ Kh8 {Rxd4! White's rook swings into the game with authority, and the attack is immediately decisive. Qe8 Rdxg4 fxg4} 21. Qxh6+ Kg8 {Rxg4+ Kf7} 22. Qg6+) 19. Rhg1 Nf6 20. dxc5 {White is very happy to grab this pawn.} Qc7 (20... Bxc5 21. Bxh6 {.}) 21. Ne5 (21. b4 {This is also possible, and White is much better, but I prefer Kamsky's choice for its direct nature.}) 21... Bxc5 {Now, all the pieces are set up for the decisive penetration. White wins by using the Shankland rule.} 22. Bxh6 {This is the move we really want to play. It looks like a non-starter- after gxh6 , we don't get to take with the queen on h6 again. But always ask what happens if I do it anyway?} gxh6 23. Rd7 {Shankland rule at play once more. White slams the rook into Black's position. It looks suicidal since the rook can be taken, but... what happens if I do it anyway?} (23. Qd2 $2 {This is the right idea, but the wrong execution.} Qxe5 $1 24. Qxh6+ Nh7 {Black defends and wins with two pieces up.}) 23... Qxd7 (23... Nxd7 24. Qd2 $1 {Now, this works like a charm. Black is unable to defend h6 , and since his knight cannot make it back to h7 , he is checkmated.} Qxe5 25. Qxh6# {or Kh7 Ng4! and mate is coming.}) 24. Nxd7 Nxd7 25. Qd2 {The final important move. White wins by making twin threats of mate on h6 with the knight on d7 . The rest was not noteworthy, and Black could have resigned here.} Kh7 26. b4 Rad8 27. bxc5 Nf6 28. Qf4 Ne8 29. Qe4+ Kh8 30. Qxb7 Ng7 31. Qxa7 Rc8 32. Rd1 Nf5 33. Rd7 Kg7 34. a4 Kf6 35. a5 *

