1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. f3 c5 5. d5 b5 6. e4 d6 ({A trendy new move.} 6... O-O {is the old mainline, and in all honesty, I think it is Black's best option.}) 7. Bd2 a6 {This move was recommended by my friend Surya Ganguly in his course on the Nimzo-Indian for Chessable. Karjakin followed his proposed repertoire for quite a while, but of course, I was ready.} 8. a4 bxc4 9. Bxc4 Nbd7 {This is Black's big idea. By offering a pawn, he accelerates his development and makes sure White cannot consolidate his position by playing Nge2 and castling.} (9... O-O $2 {is too slow. After} 10. Nge2 {, White is ready to castle next, with a pleasant position due to his extra space.}) 10. dxe6 {Very necessary. Black should not be allowed to play ... Ne5 unmolested.} fxe6 11. Bxe6 Ne5 12. Bxc8 Qxc8 13. Bf4 {Only a few games had reached this far, but Sergey and I were still blitzing out our moves.} c4 14. Bxe5 dxe5 15. Nh3 {This was the first moment Sergey took a real think. In all previous games, and in Surya's course, Black continues with ... Qc5 . I don't believe ... Bc5 , as played, is a mistake, but Black will remain a pawn down and should be precise to keep enough compensation.} Bc5 (15... Qc5 {This is Surya's recommendation, bringing the queen out to stop White from castling. I had found some new ideas to try to put Black under some practical pressure, but I do think he should be more or less okay if he knows what he is doing and finds a few good moves.}) 16. Qe2 Rb8 17. Nf2 {Still following my preparation. Here, the best move is surely ... Bd4 , which feels pretty natural. Still, White can put some pressure. Karjakin made a mistake now.} *

