[PlyCount "14"]

{Qb4 is met by Rb1 , and the engine suggests retreating to b6 , but the endgame is grim for Black.} 17... Qb7 {This is a big mistake, but it takes some serious calculation and confidence for White to punish it. How should White continue?} 18. Qxc4 {This was best, not allowing Black the option to change his mind and stick his queen on b4 . In all likelihood, we will transpose to the game, but I was worried about what would happen if Black took on f2 first.} (18. O-O {Decent, but not the most incisive.}) 18... Bxf2+ (18... Qxb2 19. O-O {transposes to the game.}) 19. Kxf2 Qxb2+ (19... Qa7+ $5 {I did not even consider this move either, which also looks scary. The machine claims White wins following} 20. Kf1 $1 Rxb2 21. Nd1 $1 Rb8 22. Ke2 $1 {When I will apparently be in time to fix my pieces with Rc1 - Rc2 , and Ne3 before Black can cause any trouble. It really takes veins of ice to play chess like this.}) 20. Kg3 {Boldly charging ahead.} (20. Ne2 $2 {is tempting, but after} Qb6+ $1 21. Ke1 Ke7 {White will struggle to get his h1 -rook into the game, ... Rhc8 is coming, and Black gets good counterplay.}) 20... Nh5+ 21. Kh4 Nf4 {I had gotten this far in my calculation, and I did not see a win for White, so I stuck with 18.O-O instead. It was there, however.} 22. Qc6+ {First White checks on c6 ...} Kf7 23. Qc7+ Kg8 24. Ra2 {This was the idea I missed, stopping any mating ideas with ... g5 + followed by ... Qxg2 . Black's queen is expelled, traded, and he remains a clean pawn down.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Bonus Level"]
[Black "Shankland-Karjakin Exercise #"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "1r2k2r/6pp/p4n2/4p3/P1Q1P3/2N2P2/1q3bPP/R4R1K b k - 0 20"]
[PlyCount "12"]

20... Qb3 {The exclamation mark is only for practical value, as Black's position was lost no matter what. But this way forces White to find the most precise moves all the way through, unlike other lines where just about anything wins. I spent almost 20 minutes calculating here, but I was up to the task. Can you do the same?} 21. Qc7 {A very powerful move, renewing all the threats. Rxf2 , Rab1 , even Qxg7 now - a lot of stuff is hanging in the air. Still, there are some details to take note of.} (21. Qc6+ $2 {This tempting check on c6 would be a huge mistake.} Kf7 $1 {and now} 22. Rxf2 {fails to} Rhc8 {and Black wins a piece. After} 23. Rb1 Qa3 24. Rxb8 Rxb8 25. Qc7+ Kg6 $1 26. Qxb8 Qxc3 $1 {, Black's king has escaped the checks, the bishop on f2 cannot be taken, the dust has settled, and White is in trouble.}) 21... Qb6 (21... Rb7 22. Qc6+ $1 {Now and only now, this check comes. Rab1 is on the way.} Kf7 23. Rab1 {Since Black's rook is on b7 rather than b8 , it will be captured.} ({O-O Now, we need to choose the right rook:  A)} 23. Rfb1 $2 {It might look like either way skewers the rook on b8 , but not so. Rbc8! Qxc8} Qxb1+ $1 {Black wins the fight for desperados since this move comes with check. If White's rook were on f1 here, he could take f8 or even check on e6 , winning easily.  B)} 24. Rxb1 $1 {Rbc8 Qxc8! This desperado wins a rook, leaving White up heavy material.} Rc8 25. Rb3 {or Qxb1 Qe6+ and White wins the queen.})) {The most resilient is Bb6 but White wins the fight of desperados. Qxb8! Qxb1 Qxf8+! Finally, White wins.} 22. Qxe5+ Kf7 {Now, Black is one move away from consolidating and forcing me to resign with a piece less. But a tempo is everything.} 23. Nd5 {White hits the queen and threatens a check on the seventh rank. Black cannot keep the position under control.} Nxd5 (23... Bd4 24. Qe7+ $1 {Nice to have this check! Any other move would lose, but now Black can resign as his queen will be taken next.}) (23... Qa7 {was probably the most resilient, but White wins.} 24. Rxf2 $1 Qxf2 25. Qc7+ $1 {The right check, making sure to leave e7 for the knight.} Kg6 26. Ne7+ $1 Kh5 27. Qe5+ g5 28. Qxf6 {White takes back all of his sacrificed material, and the attack rages on. The machine claims forced mate in 12.}) (23... Rhe8 24. Qxe8+ $1 {White ends up with a rook and 2 excellent pawns against 2 very badly coordinated minor pieces. After} Kxe8 25. Nxb6 Bxb6 26. Rab1 {, Black is absolutely busted.}) 24. Qxd5+ {Black cannot block the check with his queen since the bishop on f2 would be loose at the end. Now it's easy.} Ke7 25. Qe5+ Kf7 26. Rac1 {Black is unable to prevent a disaster on c7 .} ({Rhc8 would allow a fork after the rook exchange: Rxc8 Rxc8} 26. Qf5+ {and White picks up the rook.}) ({Rb7 fails every which way, most simply to} 26. Rxf2 Qxf2 27. Qd5+ {White would remain two pawns up, and Black's king would still be wide open. Sergey understandably resigned.}) *

