[ECO "E20"]
[PlyCount "51"]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. f3 c5 5. d5 b5 6. e4 d6 ({A trendy new move.} 6... O-O {is the old mainline, and in all honesty, I think it is Black's best option.}) 7. Bd2 a6 {This move was recommended by my friend Surya Ganguly in his course on the Nimzo-Indian for Chessable. Karjakin followed his proposed repertoire for quite a while, but of course, I was ready.} 8. a4 bxc4 9. Bxc4 Nbd7 {This is Black's big idea. By offering a pawn, he accelerates his development and makes sure White cannot consolidate his position by playing Nge2 and castling.} (9... O-O $2 {is too slow. After} 10. Nge2 {, White is ready to castle next, with a pleasant position due to his extra space.}) 10. dxe6 {Very necessary. Black should not be allowed to play ... Ne5 unmolested.} fxe6 11. Bxe6 Ne5 12. Bxc8 Qxc8 13. Bf4 {Only a few games had reached this far, but Sergey and I were still blitzing out our moves.} c4 14. Bxe5 dxe5 15. Nh3 {This was the first moment Sergey took a real think. In all previous games, and in Surya's course, Black continues with ... Qc5 . I don't believe ... Bc5 , as played, is a mistake, but Black will remain a pawn down and should be precise to keep enough compensation.} Bc5 (15... Qc5 {This is Surya's recommendation, bringing the queen out to stop White from castling. I had found some new ideas to try to put Black under some practical pressure, but I do think he should be more or less okay if he knows what he is doing and finds a few good moves.}) 16. Qe2 Rb8 17. Nf2 {Still following my preparation. Here, the best move is surely ... Bd4 , which feels pretty natural. Still, White can put some pressure.} Qb7 {This is a big mistake, but it takes some serious calculation and confidence for White to punish it. How should White continue?} (17... Bd4 {Here, there's an important detail. White has to wait on castling for a move.} 18. O-O {?} Nh5 {Surprisingly, White is completely unable to stop the knight from landing on f4 . Now, Nd5 loses the b2 pawn, and} 19. g3 {would allow} Nxg3 {, followed by ... Qh3 , with a devastating kingside attack. Therefore, Rc1! O-O O-O Now and only now White castles. The point is that he is ready to meet ... Nh5 with Nd5 , pointing out that while b2 may be loose, so is c4 , and Ne7 + is in the air as well. Still, If Black is more patient, I think he is basically okay. For instance, after Qc6 Black is ready for ... Rb3 and ... Rfb8 next, when it will not be easy for White to put his extra pawn to good use.}) 18. O-O {Decent, but not the most incisive.} (18. Qxc4 $1 {was best, not allowing Black the option to change his mind and stick his queen on b4 . In all likelihood, we will transpose to the game, but I was worried about what would happen if Black took on f2 first.} Qxb2 19. O-O {transposes to the game.} Bxf2+ $5 {Kxf2 Qxb2+ and now, Ne2? is tempting, but after Qb6+! Ke1 Ke7 , White will struggle to get his h1 -rook into the game, ... Rhc8 is coming, and Black gets good counterplay. The right move is Kg3! Boldly charging ahead. Nh5+ Kh4 Nf4 I had gotten this far in my calculation, and I did not see a win for White, so I stuck with 18.O-O instead. It was there, however. Qc6+! First White checks on c6 ... Kf7 Qc7+ Kg8 Ra2! This was the idea I missed, stopping any mating ideas with ... g5 + followed by ... Qxg2 . Black's queen is expelled, traded, and he remains a clean pawn down. Qb4 is met by Rb1 and the engine suggests retreating to b6 , but the endgame is grim for Black.}) ({Qa7+!? I did not even consider this move either, which also looks scary. The machine claims White wins following} 18. Kf1 $1 {Rxb2 Nd1! Rb8 Ke2! When I will apparently be in time to fix my pieces with Rc1 - Rc2 , and Ne3 before Black can cause any trouble. It really takes veins of ice to play chess like this.}) 18... Qxb2 {Consistent, but Black will soon enter a world of pain.} ({In hindsight, a move like} 18... Qb4 {would have been better, but I was not bothered during the game. It just felt like a horrible version of the 17...Bd4 line for Black, where he spent 2 tempi to get his queen in front of his rook, plus he let me castle without worrying about ... Nh5 . I knew White would be much better, so I thought allowing this option was not an issue. Still, Black has a fighting chance here, and I did not need to allow it.} 19. Kh1 {and Rfc1 , Rc2 , and Rac1 will come next. White is surely better.}) 19. Qxc4 (19. Rab1 $2 {Tempting but poor. Following} Qxe2 20. Rxb8+ Kd7 $1 21. Rb7+ Kc6 $1 {, Black gets his rook back with a pleasant endgame.}) 19... Bxf2+ 20. Kh1 {Black has won a piece, but he is facing major tactical problems. White is threatening to bring a rook to b1 , ending the game immediately, but what can Black do about it? His king is stuck in the center; his queen cannot vacate the second rank since the bishop on f2 will hang. I did not calculate everything all the way to completion here, but I calculated a lot of lines, and none of them were working for Black. Eventually, I just went with my instinct and trusted there would not be a defense.} Qb3 {The exclamation mark is only for practical value, as Black's position was lost no matter what. But this way forces White to find the most precise moves all the way through, unlike other lines where just about anything wins. I spent almost 20 minutes calculating here, but I was up to the task.} (20... Ba7 21. Rab1 Qc2 22. Rxb8+ Bxb8 23. Qc8+ {White wins heavy material.}) (20... Rb6 {it is hard to believe that placing the rook on a defended square can help. White wins every which way, but I saw a nice, simple one.} 21. a5 $1 {The rook has nowhere to go anyway.}) (20... Rd8 21. Rab1 Qc2 22. Rb7 {Black gets mated.}) (20... Qb4 {This extremely sad move is the machine's choice, but one does not need to be a genius to see a strong continuation for White.} 21. Qxb4 Rxb4 22. Rxf2 {White is a clean pawn up and should win.}) (20... Bb6 21. Ra2 {Black's queen gets trapped.}) 21. Qc7 {A very powerful move, renewing all the threats. Rxf2 , Rab1 , even Qxg7 now- a lot of stuff is hanging in the air. Still, there are some details to take note of.} (21. Qc6+ $2 {This tempting check on c6 would be a huge mistake.} Kf7 $1 {and now} 22. Rxf2 {fails to} Rhc8 {and Black wins a piece. After} 23. Rb1 Qa3 24. Rxb8 Rxb8 25. Qc7+ Kg6 $1 26. Qxb8 Qxc3 $1 {, Black's king has escaped the checks, the bishop on f2 cannot be taken, the dust has settled, and White is in trouble.}) 21... Qb6 (21... Rb7 22. Qc6+ $1 {Now and only now, this check comes. Rab1 is on the way.} Kf7 23. Rab1 {Since Black's rook is on b7 rather than b8 , it will be captured.} ({O-O Now, we need to choose the right rook:  A)} 23. Rfb1 $2 {It might look like either way skewers the rook on b8 , but not so. Rbc8! Qxc8} Qxb1+ $1 {Black wins the fight for desperados since this move comes with check. If White's rook were on f1 here, he could take f8 or even check on e6 , winning easily.  B)} 24. Rxb1 $1 {Rbc8 Qxc8! This desperado wins a rook, leaving White up heavy material.} Rc8 25. Rb3 {or Qxb1 Qe6+ and White wins the queen.})) {The most resilient is Bb6 but White wins the fight of desperados. Qxb8! Qxb1 Qxf8+! Finally, White wins.} 22. Qxe5+ Kf7 {Now, Black is one move away from consolidating and forcing me to resign with a piece less. But a tempo is everything.} 23. Nd5 {White hits the queen and threatens a check on the seventh rank. Black cannot keep the position under control.} Nxd5 (23... Bd4 24. Qe7+ $1 {Nice to have this check! Any other move would lose, but now Black can resign as his queen will be taken next.}) (23... Qa7 {was probably the most resilient, but White wins.} 24. Rxf2 $1 Qxf2 25. Qc7+ $1 {The right check, making sure to leave e7 for the knight.} Kg6 26. Ne7+ $1 Kh5 27. Qe5+ g5 28. Qxf6 {White takes back all of his sacrificed material, and the attack rages on. The machine claims forced mate in 12.}) (23... Rhe8 24. Qxe8+ $1 {White ends up with a rook and 2 excellent pawns against 2 very badly coordinated minor pieces. After} Kxe8 25. Nxb6 Bxb6 26. Rab1 {, Black is absolutely busted.}) 24. Qxd5+ {Black cannot block the check with his queen since the bishop on f2 would be loose at the end. Now it's easy.} Ke7 25. Qe5+ Kf7 26. Rac1 {Black is unable to prevent a disaster on c7 .} ({Rhc8 would allow a fork after the rook exchange: Rxc8 Rxc8} 26. Qf5+ {and White picks up the rook.}) ({Rb7 fails every which way, most simply to} 26. Rxf2 Qxf2 27. Qd5+ {White would remain two pawns up, and Black's king would still be wide open. Sergey understandably resigned.}) *

