{White will win both central pawns after d6 Bxd6 Bxd6 Rxd6} 20... Rac8 {Now, White has a key decision to make. ... Rc8 is not a subtle move, and it looks like ... b5 could be coming next. what would you play?} 21. Rhe1 {Now Black has to prove something very direct, or else he will just be worse due to the structure. It is hard to get ... b5 through since it always allows c6 and a disaster on e6 . It turns out that White can safely ignore the threat of ... b5 for now and simply centralize his rooks. However, this takes some calculation to justify!} b5 {Stockfish claims Black should do this anyway.} (21... Rc7 {It took me some time to understand this move. We'll see it in our next exercise.}) 22. c5 Nf5 {This variation shows why he played ... Rc7 .} (22... b4 {This should be preferred, but Black still is a little worse after} 23. c6 dxc6 24. Nxe6 Nb5 $1 25. Kc1 {, which is better than cxd6 Rxc2 dxe7 Rxg2= .}) 23. c6 Nxd4 {If Black's rook were on c7 here, he could be good to go.} 24. cxb7 Rxc2 (24... Nxc2 {is wrong because Black's rook is hanging. This is why he went 21...Rc7 .}) 25. Bxd4 Rc7 {It looks like Black is at least fine here...} 26. b8=Q {This is clearly the move White wants to make. By now, we should know what to ask!} Rxb8 27. Be5 *

