[PlyCount "8"]

21... Rc7 {It took me some time to understand this move. How should White proceed?} 22. Bc1 {Well calculated. The computer is somewhat agnostic here, but in my humble human opinion, this move is clearly best. White gets ready to regroup the bishop to the menacing b2 and a3 squares and clears the e -file to put more pressure on the hanging pawns. It looks like this move is impossible because Black will be able to successfully execute the ... b5 break. But we have to ask, what if I do it anyway?} b5 (22... a6 {In hindsight, this should have been preferred, but Black is definitely a bit worse in human terms.}) 23. c5 Nf5 {Now, unlike in the previous line, c6 doesn't work. But I had a plan.} 24. Nxf5 (24. c6 Nxd4 25. cxb7 Nxc2 {and the rook is not hanging.}) 24... Rxf5 25. Rxd7 {Based on Nikita's time management, I'm sure he simply missed this move. The point is revealed. It turns out that by bringing the rook to c7 , Black actually was not threatening to play ... b5 at all, thanks to this tactic at the end. This game is a good example of why it's important not to listen to the computer single-mindedly. According to the machine, both White and Black's last few moves have been fine, not necessarily better than any other good moves. But make no mistake: the game is clearly moving in White's favor.} *

