36. a3 {Black still has an uphill battle to fight, and luckily for me, Nikita lost his way here with his clock ticking down. Can you do better?} Rf6 {This is clearly the move Black wants to make, kicking White's queen away from the menacing f4 -square and blocking the long diagonal. It looks like it hangs the rook. But... What happens if I do it anyway?} (36... Re2 $2 37. Rc7 $1 Qe8 38. Qf5 $1 {Controlling the b1 - h7 diagonal and making numerous threats. Now Black is in bad shape. I did not give him another chance to get back into the game.}) (36... Re1+ $2 37. Ka2 Re2 38. Qd4 $1 {The rook coming to d8 next is a problem. White wins.}) 37. Qg4 {This is White's most testing option, but it can be disarmed with accurate play.} (37. Bxf6 Qxd7 {This is obvious enough.}) 37... Rf1+ 38. Ka2 Rf2 39. Qd4 {And now we see the point of having brought the rook specifically to the f2 -square rather than e2 .} Qc5 40. Qxc5 Bxc5 {Black is not mated on the back rank, and he should make a draw without much trouble.} *

