6 $1 Nxd4 $2 {If Black's rook were on c7 here, he could be good to go.} 24. cxb7 Rxc2 {Taking with the knight is wrong because Black's rook is hanging. This is why he went 21...Rc7} 25. Bxd4 Rc7 {It looks like Black is at least fine here...} 26. b8=Q {This is clearly the move White wants to make. By now, we should know what to ask!} Rxb8 27. Be5 {White will win both central pawns after} d6 28. Bxd6 Bxd6 29. Rxd6 {.}) ({b4 should be preferred, but Black still is a little worse after c6 dxc6 Nxe6} 21... Nb5 $1 22. Kc1 {, which is better than cxd6 Rxc2 dxe7 Rxg2= .}) 22. Bc1 {Well calculated. The computer is somewhat agnostic here, but in my humble human opinion, this move is clearly best. White gets ready to regroup the bishop to the menacing b2 and a3 squares and clears the e -file to put more pressure on the hanging pawns. It looks like this move is impossible because Black will be able to successfully execute the ... b5 break. But we have to ask, what if I do it anyway?} b5 (22... a6 {In hindsight, this should have been preferred, but Black is definitely a bit worse in human terms.}) 23. c5 Nf5 {Now unlike in the previous line, c6 doesn't work. But I had a plan.} 24. Nxf5 (24. c6 Nxd4 25. cxb7 Nxc2 {and the rook is not hanging.}) 24... Rxf5 25. Rxd7 {Based on Nikita's time management, I'm sure he simply missed this move. The point is revealed. It turns out that by bringing the rook to c7 , Black actually was not threatening to play ... b5 at all, thanks to this tactic at the end. This game is a good example of why it's important not to listen to the computer single-mindedly. According to the machine, both White and Black's last few moves have been fine, not necessarily better than any other good moves. But make no mistake: the game is clearly moving in White's favor.} Rfxc5 (25... Rxd7 {offered better defensive chances. Stockfish is all-seeing and says Black equalizes with this move. In human terms, does he really? For example, take the following sample variation.} 26. c6 Qc7 27. cxd7 Qxd7 28. Qe4 $1 Kf7 29. g4 {I saw this far and thought I won a pretty clean pawn, and my guess is Nikita felt the same way and chose ... Rxc5 as a result, but Black has good drawing chances here.} Rd5 30. Qxh7 Rd1 31. Rxd1 Qxd1 32. Qh5+ Kf8 33. Qxb5 Qxg4 34. Qd3 {The computer's evaluation here is 0.00 after a move like . Does it look like 0.00? White is a pawn up, will not lose it, and Black certainly does not have a clean way to save the game.}) 26. Rxc7 Rxc7 27. Qe2 Rc6 28. f5 Bb4 29. Rd1 (29. Rf1 {This strange computer move was best, and White is clearly better.}) (29. Bd2 {was also very tempting, but I could not quite make it work.} Bxd2 30. Qxd2 Qe7 31. fxe6 Rxe6 32. Qd5 Kf7 {I spent a long time looking for a win here, with no success. It felt like one should be there, but I could not find it. For example, I considered} 33. Kc2 {White is threatening to go to a pawn ending next move. The machine claims 33... a6 ! will hold. I won't argue. Instead,} Qd6 $2 34. Qxe6+ Qxe6 35. Rxe6 Kxe6 36. Kc3 {is dead lost. However, I could not bust} a5 $5 {, though now the computer points out a very nice win with Qh5+! Kg8 and now and only now Rxe6 Qxe6 Qxb5 Qe4+ Kc3 since Black cannot take on g2 due to the mate. Provoking the king to e8 was important! Even this still requires some work, though. After the text, Black is in a very tough spot, but he can keep the fight going if he plays correctly.}) 29... exf5 {The best practical try. This move looks insane. Who in their right mind would allow the queen to reach e8 ? I did not take it very seriously. But we always ask... What if I do it anyway? It turns out White does not have a clean win.} (29... Qe7 $2 30. Qxb5 Rb6 31. Qd7) 30. Qe8+ Bf8 31. Rd7 (31. Rd8 Rf6) 31... Qc8 32. Qf7+ Kh8 33. Bb2 Rg6 {At first, I thought I was winning on the spot with Rc7 followed by Qxf5 , but I missed Rg5 . Black's position looks very scary, but White does not have a win. I was quite annoyed to realize that I would have to win the game all over again!} 34. g3 (34. Rc7 Qd8 35. Qxf5 Rg5 $1 {And Black is fine. I noticed this resource only after ... Rg6 was on the board. Apparently, Rxg2 holds too.} 36. Rc8 {Rg1+ Bc1 Rf1= .}) 34... f4 35. Qxf4 Re6 36. a3 {Making luft. Black still has an uphill battle to fight, and luckily for me, Nikita lost his way here with his clock ticking down. Can you do better?} Re2 (36... Re1+ $2 37. Ka2 Re2 38. Qd4 $1 {The rook coming to d8 next is a problem. White wins.} ({Rf6! is clearly the move Black wants to make, kicking White's queen away from the menacing f4 -square and blocking the long diagonal. It looks like it hangs the rook. But... What happens if I do it anyway? Bxf6 Qxd7= is obvious enough. Instead,} 38. Qg4 $1 {is White's most testing option, but it can be disarmed with accurate play. Rf1+! Ka2} Rf2 39. Qd4 {and now we see the point of having brought the rook specifically to the f2 -square rather than e2 .} Qc5 $1 40. Qxc5 Bxc5 {Black is not mated on the back rank, and he should make a draw without much trouble.})) 37. Rc7 Qe8 38. Qf5 {Controlling the b1 - h7 diagonal and making numerous threats. Now, Black is in bad shape. I did not give him another chance to get back into the game.} Kg8 (38... Qg6 39. Qxg6 {wins for White:} hxg6 40. Rxa7 {. My plan was, however, Bxg7+! Bxg7 Rc8+ Bf8 Qxg6 hxg6 Rxf8+ Kg7 Ra8 and White should win.}) (38... Rxh2 39. Rc8 {illustrates White's main threat.}) 39. Rxa7 {Finally, I grab some material.} Qe4+ {Losing immediately, but Black's position was probably lost anyway.} (39... Rxh2 $2 40. Qd5+ Kh8 41. Bxg7+ {Well spotted.} Bxg7 42. Ra8 {is curtains.}) (39... Rd2 {Maybe Black can still fight, but I find it hard to believe he won't lose.}) 40. Qxe4 Rxe4 41. Rb7 Re1+ 42. Ka2 Re2 43. Rxb5 Be7 44. Kb1 {Not falling for the last trick.} (44. Re5 $2 Rxb2+ $1 {Oops!}) 44... Re1+ 45. Kc2 Re2+ 46. Kc1 Rxh2 {Now Black threatens a perpetual, but clearing the b3 -square is quite enough.} 47. b4 (47. a4 Rh1+ {My king will not escape.}) 47... h5 48. a4 Rh1+ 49. Kc2 Rh2+ 50. Kb3 Rh3 51. Bc3 {The final finesse. Black should not be allowed to play ... h4 .} (51. a5 h4 {White is still winning, but it's much easier to see something going wrong here.}) 51... Rxg3 52. Rxh5 g5 53. Rh6 Bf8 54. Rc6 g4 55. a5 {I was glad to use the Shankland Method to pick up a final-round victory, although I must admit, the game was extremely sloppy in the beginning.} *

