26. f4 {What should Black do?} d4 {Thematically, the move Black clearly wants to make is ... d4 . It is indeed best, but he should be careful to follow it up accurately and with the right move order.} 27. Qxd4 Nxe5 {An incredibly powerful move order. By not starting with ... Be2 , Black forces White to take the knight. It looks absurd because it loses a piece, but we must ask: What happens if I do it anyway?} (27... Be2 $2 {lets the advantage go. The rest of the game was not too noteworthy.}) 28. Qxe5 Be2 {Despite the piece up, White is collapsing on the spot if he does not play Re1 .} 29. Qc5 {Now, direct moves come all the way through.} (29. Re1 {This is objectively best, but not hard for Black to play against.} Rxd2 {Black has reached the position I was aiming for. White has a tough defense ahead.}) (29. Kf2 Bxd1 30. Rxd1 Qa7+ 31. Ke2 Qxa3 {.}) (29. Bf3 Bxd1 30. Rxd1 Qxa3 {.}) 29... Bxd1 30. Rxd1 Qa4 31. Bf3 (31. Qc1 Qd4+ 32. Kh1 Rd7 {White is stuck in an immortal and decisive pin on the d -file.}) 31... Rd3 {Followed by ... Rcd8 , and Black takes everything.} *

