1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. g3 Bb4+ 5. Bd2 Be7 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O c6 8. Qc2 b6 9. Rd1 Nbd7 10. b3 h6 11. Bc3 Bb7 12. Nbd2 Qc7 13. Rac1 Rac8 14. Qb2 Qb8 15. Ne5 Nxe5 16. dxe5 Nd7 17. b4 Rfd8 18. a3 Qa8 19. e4 Nf8 20. c5 bxc5 21. bxc5 Bxc5 22. Ba5 Bb6 23. Bxb6 axb6 24. Qxb6 Nd7 25. Qe3 Ba6 26. f4 {What should Black do?} d4 {Thematically, the move Black clearly wants to make is ... d4 . It is indeed best, but he should be careful to follow it up accurately and with the right move order.} 27. Qxd4 Be2 {This lets the advantage go.} (27... Nxe5 $3 {An incredibly powerful move order. By not starting with ... Be2 , Black forces White to take the knight. It looks absurd because it loses a piece, but we must ask: What happens if I do it anyway?} 28. Qxe5 Be2 $1 {Despite the piece up, White is collapsing on the spot if he does not play Re1 :  A)} 29. Kf2 Bxd1 30. Rxd1 Qa7+ 31. Ke2 Qxa3) {B) Bf3 Bxd1 Rxd1 Qxa3-+ . C) Qc5 Now, direct moves come all the way through. Bxd1! Rxd1 Qa4! Qc1 Qd4+ Kh1 Rd7-+ and White is stuck in an immortal and decisive pin on the d -file, or Bf3 Rd3-+ followed by ... Rcd8 , and Black takes everything. D)  Finally, Re1 is objectively best, but not hard for Black to play against. Rxd2∓ Black has reached the position I was aiming for. White has a tough defense ahead.} 28. Re1 Qxa3 (28... Nxe5 {was my original plan, intending to meet} 29. Qxe5 {with} Rxd2 {.}) ({However, once Re1 was on the board, I noticed two flaws:  A) Qe3 This is a draw directly, again due to Black playing ... Be2 before Nxe5 . Ng4 Qxe2} 28... Qa7 {Black only has a perpetual after} 29. Kh1 {Nf2 .}) ({B) Qc3! The hanging bishop on e2 ruins the day, and Black is borderline worse.} 28... Qa7 29. Kh1 {Nd3 Rxe2 Nxc1 Qxc1⩲ but not Rd3? Qc2! No ghosts. White wins.}) 29. Rc3 {The moment to strike has passed, and White is surely fine here. The rest of the game was not too noteworthy.} Qa6 30. Nb3 Bb5 31. Ra1 Qb6 32. Qxb6 Nxb6 33. Bf1 g6 34. Bxb5 cxb5 35. Rxc8 Rxc8 36. Ra5 Rc4 37. Rxb5 Na4 38. Na5 Rc5 39. Rb8+ Kg7 40. Ra8 Nb6 41. Ra6 Rc1+ 42. Kf2 Ra1 43. Rxb6 Rxa5 44. Rb2 *

