1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. c4 e6 4. Nc3 c6 5. Bg5 dxc4 6. e4 b5 7. e5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Nxg5 hxg5 10. Bxg5 Be7 (10... Nbd7 {is my recommendation and, in my view, the best move, but Be7 has some surprise value.}) 11. exf6 Bxf6 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. g3 Bb7 14. Bg2 Na6 15. Ne4 Qe7 16. O-O O-O-O 17. h4 (17. a4 {is comfortably met by} f5 {and the knight can't jump to g5 .}) 17... c5 18. a4 Rxd4 19. Qe2 b4 {All of this is heavy computerized theory. Black's position is very suspect, but as far as I can tell, it holds together with accurate play. My opponent's next move came as a surprise.} 20. Rac1 Bd5 21. Rfd1 {I had not remembered seeing this setup before, but when I got back to the room after the game, I found that I had it all the way to 23... Nb8 . It can be tough to keep everything memorized!} Rxd1+ 22. Rxd1 Rd8 23. Rc1 Nb8 (23... Qb7 {had been played, but} 24. Rxc4 {looks extremely dangerous for Black.}) 24. Qe3 (24. Rxc4 Bxc4 25. Qxc4 Rd4 26. Qe2 c4 {is not gonna work for White.}) 24... Nc6 25. Nxc5 Qf6 26. b3 {What would you play here?} *

