1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. c4 e6 4. Nc3 c6 5. Bg5 dxc4 6. e4 b5 7. e5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Nxg5 hxg5 10. Bxg5 Be7 (10... Nbd7 {is my recommendation and, in my view, the best move, but Be7 has some surprise value.}) 11. exf6 Bxf6 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. g3 Bb7 14. Bg2 Na6 15. Ne4 Qe7 16. O-O O-O-O 17. h4 (17. a4 {is comfortably met by} f5 {and the knight can't jump to g5 .}) 17... c5 18. a4 Rxd4 19. Qe2 b4 {All of this is heavy computerized theory. Black's position is very suspect, but as far as I can tell, it holds together with accurate play. My opponent's next move came as a surprise.} 20. Rac1 Bd5 21. Rfd1 {I had not remembered seeing this setup before, but when I got back to the room after the game, I found that I had it all the way to 23... Nb8 . It can be tough to keep everything memorized!} Rxd1+ 22. Rxd1 Rd8 23. Rc1 Nb8 (23... Qb7 {had been played, but} 24. Rxc4 {looks extremely dangerous for Black.}) 24. Qe3 (24. Rxc4 Bxc4 25. Qxc4 Rd4 26. Qe2 c4 {is not gonna work for White.}) 24... Nc6 25. Nxc5 Qf6 26. b3 {What would you play here?} c3 {This is obviously the move Black wants to play. It looks like it is begging to get checkmated by Qe2 - Qa6 . But we know what to ask!} 27. Qe2 Qd4 {Again, what happens if I do it anyway? Black clearly wants to centralize the queen, and we now see that, in fact, the king is not in any danger at all. He will safely hide on b6 .} 28. Qb5 (28. Qa6+ Kc7 29. Qb7+ Kd6 {Black's king is safe as can be, and he will win the game with the c3 -pawn.}) 28... Kc7 (28... Rd6 {was fine, too, but I like this one more. It plays in the same spirit as Black would have to if White had checked on a6 the previous move instead. The king is totally safe, and White is lost.}) 29. Na6+ Kd6 30. Bxd5 exd5 (30... Qxd5 {This was easier, but my move wasn't that terrible either.}) 31. a5 (31. Qb7 {is met by} Qb6 {.}) 31... Re8 32. Qb7 Re7 33. Qc8 Qd2 {White has no checks, and that's all she wrote.} 34. Nb8 (34. Rf1 c2 35. Nb8 c1=Q {is over.}) 34... Qxc1+ 35. Kg2 Nxb8 36. Qxb8+ Kc5 37. Qc8+ Kd4 {This was the only decisive game in the USA-Armenia match from the penultimate round of the 2018 Olympiad, setting us up for a final round clash with China for first. That one was split 2-2, and tiebreaks did not go our way. It's true what they say: winning an Olympic Silver can be damn painful!} *

