{Some of the notes from this game are copied from my annotations for New in Chess back in 2018, but I made sure to update them and give specific explanations of how the Shankland Method works at the critical moment where the reader will have to find the best continuation. I considered omitting the notes altogether but felt it would do a disservice to an interesting game.} 1. c4 e6 2. g3 d5 3. Bg2 g6 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. d4 Nc6 6. O-O Nge7 7. e3 O-O 8. Nc3 b6 9. Qe2 Ba6 ({Better is} 9... a5 10. b3 {.}) 10. b3 dxc4 {Everything up to here was preparation, but now I was on my own. I realized I should not be trying to take the pawn back directly since it would allow ... Na5 followed by ... c5 , and instead, I developed my pieces while leaving bxc4 as a possibility for later.} 11. Ba3 {Making use of the pin. Of course, Black cannot take on b3 .} (11. bxc4 $2 {Taking the pawn back right away is far too hasty. Black has good play after} Na5 $1 12. Nb5 {when} Nxc4 $5 {is possible but better is c6} 13. Na3 Rc8 {with ... c5 to follow.}) 11... Re8 {Black did not have many other options. Trying to hold onto the c4 pawn would have led to dire consequences.} (11... cxb3 $2 12. Qxa6) (11... Na5 $2 {loses a piece to} 12. b4 $1 {with b5 to follow.}) (11... b5 $2 12. Ne4 $1 {leaves black with horribly weak squares on the queenside, and he will still likely lose a pawn over there.}) 12. Rac1 {Remaining patient and dealing with the 'threat' of ... Nd5 .} (12. Rfd1 Nd5 {is fine for Black.}) 12... Qd7 13. Rfd1 Rad8 {At long last, it is time to take the pawn back. It makes sense since White's pieces have now found all of their best squares, but I was still hoping to take with a piece on c4 . Luckily, I managed to resist the temptation to play Nd2 .} 14. bxc4 (14. Nd2 {This move was very tempting to me, trying to take on c4 with the knight and keep the c -file open, but it is incorrect. Black has several good tries here, but the one that concerned me the most was} e5 $5 15. d5 Nd4 $1 16. exd4 exd4 {with obvious counterplay.}) 14... Qc8 (14... Na5 {The typical move, attacking the c4 pawn to try to get ... c5 in, now fails to achieve its desired result, for example, after} 15. Ne5 Qc8 ({(} 15... Bxe5 $2 {is even worse:} 16. dxe5 Qc8 17. Ne4 {)}) 16. Nb5 {when Black's position looks very unpleasant after} c5 17. Nxa7 {or c6 Nxa7 .}) 15. Nb5 Na5 {I had several good options here, but I took a positional approach.} 16. Nd2 {The knight is heading to b3 .} (16. Bb4 $1 {It was even stronger to challenge the a5 -knight immediately, pointing out that after something like} Nec6 17. Bc3 {, the bishop is better placed on c3 , where it can support either e3 - e4 or d4 - d5 , while the knight on c6 is misplaced because it prevents Black from playing ... c6 . This was a bit more to the point than my chosen move.}) 16... Qd7 {I suspect this was based on a miscalculation. Now, White has to be accurate to show an edge.} ({Still, even after the best move, Black is just worse. Following} 16... Qb8 17. Nb3 Nxb3 18. axb3 Bb7 19. Nc3 {, White's big center promises him a pleasant advantage with no risk. Black faces a long and difficult defensive task.}) *

