[PlyCount "6"]

16... Qd7 {I suspect this was based on a miscalculation. Now, White has to be accurate to show an edge.} 17. Nb3 {This is clearly the move White wants to make, pressuring the annoying a5 -knight and trying to fix the queenside structure. Additionally, my last move was Nd2 . It looks like a bad idea because Black can immediately equalize by taking on c4 . But we have to ask: what if I do it anyway?} Nxc4 {This was not played, but it is the critical move that White needs to calculate.} (17... Nxb3 {A sad necessity.} 18. axb3 {Modern engines claim White is objectively winning. I find this to be a bit of an exaggeration in human practice as Black hasn't lost material yet, but his position is definitely miserable.}) 18. Bxe7 {An important resource that must be seen in advance. It might look a little unnatural to give the bishop like this, but concretely, it wins the game. Black loses material.} (18. Nxa7 $2 {This is the right idea, but White needs to take on e7 first. Now,} c5 {also works, but stronger is Nd5!} 19. Rxc4 {c5-+ The tables have turned and turned a lot. In fact, White might as well resign.}) (18. Qxc4 {can be met by} c6 {or Bxb5 intending to meet Qxc7 with Nd5 .}) 18... Rxe7 19. Nxa7 {Black cannot deal with the twin threats of Rxc4 and Nc6 .} *

