[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "2brr1k1/2p2pbp/pp4p1/1qPPp3/1P6/B3P1P1/2Q2PBP/2RR2K1 b - - 0 25"]
[PlyCount "8"]

25... e4 {Should White take on e4 or not?} 26. Bf1 {Black's queen is forced to the extremely inconvenient e7 -square, where she will block in the c8 -bishop and be harassed by white's advancing central pawns.} (26. Bxe4 $2 {Tempting as it is to take the pawn, White does not manage to consolidate, and the Shankland Method does not work here.} bxc5 $1 27. bxc5 Qa5 $1 {There is nothing to be done about the a3 -bishop hanging.}) (26. cxb6 $6 {Including a capture on b6 first before taking e4 does not help either, since after} cxb6 27. Bxe4 $2 {Black now can play} Bg4 28. f3 Bxf3 $1 29. Bxf3 Rxe3 {.}) 26... Qd7 27. d6 {White has a simple but completely crushing threat of c6 , which compels Black to make an undesirable exchange on c5 .} bxc5 (27... cxd6 {is even worse since after} 28. Rxd6 {the b6 -pawn falls, too.}) 28. bxc5 cxd6 29. Rxd6 {Correctly choosing the c -file for the passed pawn. It will easily get to c6 , and then advancing to c7 is far easier than advancing to d7 would have been.} ({White is much better after} 29. cxd6 {as well, but the text is much more convincing.}) *

