[ECO "A13"]
[PlyCount "83"]

{Some of the notes from this game are copied from my annotations for New in Chess back in 2018, but I made sure to update them and give specific explanations of how the Shankland Method works at the critical moment where the reader will have to find the best continuation. I considered omitting the notes altogether but felt it would do a disservice to an interesting game.} 1. c4 e6 2. g3 d5 3. Bg2 g6 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. d4 Nc6 6. O-O Nge7 7. e3 O-O 8. Nc3 b6 9. Qe2 Ba6 ({Better is} 9... a5 10. b3 {.}) 10. b3 dxc4 {Everything up to here was preparation, but now I was on my own. I realized I should not be trying to take the pawn back directly since it would allow ... Na5 followed by ... c5 and instead develop my pieces while leaving bxc4 as a possibility for later.} 11. Ba3 {Making use of the pin. Of course, Black cannot take on b3 .} (11. bxc4 $2 {Taking the pawn back right away is far too hasty. Black has good play after} Na5 $1 12. Nb5 {when} Nxc4 $5 {is possible but better is c6} 13. Na3 Rc8 {with ... c5 to follow.}) 11... Re8 {Black did not have many other options. Trying to hold onto the c4 pawn would have led to dire consequences.} (11... cxb3 $2 12. Qxa6) (11... Na5 $2 {loses a piece to} 12. b4 $1 {with b5 to follow.}) (11... b5 $2 12. Ne4 $1 {leaves black with horribly weak squares on the queenside, and he will still likely lose a pawn over there.}) 12. Rac1 {Remaining patient and dealing with the 'threat' of Nd5 .} (12. Rfd1 Nd5) 12... Qd7 13. Rfd1 Rad8 {At long last, it is time to take the pawn back. It makes sense since White's pieces have now found all of their best squares, but I was still hoping to take with a piece on c4 . Luckily, I managed to resist the temptation to play Nd2 .} 14. bxc4 (14. Nd2 {This move was very tempting to me, trying to take on c4 with the knight and keep the c -file open, but it is incorrect. Black has several good tries here, but the one that concerned me the most was} e5 $5 15. d5 Nd4 $1 16. exd4 exd4 {with obvious counterplay.}) 14... Qc8 (14... Na5 {The typical move, attacking the c4 pawn to try to get ... c5 in, now fails to achieve its desired result, for example, after} 15. Ne5 Qc8 ({(} 15... Bxe5 $2 {is even worse:} 16. dxe5 Qc8 17. Ne4 {)}) 16. Nb5 {when Black's position looks very unpleasant after} c5 17. Nxa7 {or c6 Nxa7 .}) 15. Nb5 Na5 {I had several good options here, but I took a positional approach.} 16. Nd2 {The knight is heading to b3 .} (16. Bb4 $1 {It was even stronger to challenge the a5 -knight immediately, pointing out that after something like} Nec6 17. Bc3 {, the bishop is better placed on c3 , where it can support either e3 - e4 or d4 - d5 , while the knight on c6 is misplaced because it prevents Black from playing ... c6 . This was a bit more to the point than my chosen move.}) 16... Qd7 {I suspect this was based on a miscalculation. Now, White has to be accurate to show an edge.} ({Still, even after the best move, Black is just worse. Following} 16... Qb8 17. Nb3 Nxb3 18. axb3 Bb7 19. Nc3 {, White's big center promises him a pleasant advantage with no risk. Black faces a long and difficult defensive task.}) 17. Nb3 {This is clearly the move White wants to make, pressuring the annoying a5 -knight and trying to fix the queenside structure. Additionally, my last move was Nd2 . It looks like a bad idea because Black can immediately equalize by taking on c4 . But we have to ask: what if I do it anyway?} Nxb3 {A sad necessity.} (17... Nxc4 $2 {This was not played, but it is the critical move that White needs to calculate. Now,} 18. Qxc4 {can be met by} c6 {or Bxb5 intending to meet Qxc7 with Nd5 .}) ({Nxa7? is the right idea, but White needs to take on e7 first. Now,} 17... c5 {also works, but stronger is Nd5! Rxc4} 18. dxc5 {The tables have turned and turned a lot. In fact, White might as well resign.}) {Bxe7! An important resource that must be seen in advance. It might look a little unnatural to give the bishop like this, but concretely, it wins the game. Black loses material. Rxe7 Nxa7! Black cannot deal with the twin threats of Rxc4 and Nc6 .} 18. axb3 {Modern engines claim White is objectively winning. I find this to be a bit of an exaggeration in human practice as Black hasn't lost material yet, but his position is definitely miserable.} Nc8 19. d5 {Not the only good move, but the most direct one.} e5 {Now White definitely should not allow ... Bxb5 .} ({The immediate} 19... Bxb5 {didn't work well:} 20. cxb5 e5 {Black would surely have liked to get a position like this one, and if he can play ... Bf8 -... Bd6 , he will have decent defensive chances. But, White gets there first:} 21. d6 $1 {The threat of Bc6 is decisive.}) 20. Nc3 Nd6 21. Qa2 {The threat of Bxd6 forces Black's bishop back, which lets the pawns start running.} Bc8 22. b4 {With c5 incoming.} a6 23. c5 Nb5 24. Nxb5 Qxb5 (24... axb5 {I was expecting this move, trying to blunt the a3 -bishop, but Black's position still looks awful.}) 25. Qc2 e4 {Should White take on e4 or not?} 26. Bf1 {Black's queen is forced to the extremely inconvenient e7 -square, where she will block in the c8 -bishop and be harassed by white's advancing central pawns.} (26. Bxe4 $2 {Tempting as it is to take the pawn, White does not manage to consolidate, and the Shankland Method does not work here.} bxc5 $1 27. bxc5 Qa5 $1 {There is nothing to be done about the a3 -bishop hanging.}) (26. cxb6 $6 {Including a capture on b6 first before taking e4 does not help either, since after} cxb6 27. Bxe4 $2 {, Black now can play} Bg4 28. f3 Bxf3 $1 29. Bxf3 Rxe3 {.}) 26... Qd7 27. d6 {White has a simple but completely crushing threat of c6 , which compels Black to make an undesirable exchange on c5 .} bxc5 (27... cxd6 {is even worse since after} 28. Rxd6 {, the b6 -pawn falls, too}) 28. bxc5 cxd6 29. Rxd6 {Correctly choosing the c -file for the passed pawn. It will easily get to c6 , and then advancing to c7 is far easier than advancing to d7 would have been.} ({White is much better after} 29. cxd6 {as well, but the text is much more convincing.}) 29... Qc7 30. Rxd8 Qxd8 (30... Rxd8 31. Qxe4 {Is obviously lost for Black.}) 31. Qa4 {White is winning. The c -pawn is incredibly strong, and the a6 pawn should just drop. He also has a crushing threat that my opponent seems to have missed, but that only made the game shorter. The result would not have changed if he had played a better move.} h5 32. Rd1 {Now White gains a lot of tempi. The queen is hit and must stay in touch with the e8 -rook.} Qe7 33. c6 {Another tempo, and c7 is coming next.} Qc7 34. Bd6 Qb6 35. c7 Re6 36. Bf4 {Black cannot stop Rd8 +. I had to be a little careful, but a few accurate moves finished the game very quickly.} g5 37. Rd8+ Kh7 38. Rxc8 gxf4 39. Qd7 {No ghosts. Black's kingside counterplay is not so intimidating, and White queens the pawn right away.} fxe3 40. Rb8 exf2+ 41. Kg2 Qe3 42. Qxf7 {The last accurate move, stopping ... Qf3 +. Black cannot prevent a new queen from showing up, so he resigned.} *

