{I was very happy to make good use of The Shankland Method in one of the most important games of my life: the one that made me US Champion.} 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Bf4 Bg4 7. Qb3 e5 8. h3 {White ignores the threat to his bishop and threatens the other one! Black's position is already very dangerous since he must acquiesce to the bishop trade, compromising his structure and opening the h -file. Nowadays, this position is well-known, but it was quite fresh at the time.} (8. dxe5 Nh5 {is Black's point.}) 8... exf4 {Black was right that he needed to take on f4 , but he could have done himself a favor by including ... Na5 first.} (8... Na5 $1 {White can't go for} 9. Qa4+ Bd7 10. Bb5 exf4 {and has to play} 11. Qc2 {exf4 hxg4} Ng4 $1 {Black gets at least a pawn for his suffering, but I would still prefer White's position.} 12. Kf1 {was my intention in the game.}) (8... Bd7 9. Bxe5 Qe7 10. Nf3 {now that the bishop has left.}) (8... Bh5 9. Qxb7 exf4 10. Qxc6+ {and there's no ... Bd7 .}) 9. hxg4 Qe7+ 10. Kf1 O-O-O 11. Nd2 {White is a bit better, and his position is very easy to play.} g6 12. Re1 Qc7 13. g5 Nh5 14. Be2 {I think it's quite important to push the knight off of h5 so that Black cannot get ... h6 in.} (14. Ngf3 h6 {looked much less convincing to me.}) 14... Ng7 15. Ngf3 Ne6 {Black has chances to attack the g5 -pawn, but White's play is too fast.} *

