[FEN "2kr1b1r/ppq2pnp/2n3p1/3p2P1/3P1p2/1QP2N2/PP1NBPP1/4RK1R b - - 0 15"]
[PlyCount "8"]

15... Ne6 {Black has chances to attack the g5 pawn. How should White proceed?} 16. Bb5 {Fighting for control of the e5 -square. Black is unable to safely go after the g5 -pawn.} Be7 {Going after the g5 -pawn does not solve Black's problems.} ({The game continued} 16... Bg7 $6 {There wasn't anything great, but I really dislike putting the bishop on such a passive square that is so easily blunted by White's dark square pawn chain.} 17. Qa4 $1 {White's position is almost picture perfect, but he has one bad piece- the knight on d2 . I played this move both to threaten Bxc6 when Black would be forced to worsen his structure with ... bxc6 , but also to clear the b3 -square so the knight can use it to jump into c5 or reroute to d3 via c1 .}) 17. Qa4 (17. Bxc6 Qxc6 18. Ne5 Qa6+ {is not working.}) 17... Nxg5 18. Bxc6 bxc6 ({Or} 18... Qxc6 19. Qxc6+ bxc6 20. Rxe7 {.}) 19. Ne5 {Black will lose the c6 pawn, and he is clearly worse.} ({Rd6} 19. Nb3 {should be the best option, not worrying about getting the pawn back and instead bringing the knight to c5 . Black's king is about to face a serious attack. c4!? is another possibility. My plan during the game was Nxc6 , but the computer is not too impressed and finds a more barbaric move. Qxc6 Qxc6+ Rxc6 Rxe7⩲ looks really unpleasant for Black, but he is still fighting.}) *

