21... Qd8 {White has a dream position, but material is still equal, and we should take the chance to finish Black off while it is there. How should White continue?} 22. Ne5 {This is the Shankland Method at its finest. White absolutely wants to play this move, but he can't because the g5 -pawn hangs, and also, Black can trade his horrible bishop on g7 for a really good knight on e5 . But we have to ask: What if I do it anyway? The computer claims that White is totally winning after Rh3 or Rh4 as well, basically doing nothing. This would be a step in the wrong direction. Knock the guy out when you have a chance!} Qc7 (22... Nxg5 23. Nec6 {Black is crushed. White has way too many threats, including but not limited to Nxd8 +, Qxa7 +, and Re7 +.}) (22... Bxe5 23. dxe5 $1 {and we see White's point: Black could not take on e5 because he gets his rook trapped.} Nc5 {Now, once again, White is winning with more or less any sensible move, but there is only one that really puts the matter beyond all doubt.} 24. exd6 $1 {We want to take the rook, but the queen was just attacked. So... what if I do it anyway?} Nxa4 25. Re7+ $1 {and nothing works:  A)} Qxe7 26. dxe7 {is winning for White.  B)} Kc8 {Rc7+! Qxc7 dxc7+- Material has been equalized and Black will lose a piece thanks to having two hanging knights.  C) Ka8} 27. Nc6 $1 Nxc6 28. Bxc6 Kb8 {Rb7+ Ka8 Rxb6 or Kc8 d7+ crushing.}) 23. Qb3 {The final finesse. White simply attacks the d5 -pawn, but it cannot be defended without losing control of f7 or h7 .} *

