{I was very happy to make good use of The Shankland Method in one of the most important games of my life: the one that made me US Champion.} 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Bf4 Bg4 7. Qb3 e5 8. h3 {White ignores the threat to his bishop and threatens the other one! Black's position is already very dangerous since he must acquiesce to the bishop trade, compromising his structure and opening the h -file. Nowadays, this position is well-known, but it was quite fresh at the time.} (8. dxe5 Nh5 {is Black's point.}) 8... exf4 {Black was right that he needed to take on f4 , but he could have done himself a favor by including ... Na5 first.} (8... Na5 $1 {White can't go for} 9. Qa4+ Bd7 10. Bb5 exf4 {and has to play} 11. Qc2 {exf4 hxg4} Ng4 $1 {Black gets at least a pawn for his suffering, but I would still prefer White's position.} 12. Kf1 {was my intention in the game.}) (8... Bd7 9. Bxe5 Qe7 10. Nf3 {now that the bishop has left.}) (8... Bh5 9. Qxb7 exf4 10. Qxc6+ {and there's no ... Bd7 .}) 9. hxg4 Qe7+ 10. Kf1 O-O-O 11. Nd2 {White is a bit better, and his position is very easy to play.} g6 12. Re1 Qc7 13. g5 Nh5 14. Be2 {I think it's quite important to push the knight off of h5 so that Black cannot get ... h6 in.} (14. Ngf3 h6 {looked much less convincing to me.}) 14... Ng7 15. Ngf3 Ne6 {Black has chances to attack the g5 -pawn, but White's play is too fast.} 16. Bb5 {Fighting for control of the e5 -square. Black is unable to safely go after the g5 -pawn.} Bg7 {There wasn't anything great, but I really dislike putting the bishop on such a passive square that is so easily blunted by White's dark square pawn chain.} ({Going after the g5 -pawn does not solve Black's problems. After} 16... Be7 17. Qa4 $1 ({(} 17. Bxc6 Qxc6 18. Ne5 Qa6+ {is not working.)}) 17... Nxg5 18. Bxc6 Qxc6 19. Qxc6+ {neither} bxc6 20. Rxe7 {nor bxc6 Ne5! are nice. Black will lose the c6 pawn, and he is clearly worse.} Rd6 21. Nb3 {should be the best option, not worrying about getting the pawn back and instead bringing the knight to c5 . Black's king is about to face a serious attack. c4!? is another possibility. My plan during the game was Nxc6 , but the computer is not too impressed and finds a more barbaric move. Qxc6 Qxc6+ Rxc6 Rxe7⩲ looks really unpleasant for Black, but he is still fighting.}) 17. Qa4 {White's position is almost picture perfect, but he has one bad piece- the knight on d2 . I played this move both to threaten Bxc6 when Black would be forced to worsen his structure with ... bxc6 , but also to clear the b3 -square so the knight can use it to jump into c5 or reroute to d3 via c1 .} Rd6 {Black prevents me from dragging the b -pawn to c6 .} (17... a6 $2 {The machine's recommendation is beyond depressing.} 18. Bxc6 Qxc6 19. Qxc6+ bxc6 20. b4 {This looks absolutely awful for Black.}) 18. Nb3 {Simple and strong. White improves his pieces.} (18. Bxc6 $2 Rxc6 $1 19. Qxa7 $4 Ra6 {Is the tactical point behind ... Rd6 .}) (18. b4 $2 {The machine prefers the direct attack, but I prefer my move. There is no need to leave a weakness on c3 when a simple move proves a very pleasant advantage.}) 18... b6 {A poor strategic decision. Black's queenside light squares will now be chronically weak, and the b3 -knight still has a clear route back into the game.} (18... a6 19. Bxc6 Rxc6 {and now} 20. Nc1 {was my intention in the game, but the computer likes} Nc5 {Nxc5} 21. dxc5 {.}) 19. Nc1 Nb8 20. Nd3 Kb7 21. Nb4 {Stopping a6 and further attacking the weak light squares.} Qd8 {White has a dream position, but material is still equal, and we should take the chance to finish Black off while it is there. How should White continue?} ({The machine wants to expel White's knight immediately with} 21... a5 {, but after} 22. Nd3 {Black no longer can play ... a6 , so the bishop will be on b5 for the rest of the game, dominating the b8 -knight. Furthermore, b4 is on the cards. Black is dead lost.}) 22. Ne5 {This is the Shankland Method at its finest. White absolutely wants to play this move, but he can't because the g5 -pawn hangs, and also, Black can trade his horrible bishop on g7 for a really good knight on e5 . But we have to ask: What if I do it anyway? The computer claims that White is totally winning after Rh3 or Rh4 as well, basically doing nothing. This would be a step in the wrong direction. Knock the guy out when you have a chance!} Qc7 (22... Nxg5 23. Nec6 {Black is crushed. White has way too many threats, including but not limited to Nxd8 +, Qxa7 +, and Re7 +.}) (22... Bxe5 23. dxe5 $1 {and we see White's point: Black could not take on e5 because he gets his rook trapped.} Nc5 {Now, once again, White is winning with more or less any sensible move, but there is only one that really puts the matter beyond all doubt.} 24. exd6 $1 {We want to take the rook, but the queen was just attacked. So... what if I do it anyway?} Nxa4 25. Re7+ $1 {and nothing works:  A)} Qxe7 26. dxe7 {is winning for White.  B)} Kc8 {Rc7+! Qxc7 dxc7+- Material has been equalized and Black will lose a piece thanks to having two hanging knights.  C) Ka8} 27. Nc6 $1 Nxc6 28. Bxc6 Kb8 {Rb7+ Ka8 Rxb6 or Kc8 d7+ crushing.}) 23. Qb3 {The final finesse. White simply attacks the d5 pawn, but it cannot be defended without losing control of f7 or h7 .} Rhd8 (23... Bxe5 24. Rxe5 {when d5 will fall, and Black will lose.}) (23... Qd8 24. Nxf7 $1) 24. Rxh7 {The last piece enters the game with authority. In addition to all of Black's previous problems, he is now a clean pawn down as well.} a6 {Other moves wouldn't help matters:} (24... Nxg5 25. Rxg7 {.}) (24... Bxe5 25. dxe5 {.}) 25. Bd3 Ka7 {Black wanted to get a5 in but was unable to do so.} (25... a5 $2 26. Nxd5 $1 Rxd5 27. Be4 {White wins the house.} Qd6 28. Nxf7 Qd7 29. Nxd8+ Nxd8 30. Qxd5+ Qxd5 31. Bxd5+ {.}) 26. Qa4 {Provoking the pawn to a5 .} a5 27. Bb5 {And the bishop is back. The knight on b8 is once again dominated.} Kb7 28. Nbd3 {White is strategically winning. No need to calculate, though there were two 'potentially Shankland Method' winning options.} (28. Nbc6 Nxc6 29. Nxc6 Rxc6 30. Bxc6+ Qxc6 31. Qxc6+ Kxc6 32. Rxe6+ fxe6 33. Rxg7 {and the endgame is won.}) (28. Be8 axb4 29. Nxf7 {Black's position collapses.}) 28... Rg8 29. Nf3 Rh8 30. Rxh8 Bxh8 {I could have finished the game in any number of ways, but I felt no need to rush as the h8 -bishop and b8 -knight were hardly intimidating!} 31. a3 {Obviously preparing b4 . But what is Black to do about it? He has no plan for counterplay of any kind.} ({Against the immediate} 31. b4 {, Black shouldn't go} Qxc3 32. Rc1 {but} axb4 {cxb4 Nxd4 . Note that after} 33. Nxb4 {Qxc3 , now there's no Rc1 .}) 31... Nc6 {White can win in any number of ways, but I chose a simplifying sequence to pick up a second pawn and swap a bunch of pieces.} (31... Bg7 32. b4 axb4 33. axb4 Qxc3 34. Rc1 {and the queen is trapped.}) 32. Bxc6+ Rxc6 (32... Qxc6 33. Qxc6+ Rxc6 34. Nde5 Bxe5 35. Nxe5 Rc7 36. Nxf7 Rxf7 37. Rxe6 {or Nxd4 Nd6+ Kc6 Ne8+- .}) 33. Nde5 Bxe5 34. Nxe5 Rd6 35. Qe8 {And f7 falls as well. The rest requires no comment.} Rd8 (35... Nxg5 36. Nxf7 {.}) 36. Qxf7 Nxg5 37. Qxc7+ Kxc7 38. Nxg6 f3 39. Nf4 Kc6 40. gxf3 Nxf3 41. Re6+ Kb5 42. Ke2 Ng1+ 43. Kd3 {Finally US Champion!} *

