40... Bf8 {On the first move after time control, I had a tough decision to make. What should White do?} 41. Nxa4 {What happens if I do it anyway? I had to calculate well to make this one work, but I did. It was only a later error that denied me the full point.} Qxc2+ (41... Qxa4 {This is the best move, and my opponent even managed to save the game. Still, it is not hard to see that after} 42. b3 {, White is in the driver's seat. I spent most of my effort making sure my position was good if Black had taken on c2 instead.}) (41... Qxe3 {is easily refuted:} 42. fxe3 Rxa4 43. Rxc8 {.}) 42. Rxc2 Rxc2 {At first glance, this looks anything but clear. Black's rooks are very annoying! I had to work a lot to calculate this line to completion.} 43. Nb6 (43. b4 Ra2 44. Nc5 Ra8 {Black's rooks are scary.}) 43... R8c6 44. Qe8 {An important move, without which Black would be at least fine. Both of his rooks are frozen in place, defending the other one.} Kg7 {Now what?} (44... Rc1+ 45. Kd2 R1c2+ 46. Kd3 {This is easy enough.}) 45. Nd7 {White threatens the bishop on f8 .} Rc1+ {I guess this is relatively best since Black won't lose a rook, but once my king gets off the back rank, I can breathe a huge sigh of relief.} (45... Bd6 46. Nc5 $1 {Well spotted! The coordination between the rooks is cut, and White wins. This was a tough line for me to calculate before I pulled the trigger on Nxa4 .}) 46. Kd2 R6c2+ 47. Kd3 Bd6 {Still, more accuracy is needed.} 48. Qe3 {A final precise move seals Black's fate. Both of his rooks are stuck, he cannot make any threats, and White is ready for simple moves like Nb6 - Nxd5 .} ({Rc6 would run into} 48. Nc5 {.}) *

