that denied me the full point.} Qxa4 (41... Qxe3 {is easily refuted:} 42. fxe3 Rxa4 43. Rxc8 {The text is the best move, and my opponent even managed to save the game. Still, it is not hard to see that after b3 , White is in the driver's seat. I spent most of my effort making sure my position was good if Black had taken on c2 instead.} ({Qxc2+ Rxc2 Rxc2 At first glance, this looks anything but clear. Black's rooks are very annoying! I had to work a lot to calculate this line to completion. Nb6! (After b4 Ra2 Nc5 Ra8 , Black's rooks are scary.) R8c6 Qe8!! An important move, without which Black would be at least fine. Both of his rooks are frozen in place, defending the other one.} 43. Rc1 {Kd2 R1c2+ Kd3+- is easy enough. Black should play} Kg7 {and now, what? Nd7! White threatens the bishop on f8 . Bd6 and if it moves... Nc5! Well spotted! The coordination between the rooks is cut, and White wins. This was a tough line for me to calculate before I pulled the trigger on Nxa4 . I guess Rc1+ is relatively best since Black won't lose a rook, but once my king gets off the back rank, I can breathe a huge sigh of relief. Kd2 R6c2+ Kd3 Bd6 Still, more accuracy is needed. Qe3+- A final precise move seals Black's fate. Both of his rooks are stuck; he cannot make any threats, and White is ready for simple moves like Nb6 - Nxd5 .} 44. Rc6 {would run into Nc5 .})) 42. b3 Qxa3 43. bxc4 Qa1+ 44. Ke2 Bb4 {Despite time control just being reached, I burned a lot of it on calculating 41.Nxa4 . Here, I miscalculated. Can you use the Shankland Method better than I did?} 45. cxd5 {This is clearly the move I want to make. I saw that a check on a6 would be a problem. I asked what would happen if I did it anyway, and I miscalculated.} (45. Rd1 $1 {The answer was that doing it anyway could not be recommended, and this simple move should be preferred. After} Qa4 46. Qd3 dxc4 47. Qf5 $1 Re8+ 48. Kf1 {White has stopped all of the threats.}) 45... Qa6+ 46. Qd3 Re8+ {It was at this moment that I realized that my planned Kf1 was not going to work...} 47. Kf3 {A sad necessity.} (47. Kf1 $2 {When I took on d5 , I was counting on this move. I thought I had consolidated nicely, somehow missing I am simply mated in 2!} Qxd3+ 48. Rxd3 Re1#) 47... Qf6+ 48. Kg4 Bxd2 49. Rxd2 {White still has some winning chances in a tense position, but I did not make the most of them, and my opponent defended well.} Re1 50. f4 (50. Qf5 $4 h5+ {leads to mate.}) 50... gxf4 51. gxf4 h5+ 52. Kf3 Qh4 53. d6 Rg1 54. d7 Qg4+ 55. Ke3 Qxd7 (55... Qe6+ {was a draw on the spot after} 56. Kf3 {, but not Kf2} Qe1 {Kf3 Rf1+ or} 57. Qe4 {Re1+ and Black wins.}) 56. Kf2 Rh1 57. Qg3+ Kh7 58. Qg5 Qc8 59. Qf6 Qc4 60. Re2 h4 61. Qf5+ Kg7 62. Qe5+ Kh7 63. d5 (63. Re1 $1 {I don't know how I missed this move. White trades off the annoying rook and has real winning chances.} Rxe1 64. Kxe1 {I don't care what the machine says. Black has a long, tough, and possibly unsuccessful defense ahead.}) 63... Qd3 {At this point, I realized I was not winning the game, so I bailed into the rook endgame.} 64. Qe4+ Qxe4 65. Rxe4 Rd1 {Despite my extra pawn, this is clearly a draw.} 66. Re5 f6 67. Re7+ Kg6 68. Rd7 Kf5 69. Ke3 Re1+ 70. Kf2 Rd1 71. Ke2 Rd4 72. Ke3 Re4+ 73. Kd3 Rxf4 74. Re7 Rf1 75. Re2 Rd1+ 76. Kc4 Kf4 77. Kc5 f5 78. d6 Kg3 79. Kc6 f4 80. d7 f3 81. gxf3 Kxf3 82. Re6 Rxd7 83. Kxd7 h3 84. Rh6 Kg2 85. Rxh3 Kxh3 *

