16. a3 {Up to this point, I had found myself with a total opening disaster on my hands, but here, my opponent gave me an important chance. I used the Shankland Method very nicely. How would you continue?} cxd4 {This move is absolutely necessary. Black will suffocate under the space advantage otherwise. It looks like there are a lot of scary tactics at play if we go pawn grabbing, but we always have to ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} (16... Be7 17. b4 {and Black is completely busted.} cxd4 18. cxd4 {and} Nxd4 19. Be3 {just wins for White, but Black basically has no moves.}) 17. cxd4 Nxd4 18. Be3 {The first point is to realize that this natural move does not work.} (18. Nxd4 Qxd4 {was the game. We'll come back here in future exercises.}) 18... Qxb2+ (18... Bc5 {This is decent too, but I like the text more.} 19. b4 Nxf3 20. bxc5 Qb2+ 21. Nd2 Nfxe5 {is what the computer likes.}) 19. Rf2 (19. Nbd2 {This should be preferred, but it is not the move Black is scared of.} Nc2 20. Bxc2 Qxc2 {White is lucky that he can maintain the balance.}) 19... Qb3 {The key detail I needed to see. Black does not lose a piece, remains 2 pawns up, and wins.} ({Not} 19... Qxa1 20. Bxd4 {and the queen is trapped.}) *

