1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 4. g4 Be4 5. f3 Bg6 6. e6 fxe6 7. h4 Nd7 8. h5 Bf7 9. f4 e5 (9... g5 10. fxg5 Qc7 {is horrible, but better than the game continuation.}) 10. fxe5 e6 11. Nf3 c5 12. c3 Ne7 13. Bd3 Nc6 14. O-O Qb6 15. Kg2 Rc8 16. a3 {Up to this point, I had found myself with a total opening disaster on my hands, but here, my opponent gave me an important chance. I used the Shankland Method very nicely. How would you continue?} cxd4 {This move is absolutely necessary. Black will suffocate under the space advantage otherwise. It looks like there are a lot of scary tactics at play if we go pawn grabbing, but we always have to ask, what happens if I do it anyway?} (16... Be7 17. b4 {and Black is completely busted.} cxd4 18. cxd4 {and} Nxd4 19. Be3 {just wins for White, but Black basically has no moves.}) 17. cxd4 Nxd4 18. Nxd4 ({The first point is to realize that the natural} 18. Be3 $2 {does not work. Now,} Bc5 {is decent, with the point of meeting} 19. b4 {with} Nxf3 20. bxc5 Qb2+ 21. Nd2 Nfxe5 {and Black is fine. However, I like Qxb2+! more. Nbd2 should be preferred, but it is not the move Black is scared of. After Nc2} 22. Bc2 Qxc2 {, White is lucky that he can maintain the balance. The key detail I needed to see is} 23. Rf2 Qb3 $1 {Black does not lose a piece, remains 2 pawns up, and wins, but of course, not Qxa1??} 24. Bd4 {and the queen is trapped.}) 18... Qxd4 19. Bg6 {This looks like a very problematic move! Hopefully, you saw it before deciding to take on d4 .} (19. Rxf7 {This looks very scary, but Black is good to go if he plays precisely. Not} Kxf7 $2 20. Bg6+ {Oops! Instead, Rxc1! is an important resource. Black distracts the queen from the d1 -square to meet Qxc1 with Kxf7 and there's no discovered check now. After Rxf8+ , Black must take with the king to place him on a dark square, disallowing a discovered attack from the d3 -bishop. Therefore, not Rxf8? Bg6+! but Kxf8! Qxc1 Qxg4+! Black takes a ton of pawns, and White's king is in shambles. The machine claims it is totally winning, though there is still some work to do in human practice.}) 19... Qxd1 20. Bxf7+ Kd8 (20... Ke7 21. Bg5+ $1 Nf6 22. Rxd1 Kxf7 23. exf6 gxf6 24. Be3 {Black is not busted here, but I don't really buy the compensation for the piece. From a human point of view, I think the game continuation was stronger.}) 21. Rxd1 ({Now,} 21. Bg5+ {leads nowhere after} Be7 22. Rxd1 Bxg5 {but even worse is Bxe7+ Kxe7 Rxd1 Kxf7 and Black is winning.}) 21... Ke7 {And now back to e7 ! The bishop on f7 is trapped, and Black can meet Rf1 with ... Nxe5 , with or without a check on c2 first. It turns out Black could take on d4 ! But you had to see it all the way to here.} 22. Rf1 Rc2+ {This is not terrible, but it was better to take on e5 right away.} (22... Nxe5 $1 23. Bg5+ Kd7 24. Nc3 Bd6 {I thought I would rather have a position like this one with my rook on c2 rather than c8 , but it turns out that the idea of ... Rc4 is quite annoying for White. The machine screams equal here, but in human terms, anything can happen, and it is a game for 3 results.}) 23. Kh1 (23. Kh3 {was not working.} Nxe5 24. Bg5+ Kd7 25. h6 {This looks scary for Black. How can the Shankland Method help?} gxh6 {Anyway!} 26. Bf6 Nxf7 {The key move. White gets mated if he takes the rook.} 27. Bxh8 {and} Bd6 {may be the cleanest, but after Ng5+ , White can never save this game. E.g.:} 28. Kh4 Rh2# {or Rf4 Rc4 and White is busted.}) 23... Nxe5 24. Bg5+ Kd7 25. Nc3 Nxg4 {At this point, I thought I was doing quite fine, but Ian found the best way to increase the pressure.} 26. Bxe6+ (26. Bf4 {This allows a draw on the spot. Black can consider playing on, but he certainly is not better.} Nf2+ 27. Kg1 Nh3+ 28. Kh1 Nf2+ {.}) (26. Rae1 $2 {Don't be that guy.} Rh2+ $1 27. Kg1 Bc5+ {White has to lose some massive amount of material to avoid mate.}) 26... Kxe6 27. Rae1+ {This position is very difficult for Black.} Kd6 (27... Kd7 28. Rf7+ Kc6 29. Re6+ Kc5 30. Rc7+ Kd4 31. Ne2+ Kd3 32. Nc1+ {If nothing else, White wins the rook here.}) (27... Ne5 28. Bf4 Bd6 29. Nb5 {and Black can't stop all the threats.}) 28. Rxf8 {I correctly used the Shankland Method once already this game, and now I should use it again.} Nf2+ {This was not terrible, and the position is still defensible, but there was a much cleaner solution.} (28... Rxf8 $1 {Obviously, Black would love to take this rook. It looks like Be7 is an issue, but we have to ask, what happens if I do it anyway? I did ask, but I came to the wrong conclusion.} 29. Be7+ Kd7 30. Bxf8 Nf2+ $1 {and neither} 31. Kg1 Nh3+ {nor Kg2? Nd3+ , when Re2 is met by Nf4+ , are a problem. I saw up to Kh2 and thought I was done for because Nd3 can be met with Re2 But... Black can kick the knight with d4! and, if it leaves, ... Nd3 becomes the real deal. Re2 d3! Rxc2 dxc2 Ne2 Nd3! Black equalizes. Bxg7 c1=Q Nxc1 Nxc1= This endgame should be drawn.}) 29. Rxf2 (29. Kg1 {allows the repetition} Nh3+ {.}) ({Worse is} 29. Kh2 $4 Ne4+ {and I can take the rook.}) 29... Rxf2 30. Nb5+ ({It is rather annoying that I cannot go Kc5 due to} 30. Be3 {.}) 30... Kc6 31. Nxa7+ Kc7 32. Nb5+ Kb8 33. b4 {The position is still holdable but very tough in practice. I defended well to a point.} Rf5 34. Be7 Rxh5+ 35. Kg2 d4 36. Nxd4 Rd5 37. Bc5 Rhd8 38. Re4 h5 39. a4 g5 40. a5 g4 41. a6 bxa6 {This is still a draw, but there was a much easier solution.} (41... Rc8 $1 {This was a clean hold, but I did not calculate it right. It was hard playing board 1 for the US team for 9 rounds in a row! I was definitely tired by the end.} 42. a7+ Ka8 43. Bb6 Rd6 44. Re6 {I saw up to here and thought I was done for. The machine claims I am even okay with a quiet move like ... Rd7 , but there is a much more direct solution.  A)} Rxe6 45. Nxe6 h4 46. Nc7+ Rxc7 47. Bxc7 Kxa7 48. b5 {I thought I was lost here but after} b6 {, White can't win. Some sample lines:} 49. Bd8 h3+ 50. Kg3 Kb7 51. Bf6 Kc7 52. Bd4 {and now not} Kb7 53. Bg1 {but Kd6 Bxb6 Kd7 Bg1} Kc7 54. Kxg4 h2 {.}) ({Kf2 Kb7 Bf4} 41... Kc8 {Bg5 Kd7 Bxh4 is also a draw.  B) Rxb6! Rxb6 Rc4! (Not Kxa7? Rh6+- Rc4 Nb5+ and White wins.)} 42. Nb5 {I stopped calculating here, thinking I lost. Obviously, Rxb4? Nc7+ does lose, but I missed an important final move... Rc6! Very nice! Rxc6 bxc6 Nd4 Kxa7 Nxc6+ Kb6 Nd4} h4 {White's king will never be able to connect with the b -pawn. Draw.} 43. Kf2 {Ka6 Ke3 Here, Black has to be careful: Kb6 would lose. E.g.: Kd3} h3 {Ne2 or g3 Ne2 g2 Ng1 Kb5 Kc3 when White's king has connected with the b -pawn, and Black will eventually run out of moves and lose the kingside pawns. Instead, h3 holds: Kf2 h2 Kg2 g3= or Ne2 Kb5= .}) 42. Nc6+ Kc7 43. Nxd8 Kxd8 44. Kg3 ({Stronger than} 44. Re6 a5 45. Bb6+ Kd7 {.}) 44... Rf5 45. Kh4 Rd5 46. Re3 Kc7 47. Re7+ Kb8 48. Bb6 Rb5 49. Bc7+ Ka8 50. Ba5 Rf5 51. Re6 Kb7 52. Rg6 Ka7 53. Rb6 Rd5 (53... Rf1 54. Kxh5 {.}) 54. Rf6 Kb7 55. Rf4 Ka7 56. Kg3 Rd3+ 57. Kf2 Rd5 58. Ke3 Re5+ 59. Kf2 Kb7 60. Rf7+ {Weary from a long defense, I finally faltered.} Ka8 {Now, White wins. The rook on g7 will be too good.} (60... Kc6 $1 {I had to go active here and give up the a-pawn.} 61. Rf6+ Kb5 62. Rb6+ Kc4 63. Rxa6 h4 {Black should have enough counterplay to make a draw.}) 61. Rg7 Re4 62. Kg3 Re5 63. Kf4 {White's king is coming to the queenside, and my pawns are held firmly at bay. It's all over.} Rd5 64. Ke4 Rb5 65. Kd4 Rf5 66. Bb6 Rb5 67. Rg8+ Kb7 68. Bc5 Kc7 ({Or} 68... a5 69. Kc4 {.}) 69. Kd5 {Mate is coming.} *

