{In this final game of the course, there is only one Shankland Method moment, but it's really tough!} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 e6 3. e3 d5 4. Nf3 Bd6 5. Be2 O-O 6. O-O b6 7. Bxd6 cxd6 8. c4 dxc4 9. Bxc4 Bb7 10. Nc3 Nc6 11. Rc1 Ne7 12. Bd3 Ned5 13. Nxd5 Bxd5 14. Qa4 a6 15. Nd2 b5 16. Qa3 Qb6 17. Rc2 e5 18. Qb4 exd4 19. Qxd4 Qxd4 20. exd4 Rfc8 21. Rfc1 Rxc2 22. Rxc2 Kf8 23. a3 Ke8 24. f3 Kd7 25. Kf2 g6 26. Ne4 Nxe4+ {A step in the wrong direction.} 27. fxe4 Bb7 28. Kg3 Rc8 29. Rxc8 Bxc8 30. Kf4 f6 31. e5 Ke7 32. h4 Bd7 33. b4 {White has a clear threat.} d5 (33... Bc6 {was a better defense.}) 34. exf6+ Kxf6 35. g4 Be8 36. g5+ Ke6 37. Be2 Kd6 38. Bg4 Bf7 {I had outplayed my young opponent from a very dry position and reached a promising bishop endgame. E.g.:} (38... Bd7 39. Bxd7 Kxd7 40. Ke5 {and the endgame is winning for me.}) (38... Kc7 39. Ke5 {. Instead, Awonder played the best move, but White is winning here. However, the path is very narrow. Can you find the way?}) *

