{In this final game of the course, there is only one Shankland Method moment, but it's really tough!} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 e6 3. e3 d5 4. Nf3 Bd6 5. Be2 O-O 6. O-O b6 7. Bxd6 cxd6 8. c4 dxc4 9. Bxc4 Bb7 10. Nc3 Nc6 11. Rc1 Ne7 12. Bd3 Ned5 13. Nxd5 Bxd5 14. Qa4 a6 15. Nd2 b5 16. Qa3 Qb6 17. Rc2 e5 18. Qb4 exd4 19. Qxd4 Qxd4 20. exd4 Rfc8 21. Rfc1 Rxc2 22. Rxc2 Kf8 23. a3 Ke8 24. f3 Kd7 25. Kf2 g6 26. Ne4 Nxe4+ {A step in the wrong direction.} 27. fxe4 Bb7 28. Kg3 Rc8 29. Rxc8 Bxc8 30. Kf4 f6 31. e5 Ke7 32. h4 Bd7 33. b4 {White has a clear threat.} d5 (33... Bc6 {was a better defense.}) 34. exf6+ Kxf6 35. g4 Be8 36. g5+ Ke6 37. Be2 Kd6 38. Bg4 Bf7 {I had outplayed my young opponent from a very dry position and reached a promising bishop endgame. E.g.:} (38... Bd7 39. Bxd7 Kxd7 40. Ke5 {and the endgame is winning for me.}) (38... Kc7 39. Ke5 {. Instead, Awonder played the best move, but White is winning here. However, the path is very narrow.}) 39. Bd1 (39. Bc8 $1 {Shankland Method! Of course, this is the move White wants to make, going after the a6 -pawn. I saw it, and I saw a problem with it. I did ask what would happen if I did it anyway, but I came to the wrong conclusion.} Be6 {Somehow, I had only considered} 40. Bxa6 {, which is obviously natural but not forced.} Bd7 {probably holds too, but Kc6! is very direct and clean. Now, things get very forced. Ke5 Bg4} 41. Bxb5 {Kxb5 Kxd5 Bf3+! and after both Ke5 Kc4 Kf6 Kxd4 Kg7 Kc4 Kxh7 Bh5= and Kd6 Kc4!} Kc7 {Kb5! , Black is making a draw. His control of the long diagonal is too annoying.}) ({The only way is Bb7!! and now:  A) Kc7? Ke5+- Well spotted. B) Bf7? Bxa6! Now this works since the bishop will get back out. Kc6} 39. Bc8) ({C)} 39. Bd7 {and now, Bxa6 Kc6! Ke5 Bg4= transposes to the 40.Bxa6 variation seen above. We still cannot take on a6 . Instead, Ke3!} Be6 {Now, Black is ready for ... Kc7 next. But we basically got the move Ke3 for free compared to taking on a6 directly without flicking in Bb7 first. So... Bxa6! Kc6} 40. a4 $1 {Now we see the point of getting the king to e3 . It is in the square of the pawn.} bxa4 {Kd2! Kb6 and now, we can't let Black's bishop reach f5 with a gain of tempo. Bd3? Bf5! Black is all too happy to take this diagonal. Bxf5 gxf5} {(Not Kd3? Kb6! when, all of a sudden, the bishop is trapped! White is lucky not to be lost.)} 41. h5 a3 {or Be2 a3 Kc3 a2 Kb2 Bb1= lead to draw.}) ({The winning sequence is} 39. Be2 $1 {Bd7 Kc3+- Bb5 and now one final good move to seal the deal... Bg4! White's king will handle the a -pawn, and the bishop ransacks the kingside. White wins. (Black holds after Bd1? a3= because Kb3? is not to be recommended: Ba4+-+ Oops.) Bc6 but it's not over yet: Bf3 Bb7 Bd1 Kb5 Be2+ Kb6 Kb2+- This wins because I've managed to reach this position with Black's bishop on b7 . Incredibly difficult! Ba6 Bd1 Kb5 Ka3 Bb7 Be2+ and the black king can't get in. Note that after} Be6 {Kb5 , White should return to g4 and amend his ways because Bg8 is insufficient due to a3 Kb3 a2! Kxa2 Kxb4 Bxh7 Be8= and Black holds.}) 39... Be6 {Now Black has a fortress, no matter how many pawn advances White gets.} 40. Bc2 Bf7 41. h5 Be8 42. h6 Bf7 43. a4 Be8 44. a5 (44. axb5 {This leaves Black with a weakness on b5 , but not on a6 .} axb5 45. Bd3 Ke6 $1 {with a5 and a6 on the board, this would be impossible due to bishop around to c8 . As is, it's a dead draw.} 46. Be2 Kd6 47. Bg4 {and there's no target on a6 .}) 44... Bf7 45. Bb1 Be8 46. Bd3 {Now, since a5 and a6 are on the board, Ke6 fails. However, Black can also simply shuffle his bishop without fearing the loss of b5 .} Bf7 (46... Ke6 {would lose to} 47. Bf1 Kd6 48. Bh3 {and Black can't allow Bc8 but} Bd7 49. Bxd7 Kxd7 50. Ke5 {is hopeless.}) 47. Be2 ({After} 47. Bf1 Be6 {, Black has no worries.}) 47... Be6 48. Bd3 Bf7 49. Bc2 Be8 50. Bd1 Bf7 51. Bg4 Be6 52. Bf3 Bf7 53. Bd1 Be6 54. Be2 Bf7 55. Bd3 Be8 56. Bf1 Bf7 57. Be2 Be6 58. Bd3 Bf7 *

